<p>Regarding your other questions, modern awards are federal instruments, meaning there are no specific NSW or Sydney-based overtime nuances for this industry; the rules apply nationally across Australia. As a general principle, overtime is triggered for time worked in excess of 38 hours per week or outside of ordinary hours (with maximum ordinary hours in a day being 8 hours). </p>
<p>Because specific overtime rates, thresholds, and conditions depend entirely on the award, please confirm the exact modern award your employees are covered by so I can outline their precise entitlements.</p>', NULL, NULL, 0, 0, '2026-08-19 02:02:10.329839+00', '89b42e3b-00d3-4097-9e7f-79ca37fe0920', 'complete', false, 'The response provides general information about how overtime works under Australian federal awards and correctly identifies that specific rates depend on the particular award coverage.');
<p>These unpaid or unapproved absences do not count towards the accrual of leave entitlements, such as annual or personal leave. Unpaid time is not considered service for accrual purposes, so leave will only accrue on the ordinary hours the employee actually worked or was paid for.</p>
<p>For superannuation, the unpaid absence reduces the employee''s Ordinary Time Earnings (OTE). Because no earnings are generated for the unworked hours, you do not pay the 12% superannuation guarantee on the unpaid portion. </p>
<p>For record-keeping and Single Touch Payroll (STP) Phase 2 reporting, unpaid leave is not reported as a separate leave type; it is simply reflected through reduced gross earnings. Your payslips should clearly show the adjustment or the reduced ordinary hours paid. You must also retain internal records of the specific hours not worked, any communications regarding the absence, and the calculations used to determine the pay reduction.</p>', NULL, NULL, 0, 0, '2026-08-28 05:00:52.100398+00', 'd01a6f8d-9a9e-492a-a7e1-4f1ffe8cca50', 'complete', false, 'The answer provides a complete and clear explanation of how to treat partial-day unpaid absences, covering gross pay adjustments, leave accruals, superannuation impact, and reporting requirements.');


































































