 you allow leave in advance, in excess of the balance, document it in a written agreement, because without one you cannot recover a negative balance from a final pay if the employee leaves. You can also direct an employee to take leave in some situations, such as a shutdown or an excessive balance, but the award sets the rules, including how much notice you must give, often around eight weeks. What do you actually pay on annual leave? The standards say the base rate. But almost every award adds more. Most provide leave loading of at least seventeen and a half percent, or the relevant shift penalty if that is higher, whichever is greater. And if the award has all purpose allowances, a leading hand allowance for example, those are paid on annual leave too. So check the award before you assume base rate only. Finally, cashing out. The standards allow an employee to cash out annual leave as long as at least four weeks remain. Most awards then cap it at two weeks in any twelve month period, though award free employees can cash out more. You need a written agreement each time, and you must pay what the employee would have received had they actually taken the leave, including any leave loading and all purpose allowances. That covers parental leave and annual leave, two of the entitlements you will deal with most.', '0dbff48c25aea17f81ba425bbe401f915fea55435229902e8474edccd53e51c8', 5141, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:48:20.227+00', '2026-07-30 08:48:20.351773+00', '2026-07-30 08:48:20.227+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('fdd6bec9-dc7b-4ddb-b1dd-49524754fef7', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers the Fair Work Information Statement and the Casual Employment Information Statement, the twelfth of the National Employment Standards. The Fair Work Information Statement, or FWIS, is a document published by the Fair Work Ombudsman summarising the National Employment Standards, modern awards, agreement making, freedom of association, individual flexibility arrangements, termination of employment, right of entry, and where to go for more information. Every new employee, full time, part time or casual, must be given the current FWIS before, or as soon as practicable after, they start work. It''s a one off requirement for ongoing employees, given once at the start of employment, not repeated. Casual employees have an additional obligation, the Casual Employment Information Statement, covering the definition of casual employment, casual conversion rights, and where to get more information or help with a dispute. This is given at the same time as the FWIS when a casual starts, and then again at set intervals as their employment continues. Timing for the Casual Employment Information Statement differs by employer size. A small business employer, fewer than 15 employees, gives it again after 12 months and 24 months. Other employers give it again after 6, 12, 18, 24, 36, 48 and 60 months of employment. There''s no prescribed method of delivery, email, a link, a printed copy, or an employee portal are all acceptable, as long as the employee actually receives it. What matters for compliance is that it happened, and can be evidenced, not the specific channel used. Failing to provide either statement is a breach of the NES, and while it rarely causes financial loss on its own, it commonly surfaces as an add on claim in a broader underpayment or unfair dismissal matter, weakening the employer''s position on other issues. Keep a dated record every time a statement is issued, including for issue. Getting the Fair Work Information Statement and Casual Employment Information Statement right is a simple, low cost compliance step, issue on time, issue again on schedule for casuals, and keep the record, so it''s never in question later.', '27f58ad121838c98c3d00ef0060d90cf77f65a27834ed92eac884d9be94ca2e8', 2199, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:46:58.32+00', '2026-07-30 08:46:58.440319+00', '2026-07-30 08:46:58.32+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e6fac90d-4003-4065-99a8-89ab87d6581c', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers requests for flexible working arrangements, the second of the National Employment Standards. Flexible working arrangements let certain employees ask their employer to change their working pattern, hours, location or start and finish times. Eligible employees include those who are parents or carers of a school aged or younger child, carers under the Carer Recognition Act, people with a disability, employees 55 or over, and those experiencing family and domestic violence or supporting an immediate family member experiencing it. An eligible employee needs at least 12 months of continuous service, or for a casual, a firm ongoing pattern with a reasonable expectation of continuing work. The request must be in writing and set out the change sought and the reason for it. An employer must respond in writing within 21 days. They can only refuse on reasonable business grounds, such as excessive cost, no practical way to accommodate the change, or a significant loss of efficiency or productivity, and must first genuinely discuss the request with the employee, trying to find a compromise. If refusing, the written response must set out the specific business reasons, address any alternative arrangement discussed, and explain the process to dispute the decision. Refusing without these details, or without genuinely discussing options first, is itself a breach of the NES, separate from whether the refusal was ultimately justified. Since the Fair Work Legislation Amendment, employees have a right to escalate an unresolved dispute over a flexible work request to the Fair Work Commission, which can now arbitrate and make binding orders, not just conciliate. Payroll''s role is largely downstream of HR''s decision, but it still matters, once an arrangement is approved, pay, timesheets, allowances and rostering must all be updated to reflect it accurately and on time, and any change in ordinary hours can affect leave accrual, allowance eligibility and even award or agreement coverage. Getting flexible work requests right means responding in writing within 21 days, genuinely discussing the request first, and giving specific reasons for any refusal, protecting both the employee''s rights and the business from an avoidable dispute.', '07025fae9bf626372bee3b6524ffd0568e9d9ee74d0bfdb9fd112796335fbe99', 2291, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:47:14.528+00', '2026-07-30 08:47:14.644006+00', '2026-07-30 08:47:14.528+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('17ef2fa2-10fc-4240-a19e-c6212a74c19b', 'Welcome to Payroll tax, nexus provisions. This video covers the nexus provisions: the rules that decide which state or territory gets the payroll tax when an employee works in more than one place. If all of an employee''s work in a month is in one state, that state gets the tax. Simple. The nexus rules only matter when, within a single month, an employee performs services in more than one jurisdiction. Think of a sales rep travelling Queensland, New South Wales, the ACT and Victoria in one month. Whose payroll tax is it? All jurisdictions agree on the answer, and it follows a strict four tier order. The test follows a strict order. Tier one is the employee''s principal place of residence. Tier two, if there is no residence, is the employer''s registered business address. Tier three, if neither applies, is where most of the wages are paid. And tier four, rarest of all, is where the services were mainly performed. You apply them in order, top to bottom. Tier one, and the answer in almost every case: the employee''s principal place of residence. Wherever the employee actually lives is where the whole month''s remuneration is liable, even if they spent most of the month working elsewhere. So if our travelling rep lives in Queensland, Queensland gets it. You only move to the next tier if there genuinely is no principal place of residence. Tier two applies when the employee has no fixed address, for example a long distance driver who lives in the cab, or an overseas worker moving between sites. Then you look at where the employer has its registered business address, its physical, bricks and mortar presence. That state gets the liability. Tier three is rarer still: no employee residence and no employer presence in Australia. Then you look at where most of the wages are paid, which in practice means the state of the bank account the wages go into. And tier four, rarest of all, when none of the above resolves it: where the services were mainly performed. Two more situations. Overseas work has its own rule: the first six months an employee is overseas are taxable, but once they are away for more than six months, the whole period becomes exempt, including those first six months, which may mean going back for a refund. And if a one off cross border month creates a tiny liability in a new state, it can be worth asking that revenue office for an exemption rather than registering for a few dollars of tax. So the discipline is: only apply nexus when work crosses borders within a month, then walk the four tiers in order, starting with where the employee lives. Watch the six month overseas rule, and remember that a working from home move can quietly shift an employee''s residence, and the revenue, to another state.', 'bfb216ce6219807e43d8adccbcf931e320a7e30d79db02c90bf38ec8a32df4e0', 2740, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:47:22.899+00', '2026-07-30 08:47:23.01471+00', '2026-07-30 08:47:22.899+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('462af05e-beea-407c-ab92-290189fe4d45', 'The NES sets a floor for annual leave payments, but it''s the award that actually decides what an employee gets paid while they''re on leave, and that difference matters more than most people expect. In this video we''ll cover why you always look to the award first, and work through what that means in dollar terms. Under the NES alone, annual leave is paid at the employee''s base rate for their ordinary hours. But almost every employee sits under an award as well, and it''s the award that decides whether anything gets added on top, most commonly annual leave loading, typically 17.5%, but potentially allowances or penalty rates too, depending on what that specific award says. This is why you can''t just default to ''base rate'' for annual leave payments across your whole workforce. Two employees under two different awards, doing similar work, can be paid completely differently while on leave, one getting just their ordinary rate, another getting that rate plus a loading, because their awards made different choices. Where leave loading is payable, it exists to make up for the loss of opportunity to earn overtime or penalty rates while at work, that''s the underlying policy reason it exists at all. Some awards pay it as a flat percentage regardless of what the employee would have actually earned; others compare loading to the penalty rate the employee would have earned that week and pay whichever is greater. For a full time employee earning $30 an hour on a $1,140 standard week, a 17.5% loading adds $199.50 to their leave payment for that week. For that same calculation to make sense, you need to know two things from the award: does it apply loading at all, and if so, is it always applied, or only when it beats the equivalent penalty rate. Never assume base rate is enough for annual leave payments, always check the specific award. Confirm whether loading applies, at what percentage, and whether it''s compared against penalty rates or simply added on top regardless. Getting this step wrong on a routine annual leave payment is one of the most common and easily fixed payroll errors.', 'eeb7224e87704da15c9f9bca0eb049c34b80ae61d81b5f349dda1d4188e994c8', 2103, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:49:31.531+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:31.660709+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:31.531+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d3111950-0509-43f7-962f-3ed5e9c821fa', 'Welcome to Payroll essentials, all about Payday Super. Payday Super has received Royal Assent and takes effect from 1 July 2026, and it changes one of the most fundamental rhythms of payroll: how often we pay superannuation. In this video we will cover exactly what''s changing, who it affects, and the practical steps to take now, before the change lands. The headline change: superannuation guarantee contributions must reach the employee''s fund within seven business days of payday, replacing the current quarterly cycle. If you currently pay quarterly, that could mean going from four payments a year to fifty-two or more, depending on how many pay cycles you run. This is not optional and not phased in gently, once it lands, it applies. The seven day rule also applies to eligible contractors, contractors paid wholly or principally for their labour are treated as employees for superannuation purposes, even if they invoice you with an ABN. If accounts payable process contractor payments outside of payroll, they need to know about a contractor''s superannuation obligation quickly enough to meet that seven day window, so this is a conversation to have with finance now, not in June 2026. There is no upper age limit for superannuation guarantee, employees over 18 receive it no matter their age or how close to retirement they are. Be proactive with employees who are about to retire or resign: under Payday Super, closing a super fund early, before their final payment lands, can cause the payment to bounce back after their fund has closed, leaving payroll scrambling to find a new complying fund at the worst possible time. Employees sometimes ask about splitting their super into a spouse''s account, commonly where a partner is on parental leave or not working. Do not process this through payroll: paying super to someone who isn''t your employee can trigger a fringe benefits tax liability for the employer. Direct these requests to the employee''s super fund, it has nothing to do with payroll. Before 1 July 2026, get ahead of it: confirm with your clearing house how long processing currently takes and whether they''ll be ready for a 7 day cycle, review your system''s ability to process super more frequently, and talk to your finance team about contractor payments and cash flow under a weekly or fortnightly super cycle instead of quarterly.', '84bc3f4e016790b97ff2e6ad4f99f834942f7356fe115ecde450b5e2b044dc7b', 2358, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:48:48.067+00', '2026-07-30 08:48:48.20285+00', '2026-07-30 08:48:48.067+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('cf533a68-f7d2-450c-acb3-f30b5a1f8ddf', 'Payday Super has received Royal Assent and takes effect from 1 July 2026, and it changes one of the most fundamental rhythms of payroll: how often we pay superannuation. In this video we will cover exactly what''s changing, who it affects, and the practical steps to take now, before the change lands. The headline change: superannuation guarantee contributions must reach the employee''s fund within seven business days of payday, replacing the current quarterly cycle. If you currently pay quarterly, that could mean going from four payments a year to fifty-two or more, depending on how many pay cycles you run. This is not optional and not phased in gently, once it lands, it applies. The seven day rule also applies to eligible contractors, contractors paid wholly or principally for their labour are treated as employees for superannuation purposes, even if they invoice you with an ABN. If accounts payable process contractor payments outside of payroll, they need to know about a contractor''s superannuation obligation quickly enough to meet that seven day window, so this is a conversation to have with finance now, not in June 2026. There is no upper age limit for superannuation guarantee, employees over 18 receive it no matter their age or how close to retirement they are. Be proactive with employees who are about to retire or resign: under Payday Super, closing a super fund early, before their final payment lands, can cause the payment to bounce back after their fund has closed, leaving payroll scrambling to find a new complying fund at the worst possible time. Employees sometimes ask about splitting their super into a spouse''s account, commonly where a partner is on parental leave or not working. Do not process this through payroll: paying super to someone who isn''t your employee can trigger a fringe benefits tax liability for the employer. Direct these requests to the employee''s super fund, it has nothing to do with payroll. Before 1 July 2026, get ahead of it: confirm with your clearing house how long processing currently takes and whether they''ll be ready for a 7 day cycle, review your system''s ability to process super more frequently, and talk to your finance team about contractor payments and cash flow under a weekly or fortnightly super cycle instead of quarterly.', 'edb50d91458b99fa7b3fea3ef45bbf39c1d772ba181a489d96ac7d9944ade2e1', 2303, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:49:02.757+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:02.887613+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:02.757+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('288f9113-a848-4d98-8304-150c0511dcf2', 'PAYG withholding on everyday salary and wages is the routine engine running behind every single pay run, and it''s worth understanding clearly before looking at the special rules used for termination payments. In this video we''ll cover how standard withholding works and why it''s different from the termination methods covered elsewhere. For ordinary salary and wages, PAYG withholding uses the tax tables published by the ATO, matched to the employee''s pay frequency, weekly, fortnightly or monthly, and their declared circumstances from the tax file declaration, primarily whether they''re claiming the tax-free threshold. Your payroll system applies the relevant table automatically, provided the underlying employee data is correct. This standard method assumes a consistent, regular income, it''s built for the normal case of someone earning roughly the same amount each pay period. That''s precisely why it doesn''t work well for one-off or irregular payments like termination payments, back pay, or bonuses, those need different, purpose-built withholding methods, which is why this course covers them separately. Getting standard PAYG withholding right starts upstream of the actual calculation, with a correctly completed tax file declaration and the right pay frequency set up in the system. If either of those is wrong, the tax table applied will be wrong too, even though the payroll system''s math itself is functioning perfectly. It''s worth remembering that PAYG withholding is fundamentally an estimate, a reasonable approximation of the tax the employee will ultimately owe for the year, reconciled properly at tax time through their tax return. Employers aren''t trying to calculate someone''s exact final tax liability every pay run, they''re applying the correct standard method as published by the ATO. Standard PAYG withholding on salary and wages is mechanical once the inputs are right: correct tax file declaration, correct pay frequency, correct tax table. Save the special methods, marginal rate, back pay averaging, for the one-off and irregular payments that genuinely need them, and don''t force those special methods onto routine pay runs where they don''t belong.', 'a6299032aa06997634c0563fc358c0957eae76675f4ec4a43645950239ba8ece', 2183, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:49:14.803+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:14.933886+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:14.803+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e0fbc2fb-9f1e-4571-8aa7-cc4702e389d5', 'Once a payment on termination isn''t unused leave and isn''t within a tax-free redundancy limit, it likely becomes an employment termination payment, an ETP. In this video we will define what is and isn''t an ETP, walk through the Schedule 11 decision table, the excluded versus non-excluded split, and the ETP cap itself. An ETP can only be paid when an employee has actually terminated, it is right there in the name. A bonus paid to a still-employed person for great work is a bonus, not an ETP, no matter how large. ETPs typically include gratuities and golden handshakes, severance pay, payment in lieu of notice outside a redundancy, unused RDOs, unused sick leave where the award requires payout, compensation for wrongful dismissal, and amounts above the tax-free redundancy or early retirement limit. Schedule 11 splits into two sections, and you always start at the top. If the payment relates to an early retirement scheme, genuine redundancy, invalidity, or compensation for personal injury, unfair dismissal, harassment or discrimination, it''s an excluded ETP, code R. If it doesn''t fit any of those, drop to the second section: golden handshake, gratuity, or payment in lieu of notice outside redundancy, and it''s a non-excluded ETP, code O. High income earners often want to sit in the excluded section, because it uses a simpler cap, but that''s determined by the facts, not by preference. Excluded ETPs, code R, are capped only by the ETP cap, currently $260,000 and indexed annually. It only reduces if the employee has already received an early retirement scheme, genuine redundancy, invalidity, or personal injury payment earlier in the same financial year, which is rare. Below the cap, tax is 32% if the employee is under preservation age, or 17% if at or over preservation age, at their age as at 30 June, not at termination. Above the cap, the excess is taxed at 47% regardless of age. Non-excluded ETPs, code O, must be checked against the smaller of two caps: the ETP cap, and the whole of income cap, which we''ll unpack in full in the next video. Whichever cap is smaller applies. Below that smaller cap: 32% under preservation age, 17% at or over. Above it: 47%, no matter the age. Worked example: Winnie, 63, three years of service, $50,000 year to date, $15,000 annual leave, $3,500 final pay, and $44,000 redundancy pay plus in lieu of notice bringing her ETP to $14,744 above her tax-free limit. She''s under the $260,000 ETP cap, so we check preservation age: she''s over it, so it''s taxed at 17%, code R, since it''s a genuine redundancy. The path is always the same: confirm it''s actually an ETP, not leave or a bonus. Start at the top of Schedule 11 and check for early retirement, genuine redundancy, invalidity or compensation, if none apply, move to the bottom section. Excluded, code R, uses only the ETP cap. Non-excluded, code O, uses the smaller of the ETP cap and the whole of income cap. Then tax at 32% or 17% up to the cap based on preservation age at 30 June, and 47% on anything above it.', 'f3837b0338544e359cf463846cbbfeece4e2522b596226602b3222c378f6feb2', 3030, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:49:44.247+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:44.376096+00', '2026-07-30 08:49:44.247+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('1d1bc8f3-f0e2-47b7-9595-6c4ce16432e0', 'A genuine redundancy can unlock a tax-free portion of a termination payment, but it is not automatic. In this video we will cover the conditions an employee must meet under the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997, how to calculate the tax-free limit, what can be included in it, and the rare early retirement scheme that gets the same tax treatment. A genuine redundancy is where the role itself is no longer required to be done by that person or anyone else in the business, think of self-checkouts replacing cash register staff. To access the tax-free portion, four conditions must all be met at the time of dismissal: the employee must be under pension age, there must be no plans to rehire them in any capacity including casual, they must not be finishing at the end of a fixed term contract, and the payment must genuinely be in consequence of the redundancy. Pension age is different from preservation age, which we''ll cover in the ETP video, pension age is checked against a birth date table specifically to determine eligibility for the tax-free portion, at the time of termination. An employee above pension age can still receive redundancy pay and notice, they just won''t get the tax-free treatment, and their leave is taxed at standard marginal rates instead. You can include in the tax-free portion anything the employee would not have received had they simply resigned. Redundancy pay itself always qualifies, since a resigning employee wouldn''t get it. Payment in lieu of notice usually qualifies too, since a resigning employee would typically have had to work their notice. Ex gratia or golden handshake payments can qualify if there''s no guarantee they''d be paid on resignation. If a payment would have been paid regardless of how the employee left, it doesn''t qualify. The tax-free limit itself is indexed every 1 July. This financial year it is a $13,100 base amount, plus $6,552 for each completed year of continuous service, using the same continuous service definition as redundancy pay: hire date to termination date, minus periods of casual employment, unauthorised leave, unpaid parental leave and leave without pay. Anything within that limit is reported as tax-free lump sum D; anything above it becomes a taxable ETP. The rare cousin of genuine redundancy is the early retirement scheme, where an employer selects a defined group of employees nearing retirement age and offers them the same tax-free treatment in exchange for exiting the business, often to bring in new skills or technology. This is not automatic, the scheme must be submitted to and approved by the ATO before it can proceed, and if the ATO doesn''t approve it, the tax-free treatment cannot be applied.', 'fed395cbf4fe36e68c0c30564c1a24fec01fd50e82681fdebd7af5e0d8a89a47', 2693, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:50:03.453+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:03.584561+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:03.453+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ef4d4fd4-ccab-4e94-83b3-c599ae05eeb5', 'Most payroll systems calculate marginal rate tax on termination payments correctly, because they use year to date figures to work out an average pay. But there are a few situations where that same logic quietly breaks down, and if you don''t know to check for them, an employee can walk away underpaying tax without anyone noticing until their next tax return. In this video we will look at exactly when your system can fail, and prove the marginal rate method by hand. Your payroll system typically takes the employee''s year to date gross earnings and divides it by the number of pay periods that have already happened this financial year, to estimate their average pay. That estimate feeds the marginal rate calculation. The problem is what happens when that year to date figure is zero. Year to date figures sit at zero in three situations: right at the start of a new financial year, before your system has processed any pays; when a brand new employee terminates before receiving their first pay; or after a system migration where historical year to date figures were not carried over correctly. In every one of these cases, dividing by zero pay periods breaks the averaging logic your system relies on. Let''s prove it. Same employee, same $3,864 annual leave payout, but this time their year to date is zero because they are terminating in the first week of the new financial year. Step one: tax on $0 is $0. Steps two and three are unaffected, still $74. Step four: $0 + $74 = $74, and the tax on $74 is also $0. Step six: $0 minus $0 is $0. Step seven: $0 times 52 is $0. Your system will show zero tax withheld on a $3,864 payment. That employee walks away thinking they''ve had a great result, no tax on their leave payout. But when they lodge their tax return the following year, that $3,864 is assessable income, and based on our earlier calculation, they will owe roughly $1,196 in tax they were never asked to pay at the time. The employer hasn''t done anything wrong technically, but the employee is caught out, and often blames payroll. The fix is procedural, not technical: whenever an employee terminates with a zero or unusually low year to date figure, or your system returns a suspiciously low tax result on a large termination payment, run the seven step calculation manually using what their pay would look like if it continued for a full year. Since payroll doesn''t have many KPIs to lean on, build in a quarterly check of your termination tax calculations as a standing discipline, not just a one-off review.', 'd77043bd449a959184ddc40027b82a1b6758d903cc2dd25f9f14ba28745bc121', 2530, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:50:18.021+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:18.355868+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:18.021+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('b81d5859-f1c9-45f1-a721-96c14b304659', 'Payroll tax catches out even experienced payroll professionals, because unlike long service leave, it''s one of the few areas that has actually been harmonised well across states and territories. This video is a condensed refresher: registration, due dates, the nexus provision for employees working across state lines, what wages are liable, and the main exemptions. Employers become liable for payroll tax once their total Australian wages exceed each state or territory''s exemption threshold. It''s a state and territory tax on total wages, and the onus is on the business to assess its own liability and register, which can now be done online. Never assume it doesn''t apply to you: Victoria removed a long-standing school exemption, so eligibility can change. Most states require monthly payment by the 7th, except the Northern Territory, which allows until the 21st. Smaller businesses can sometimes pay half-yearly or annually, check with the relevant state. Every state and territory also requires an annual reconciliation, due 21 July, except the ACT, New South Wales and South Australia, which have until the 28th. If you operate in multiple states, reconcile them all at once rather than splitting the work. A due date falling on a weekend or public holiday simply moves to the next business day. When an employee works across more than one state or territory in a month, the nexus provision decides where their payroll tax is paid, a four-tier test, moving to the next tier only if the previous one can''t be determined. Tier one: principal place of residence. Tier two: where the employer''s ABN is registered, or the business is located. Tier three: where their wages are actually paid, their bank account. Tier four: where they perform more than 50% of their work. A flight attendant living in Queensland but flying to four other states in a month still pays payroll tax to Queensland, tier one settles it immediately. An interstate truck driver with no fixed residence moves to tier two, the state where the employer is registered. Only when residence, employer location and pay location all fail to resolve it do you fall back to tier four, physical work location, which is exactly why most cases resolve well before reaching it. Employees seconded overseas remain liable for payroll tax in Australia for the first six months, since you don''t yet know if they''re returning. Only once they''ve been overseas for six continuous months do you stop paying, then claim a refund for that initial period through the annual reconciliation. Brief returns for meetings or holidays don''t reset that six-month clock, provided the employee goes back overseas afterwards. Payroll tax applies to almost everything: salary and wages, bonuses, commissions, allowances, superannuation, termination payments, director remuneration, fringe benefits, leave payments, shares and options, and contractor payments. The exemption list is short: parental leave for the primary carer (14 weeks full pay, 28 at half pay, secondary carer varies by state), emergency service or defence force leave (only if provided as paid leave, not annual leave), workers'' compensation from an insurer (but not employer top-up pay), the tax-free lump sum D portion, award cents-per-kilometre payments, and some not-for-profits and local governments (though not universally, Tasmania still taxes local government). Companies can also be grouped together for payroll tax, sharing a single threshold between them and each becoming liable for the others'' payroll tax, though this determination is usually made at a business level, not by payroll. This condensed refresher is deliberately a taster, if payroll tax is a significant part of your role, our full payroll tax course goes into thresholds, grouping, and calculations in much greater depth.', 'a279d3e65cee70c80763150d2ccc964475b73f18c20d585d9721caef6636b56f', 3811, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:50:57.857+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:57.98837+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:57.857+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e406f489-32fb-4b5c-8f6a-dce86153a0c9', 'The twelfth and last National Employment Standard covers the information statements. There are now three of them, and each one goes to a different group of employees at a different point in time. This standard is easy to comply with and easy to overlook, and it is one of the first things a Fair Work inspector will ask to see evidence of. Click start to begin Here are the three statements and who gets each one. First, the Fair Work Information Statement, or FWIS. Employers have to give every new employee a copy before, or as soon as possible after, they start their new job. Every new employee, without exception. Second, the Casual Employment Information Statement, or CEIS. Employers also have to give every new casual employee a copy at the same time as the FWIS. So a new casual gets two statements, not one. The CEIS is also required at set times throughout a casual''s employment, so it is not only a commencement document. Third, the Fixed Term Contract Information Statement, or FTCIS. Employers must give every employee engaged on a new fixed term contract a copy when they enter into the contract, and the Fair Work Information Statement itself describes this as before, or as soon as possible after, entering into the contract. So a new fixed term casual would receive all three. One further reminder from the workbook. An employer cannot provide an employee with an agreement with entitlements that are less than the NES. Because the Fixed Term Contract Information Statement is the newest of the three, it is worth knowing what it tells the employee. From the sixth of December 2023 there are limitations on the use of fixed term contracts. A fixed term contract cannot be for longer than two years, including any extensions or renewals. It cannot have more than one option to extend or renew. And there are consecutive contract limitations, which restrict offering a new fixed term contract for the same or substantially similar work where there was substantial continuity in the employment relationship. There are exceptions, so the rules do not apply to every fixed term contract. The exceptions include specialised skills, training arrangements such as apprenticeships and traineeships, essential work during a peak demand period, emergency circumstances or replacing someone temporarily away, employees earning above the high income threshold, positions dependent on government funding, governance positions with a time limit, and certain sport and international event roles. These limitation rules do not apply to casual employees. If a contract does not meet the rules, the end date in the contract no longer applies, which means the contract does not automatically come to an end at the end of the contracted period, and civil penalties could apply. Employees engaged on fixed term contracts on a full time or part time basis have similar conditions and entitlements to permanent employees, including leave entitlements. Fixed term employees are not the same as independent contractors. That brings us to the end of Module 2. Let us pull the whole thing back to a few points worth carrying with you. The National Employment Standards are a safety net of twelve minimum entitlements. An award, agreement or contract can always provide more than the NES. It can never provide less. Ordinary hours of work are the foundation for almost everything else. They determine overtime, they determine annual leave accrual, and they determine personal leave accrual, so getting ordinary hours right is the first step in getting the rest right. Several entitlements depend on the state or territory, not on the NES. Long service leave, jury duty payment, and whether public holidays are paid in addition to long service leave. For those, you cannot answer the question without knowing where the employee is. And watch the two rates. Base rate of pay applies to annual leave, personal and carer''s leave, compassionate leave, public holidays and redundancy pay. Full rate of pay applies to payment in lieu of notice and to paid family and domestic violence leave. Finally, remember what is mandatory and what varies. The NES entitlements are mandatory. Leave loading, cashing out, penalty rates and averaging arrangements come from the award or agreement, so always check the instrument covering the employee. Thank you for working through Module 2.', '99838bf59b1a5808553273b1b76962a3f2b2fef970097a570cbf8ce6e93e28d3', 4359, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:27.351+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:27.35913+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:27.351+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7a7fd610-bb2f-4a56-97f5-c926ec5e65ee', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers payroll governance and legal risk, why payroll now sits at the centre of legal scrutiny over underpayments, notice, and record keeping. Payroll is no longer purely administrative. Recent case law places payroll teams at the centre of scrutiny over underpayments, reasonable notice, and record keeping, making governance and documentation a core payroll responsibility, not just a legal or HR one. A well drafted employment contract gives clarity of expectations for both parties, is legally binding, and protects the business''s interests. At minimum it should address wages, bonuses, award coverage, notice of termination, hours of work, job description, confidential information, any probation period, and the commencement date. A contract that is silent or vague on notice, especially for senior or long serving employees, does not limit the employer to a specific period. Courts can instead imply a reasonable notice period based on age, seniority, salary and length of service, frequently far longer, and far more expensive, than what the employer assumed applied. In Lord v Toll Transport, a senior executive of 35 years'' service was made redundant with 3 months'' notice. He lost his general protections claim, but was still awarded $176,000 in reasonable notice, because his contract''s notice clause was vague, simply reading by giving blank months'' written notice, and his age, seniority and salary meant a court assessed reasonable notice well beyond the 3 months actually given. Reasonable notice is assessed against four factors, the employee''s age, their seniority in the organisation, their salary, and their length of service. The lesson is not about which specific claim succeeded, it''s that an unclear or generic notice clause creates open ended exposure, particularly for older, senior or long serving employees. Recommended governance steps include auditing contracts for staff who are senior, long serving, or otherwise high risk, and making sure notice periods are specific rather than left blank or generic. Contracts should also reflect what actually happens day to day in a role, not an outdated job description. Payroll, HR and People and Culture should actively work together rather than each assuming another team owns compliance, with roles and responsibilities for payroll decisions clearly documented. Be cautious about over relying on AI, automation or system defaults without a human review step for high risk transactions, senior terminations, large ETPs, restructures, and invest in targeted training wherever a gap is identified.', '4feec9db2b41df7a577ef85cb954dfe6958610cae337152d31e87edcb09892a7', 2611, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:50:28.785+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:28.913614+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:28.785+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('9f2be75c-618b-47cc-808e-ad9055f3acc5', 'Welcome to payroll tax, a condensed refresher. Payroll tax catches out even experienced payroll professionals, because unlike long service leave, it''s one of the few areas that has actually been harmonised well across states and territories. This video is a condensed refresher: registration, due dates, the nexus provision for employees working across state lines, what wages are liable, and the main exemptions. Employers become liable for payroll tax once their total Australian wages exceed each state or territory''s exemption threshold. It''s a state and territory tax on total wages, and the onus is on the business to assess its own liability and register, which can now be done online. Never assume it doesn''t apply to you: Victoria removed a long-standing school exemption, so eligibility can change. Most states require monthly payment by the 7th, except the Northern Territory, which allows until the 21st. Smaller businesses can sometimes pay half-yearly or annually, check with the relevant state. Every state and territory also requires an annual reconciliation, due 21 July, except the ACT, New South Wales and South Australia, which have until the 28th. If you operate in multiple states, reconcile them all at once rather than splitting the work. A due date falling on a weekend or public holiday simply moves to the next business day. When an employee works across more than one state or territory in a month, the nexus provision decides where their payroll tax is paid, a four-tier test, moving to the next tier only if the previous one can''t be determined. Tier one: principal place of residence. Tier two: where the employer''s ABN is registered, or the business is located. Tier three: where their wages are actually paid, their bank account. Tier four: where they perform more than 50% of their work. A flight attendant living in Queensland but flying to four other states in a month still pays payroll tax to Queensland, tier one settles it immediately. An interstate truck driver with no fixed residence moves to tier two, the state where the employer is registered. Only when residence, employer location and pay location all fail to resolve it do you fall back to tier four, physical work location, which is exactly why most cases resolve well before reaching it. Employees seconded overseas remain liable for payroll tax in Australia for the first six months, since you don''t yet know if they''re returning. Only once they''ve been overseas for six continuous months do you stop paying, then claim a refund for that initial period through the annual reconciliation. Brief returns for meetings or holidays don''t reset that six-month clock, provided the employee goes back overseas afterwards. Payroll tax applies to almost everything: salary and wages, bonuses, commissions, allowances, superannuation, termination payments, director remuneration, fringe benefits, leave payments, shares and options, and contractor payments. The exemption list is short: parental leave for the primary carer (14 weeks full pay, 28 at half pay, secondary carer varies by state), emergency service or defence force leave (only if provided as paid leave, not annual leave), workers'' compensation from an insurer (but not employer top-up pay), the tax-free lump sum D portion, award cents-per-kilometre payments, and some not-for-profits and local governments (though not universally, Tasmania still taxes local government). Companies can also be grouped together for payroll tax, sharing a single threshold between them and each becoming liable for the others'' payroll tax, though this determination is usually made at a business level, not by payroll. This condensed refresher is deliberately a taster, if payroll tax is a significant part of your role, our full payroll tax course goes into thresholds, grouping, and calculations in much greater depth.', '85120663d45a0119ed5c0d760a5bfdf6d9b91dc7b65b9858bed9390a30fc9b28', 3858, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:50:42.876+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:43.011043+00', '2026-07-30 08:50:42.876+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d3c57c49-e209-419a-8c00-a0f2c0cabcdd', 'Payroll tax casts a wide net over what counts as wages, but a genuine list of exemptions sits alongside it, and both vary by state. In this video we will cover the broad definition of wages, what typically attracts payroll tax, which organisations are commonly exempt, and how apprentice and trainee wages are treated differently across jurisdictions. The term wages varies between states but is generally defined broadly: any wages, salary, bonuses, commission, allowance or fringe benefit paid or payable, in cash or in kind, to an employee or another person. Wages paid to foreign expatriates working in Australia are also subject to payroll tax. That broad definition covers gross salaries and wages, superannuation, director''s remuneration, termination payments, fringe benefits, leave payments, allowances, shares and options, bonuses and commissions, and payments to contractors caught by the expanded definition. Almost every component of remuneration is captured somewhere. Exemptions are generally grouped into exempt organisations and exempt wages, and while there''s overlap between states, they aren''t identical. In NSW, for example, exempt organisations include religious institutions, public benevolent institutions, non-profit charities, schools and educational institutions, public and non-profit hospitals, and some councils. Victoria has a similar but not identical list, with its own conditions on commercial activity. Exempt wages categories commonly include Community Development Employment Project wages, maternity, paternity or adoption leave paid for the statutory period, volunteer firefighters and emergency service volunteers, non-profit group apprentice and traineeship schemes, and defence personnel, though the exact list and conditions differ by state. Apprentice and trainee wages get their own treatment, and it varies widely. Some states exempt them outright where an approved training contract with a group training organisation applies, others tax them but offer a rebate, sometimes for a limited period or with a sunset date on the rebate rate. Queensland, for example, exempts registered apprentices and trainees under an approved contract and layers a 50% rebate on top until 30 June 2026, while NSW taxes them but allows a rebate claimed for the eligible training period. Never assume a payment is exempt just because it looks like a common exclusion elsewhere. Confirm the specific state''s rules for both the organisation and the wage type, and check whether apprentice and trainee wages in that jurisdiction are exempt outright, taxable with a rebate, or fully taxable.', '042776598dcdbed4130cfb60769b8aae59ccee0885c82794740528e1014d076d', 2611, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:51:16.939+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:17.072893+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:16.939+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('12af9d56-ba40-4ec0-b84a-6752f1db6a94', 'Payslips and time and wages records aren''t just good practice, they''re legal obligations with specific content and retention requirements, and gaps here are exactly what a Fair Work audit looks for first. In this video we''ll cover what must be recorded and kept, and for how long. Every payslip needs to clearly show what was paid and how it was calculated: gross and net pay, the pay period, the applicable rate, hours worked where relevant, and any deductions itemised individually rather than lumped together. An employee should be able to look at their payslip and understand exactly how their pay was arrived at. Beyond payslips, employers need broader time and wages records covering things like leave taken and leave balances, agreements to take annual leave in advance or cash it out, individual flexibility arrangements, superannuation contribution records, and termination records including how much was paid and what notice was given. The rule of thumb is simple: keep it all. Whether it''s a leave agreement, an IFA, or documentation around a termination payment, treat it as something that needs to be retrievable well after the fact, not just at the time it happened. Fair Work record keeping obligations generally require records to be kept for a minimum period of years, and the safest approach is to retain more than the legal minimum where storage allows. These records exist for the employee''s protection as much as the employer''s, if a dispute arises about what was paid, when leave was taken, or what was agreed to, well-kept records are what resolves it quickly. Poor records don''t just create risk in an audit, they also make it much harder to resolve a genuine employee query. Build payslip content and record retention into your standard process, not something reconstructed after a query comes in. Every payslip should be self-explanatory, every agreement documented, and every record retrievable for as long as the law requires, and ideally longer.', '3e1905aaeca7c0d6fbf86fb59cba591659469aa4a713fad573d295ff901195b4', 1974, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:51:25.749+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:25.877752+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:25.749+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('cb6a21b8-5876-4f2a-ac2a-69e5ce9721e2', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers payslips, what must be issued, when, and exactly what information they must contain. Payslips are the employee''s window into how their pay was calculated, and the law is specific about both timing and content. Employers must issue a payslip within one working day of pay day, even if the employee is on leave at the time. The payslip can be issued electronically or as a hard copy, but whichever format is used, it must be legible, and if electronic, in a format that is easily printed and can be distributed confidentially. Every payslip must show the employer''s name and Australian Business Number, alongside the employee''s name and the pay period it covers. It must also show the date of payment, and if the employee is paid an hourly rate, the ordinary hourly rate, the hours worked at that rate, and the total pay at that rate. If they are salaried, the annual rate as at the last day of the pay period. Payslips must itemise gross and net pay, and any overtime or penalty rates and other separately identifiable amounts paid, such as allowances or bonuses. Any deductions made must be listed with the amount and details of each one. Where super applies, the payslip must show the superannuation contribution amount for the period, or the amount the employer is liable to pay, and the name of the fund it is or will be paid into. One entitlement is deliberately hidden, paid family and domestic violence leave must never be named on a payslip. It should instead appear as ordinary hours or another payment type, so the payslip looks like it normally would, protecting the employee''s privacy and safety.', 'f2b663b22124560a3ed96cede7166f0ab2d4d49f9a6559c7e837d5c6a1ca03f4', 1656, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:51:52.154+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:52.287318+00', '2026-07-30 08:51:52.154+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ac73b295-fb97-4445-87a0-5d6ed23dca6e', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers three closely related entitlements that often cause confusion: personal and carer''s leave, compassionate leave, and family and domestic violence leave. Each works differently, and one of them changes how you build the payslip itself. Personal and carer''s leave sit together as one entitlement. Permanent employees receive ten days a year. Casuals do not. There is no limit on how much can build up. If an employee takes nothing for three years and then breaks a leg in year four, they can use everything they have accrued. So unlike some entitlements, this one keeps accumulating until it is used. Here is a subtlety. The entitlement is ten days, but it is measured as one twenty sixth of the employee''s ordinary hours over the year, so it scales with the hours they actually work, not a literal day count. For someone on a thirty eight hour week that is seventy six hours a year. In practice the accrual works exactly like annual leave, just at half the rate, because it is two weeks instead of four. And what you pay is the base rate. There is no leave loading and no shift penalties on personal leave, though a few awards do add all purpose allowances, so check yours. Personal leave is not only for when the employee is unwell. It can be used as carer''s leave, for an emergency involving an immediate family member, and the family member does not have to be ill. Picture a childcare centre closing after a fire. That is a valid reason. Be careful with evidence policies. If your policy demands a doctor''s certificate, where would the employee get one when nobody is sick? The safer wording is to ask for evidence, which could be a statutory declaration or a letter, rather than a medical certificate. Compassionate leave works differently again. It is two days for each and every occasion, not two days a year. If one family member passes away, that is two days. If another passes two months later, that is a fresh two days. It does not accrue in your system, it is simply granted when the occasion arises. It is paid for permanent employees and unpaid for casuals, and it can be taken as a single block or split, for example one day to visit and one day for the funeral. Family here includes blended and step relationships. Family and domestic violence leave is ten days, and it has some important differences. It is granted, not accrued, and it renews on the anniversary of the employee''s start date. It applies to all employees, including casuals. It is paid at the employee''s full rate, including shift penalties, any overtime they were rostered for, and allowances they would normally receive. An employee can use it to arrange their safety, access services such as a shelter, or attend court. It is for the person experiencing the violence, not the perpetrator. Now the part that is unique to payroll. When an employee takes family and domestic violence leave, it must not appear on the payslip in any way that identifies it. The law requires the payslip to look as normal as possible, for the employee''s safety, because a controlling person may be checking it. So you process those hours as ordinary hours, or set up a separate ordinary hours code, never as a leave type, and never as a bonus. The pay should look completely routine.', 'e1648a592620430684fca15424467e415b7a4dc0a6f1e24c080529fc8dd082d4', 3293, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:52:42.694+00', '2026-07-30 08:52:42.826706+00', '2026-07-30 08:52:42.694+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('600d806c-0fe7-4af5-b5db-5af402c51fd4', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This is Module One, the Australian Industrial Relations System, in this part we are going to walk through the warm up activity and close out this module. Let''s get started. Before we finish, let''s put some of this into practice with the warm up activity on page 12 of your workbook. It is straightforward arithmetic, but it is the arithmetic you will use every day in payroll. Here is the activity. There are four parts. Part one gives you a weekly rate and a number of hours, and asks for the hourly rate to four decimal places. That is a simple division. Part two asks which of those calculated rates falls below the national minimum wage. Part three gives you annual salaries and pay frequencies and asks for the period salary to two decimal places, based on 52 weekly, 26 fortnightly and 12 monthly pay periods in the year. Part four asks you to fill in the missing hours or dollars, rounded to two decimal places. Pause here and work through it in your workbook. Give yourself around five minutes. One point on rounding before you start. There is no legislation that requires a specific number of decimal places. Some payroll systems round to four, some round to two, and Fair Work rounds its own pay guides to two. Here we are asking for four decimal places in part one and two in the rest, simply so your answers match ours. When you are ready, we will work through each part together. Let''s check part one. All you are doing is dividing the weekly rate by the number of hours. One thousand five hundred and eighty four dollars and fifty eight cents divided by 38 gives 41 dollars and 6995 ten thousandths. One thousand seven hundred and eighty four dollars and sixty four cents divided by 40 gives 44 dollars and 616. Eight hundred and ninety two dollars and seventy cents divided by 37 and a half gives 23 dollars and 8053. And eight hundred and fifty four dollars and thirty five cents divided by 28 gives 30 dollars and 5125. Now part two. Which of those is below the national minimum wage? The answer is 23 dollars and 8053, because the adult national minimum wage is 26 dollars and 44 cents an hour. If you saw that rate in a real payroll you would want to check whether the employee is an apprentice, a junior, or on a supported wage. If they are none of those, something is wrong. One other thing to notice in this table. The second row shows 40 ordinary hours in a week. Under the National Employment Standards the maximum is 38 ordinary hours, so anything above that would normally be overtime. Part three. Here you take the annual salary and divide it by the number of pay periods in the year. Seventy five thousand dollars paid fortnightly, divided by 26, gives two thousand eight hundred and eighty four dollars and sixty two cents. One hundred and fifty thousand dollars paid monthly, divided by 12, gives twelve thousand five hundred dollars. Ninety six thousand dollars paid weekly, divided by 52, gives one thousand eight hundred and forty six dollars and fifteen cents. And sixty five thousand dollars paid monthly, divided by 12, gives five thousand four hundred and sixteen dollars and sixty seven cents. These divisors of 52, 26 and 12 are what most organisations use, and they are what the Australian Taxation Office always uses. Some employers, particularly in government, use a different fortnightly divisor, and we will look at why on the next slide. Part four. On the dollars side, an employee on ninety thousand dollars a year. Divided by 52 that is one thousand seven hundred and thirty dollars and seventy seven cents a week. Divided by 26 it is three thousand four hundred and sixty one dollars and fifty four cents a fortnight. Divided by 12 it is seven thousand five hundred dollars a month. On the hours side, an employee working one thousand nine hundred and seventy six hours a year works 38 hours a week, 76 hours a fortnight, and 164 point six seven hours a month. That figure of one thousand nine hundred and seventy six is worth recognising on sight. It is 38 hours a week multiplied by 52 weeks. If you see it in your payroll system, that is where it comes from. One point worth understanding before we finish, because it comes up often. There are not exactly 52 weeks or 26 fortnights in a year. In three years out of four there are 365 days, which is 52 point 14 weeks or 26 point zero seven fortnights. Every fourth year has 366 days. Averaged across four years, 365 point two five days divided by 14 gives 26 point zero eight nine three fortnights. Some employers, particularly in government and under some enterprise agreements, use that divisor of 26 point zero eight nine three. Most employers use a simple 26. The difference is small. On a salary of one hundred and four thousand dollars, a divisor of 26 pays four thousand dollars a fortnight, while 26 point zero eight nine three pays about thirteen dollars and sixty nine cents less. Using the simpler divisor pays the employee slightly more, so there is no compliance problem. What matters is that you know how your own system is configured, because an employee may one day ask you why their income statement does not exactly match their contracted salary. And keep in mind that whatever Fair Work or an agreement says, when it comes to tax calculations the Australian Taxation Office always uses 52, 26 and 12. Let''s bring it together. Most employees in Australia are covered by the national workplace relations system under the Fair Work Act and Regulations, but some state public sector, local government and Western Australian employers remain under a state system, so always check. Employment conditions sit in a hierarchy. The National Employment Standards form the floor, awards add industry or occupation specific terms, registered agreements can replace the award as long as employees are better off overall, and individual contracts sit on top without undercutting anything below. The type of employment determines the entitlements. Full time and part time employees accrue leave and receive notice, while casual employees receive a loading in place of those entitlements and are defined by the absence of a firm advance commitment. And whether a worker is an employee or an independent contractor is decided by the substance of the relationship, weighing control, exclusivity, payment, delegation, risk, hours, tools and how they are engaged. In the next module we move on to the twelve National Employment Standards in detail, starting with maximum weekly hours of work.', 'c1535c5a04538acf81ab5e7dbeef074905a6dbbeeec6058d7c6936e0952f8877', 6522, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:57.514+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:57.53149+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:57.514+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e6fa96e0-26b4-4a7f-8c7a-44b2bab6d5e0', 'Next we look at the instruments that sit above the National Employment Standards. Awards, registered agreements and individual contracts, and how they stack together. Click Start to begin learning. Australia''s industrial relations system works as a hierarchy, and it helps to picture it as four layers. At the base sit the National Employment Standards. These are the absolute minimum for every employee in the national system. An employer cannot offer less, cannot contract out of them, and cannot swap one entitlement for another. Above that sit modern awards, which add terms and conditions for a specific industry or occupation. Above that sit registered agreements, which apply to a particular business or group of businesses. When a registered agreement applies, the award no longer does, but the base pay rate in the agreement cannot be less than the base rate in the award, and the National Employment Standards still apply. At the top sit individual contracts, which set out terms specific to one employee. A contract cannot undercut the N E S, the award or the agreement. The rule to remember is simple. Each layer can improve on the one below it, but never reduce it. Modern awards commenced on the first of January 2010. Before that, each state and territory wrote its own awards, and there were more than a thousand of them. A hairdresser in Queensland, a hairdresser in Tasmania and a hairdresser in Western Australia could have identical qualifications and experience and still be on three different rates of pay. Modern awards replaced that patchwork with a single national set. There are 122 of them, and they cover most people working in Australia. An award applies in addition to the National Employment Standards. It sets the minimum wages for each classification level, along with ordinary hours of work, overtime and penalty rates, allowances and loadings. Awards also contain classifications, which are levels or grades that describe the duties an employee performs. This is important. It does not matter what job title you give someone. What matters is the work they actually do, because that is what determines their classification and therefore their minimum rate of pay. When an award is described as industry based or occupation based, that raises a practical question. Which award applies to a particular employee? The order is straightforward. Go to the industry award first. Look at its classifications and see whether one of them fits the work the employee actually performs. If it does, that is the award. If it does not, go to the occupation award. Here is an example. Two people both do payroll. One works for a building and construction company. The other works for a large hotel group. If you open the building and construction award and look at its classifications, they describe on site trades and labouring roles. There is nothing there for a payroll officer. So that employee falls under the Clerks award, which is an occupation award. Now open the hospitality award. It has food and beverage classifications, front of house and back of house, and it also has administrative classifications that cover payroll roles. So that employee is covered by the hospitality award. Same job, different awards, because the industry award only applies where a classification actually fits. Modern awards do not apply to high income employees, but the definition is narrower than people expect. A high income employee is one who has accepted a written guarantee of annual earnings, and who is guaranteed to earn more than the high income threshold. From the first of July 2026 that threshold is one hundred and ninety thousand one hundred dollars. Both parts matter. Simply earning above the threshold is not enough. Without a valid written guarantee that the employee has accepted, award coverage continues to apply. There is a second consequence worth knowing. An employee guaranteed to earn above the threshold cannot lodge an unfair dismissal claim. That does not leave them without options. They may still bring a breach of contract claim, an anti discrimination claim, or a claim under the general protections provisions of the Fair Work Act. For that reason the recommendation is to seek legal advice before dismissing a high income employee. From a payroll point of view, your involvement is usually limited to confirming what the employee earns. Registered agreements include enterprise agreements and enterprise bargaining agreements. Unlike an award, which the Fair Work Commission writes and applies across a whole industry, an agreement is negotiated. Employer representatives on one side, usually human resources and employment lawyers, and employee representatives on the other, often a union or nominated employee representatives. They work through the terms they want, and an agreement can cover one business or a group of businesses bargaining together. Once the parties reach a proposed agreement, it goes to a vote of the eligible employees. If the majority vote in favour, it is submitted to the Fair Work Commission for approval. Once approved, the agreement covers those employees for a set period, typically three to four years. An important point for payroll. When a registered agreement applies, the relevant modern award no longer applies. But the base pay rate in the agreement cannot be less than the base pay rate in the award, and the National Employment Standards continue to apply in full. Award rates usually increase on the first of July, so that is a sensible time to check your agreement rates still sit above them. Before the Fair Work Commission approves an agreement it applies the better off overall test, commonly called the BOOT. The purpose is to confirm that every employee covered by the agreement will be better off overall than they would be under the relevant award. In practice the Commission cannot model every possible roster and every combination of overtime and penalty rates. So the assessment focuses on two things. First, is the rate of pay for each classification at least equal to the rate for the corresponding classification in the modern award. Second, are the twelve National Employment Standards still intact. An agreement can restructure or offset certain entitlements. It might trade a penalty rate for a higher base rate, for example. What it cannot do is leave an employee worse off overall, and it can never go below the National Employment Standards. To use annual leave as an example, the minimum entitlement for a full time employee is four weeks per year. An agreement cannot provide two weeks. The final layer is the individual employment contract. This is an agreement between one employer and one employee setting out the terms and conditions of their employment. There is no law requiring a contract to be in writing, but it is strongly recommended. If something goes wrong and the arrangement was only verbal, it becomes one person''s word against another''s. A contract cannot provide less than the National Employment Standards, and it cannot provide less than any applicable award, enterprise agreement or other registered agreement. All employees are covered by the N E S regardless of whether they have signed anything. There is a common misconception worth clearing up. People often say that because an employee is on a salary and has a contract, they are award free. That is usually not the case. In many instances the award still applies and the salary simply compensates for entitlements such as overtime, penalty rates or allowances. An employee on one hundred thousand dollars might still be covered by the Clerks award. A well written contract states which award applies and what the salary is compensating for. The only genuinely award free roles are those where no industry or occupation award contains a classification that fits, and for those employees the minimum wage and the National Employment Standards still apply.', '9759613c5c833c54b277ac0269b30439bcdbf289ebe3b2857f65a8609c4af936', 7939, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:14.132+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:14.149624+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:14.132+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('a6fb3dcd-55df-4457-ae58-6fe7b06eb36f', 'Welcome to our Introduction to Payroll course  - the first module I''m going to walk you through is, What is payroll and why is it important? During this Module we will define payroll, look at why it is so important to a business, explore how payroll sits between Human Resources and Finance, and consider the qualities that make an effective payroll professional. Click the start button to get started. Payroll typically refers to the process of paying a company’s employees. This can include tracking employees’ hours of work, calculating employees’ pay, including salaries, wages, overtime, penalties and allowances, and disbursing the payments to employees. Payroll is often one of the largest expenses a company has. The term payroll is also commonly used to identify the department in a company which is responsible for processing employees’ pays. So depending on the context, payroll may mean the process, or it may mean the team. A company may use different strategies to process their payroll, including managing payroll in-house, using an outsourced payroll provider, or a hybrid combination of the two. In-house payroll typically means having a dedicated payroll resource or team to process the company’s payroll in a payroll system. Outsourced payroll means that an external resource or vendor is engaged to process the payroll. A combination may involve some of the payroll processing being managed in-house, with parts of the process outsourced, depending on the agreement between the company and the outsourced payroll provider. Payroll is critically important for several reasons, both from an operational and a legal standpoint. The first reason is employee satisfaction and retention. Payroll ensures that employees are compensated fairly and on time. When employees are paid correctly and promptly, it boosts morale and satisfaction. Delayed or incorrect payments can lead to dissatisfaction, lower productivity, and higher turnover. The second reason is legal compliance. Businesses must comply with various laws regarding wages, tax withholdings, and superannuation. Failure to meet legal requirements can lead to fines, penalties, and even legal action. For example, companies must adhere to minimum wage laws and overtime pay, and ensure proper deductions for taxes and correct calculation of superannuation contributions. The third reason is tax compliance. Payroll involves calculating and remitting taxes on behalf of employees and for employers, for example income tax or payroll tax. Employers are required to submit these taxes to government agencies, and any errors can lead to penalties or audits. The fourth reason payroll matters is financial management and budgeting. Payroll is a significant expense for most businesses, and proper payroll management ensures that companies can budget effectively. Accurate payroll helps in forecasting cash flow and understanding the total cost of labour. The fifth reason is reporting and record keeping. Accurate payroll systems maintain essential records for both internal use and external audits. Companies need payroll records for financial audits, tax returns, and potential legal disputes. Taken together, these five reasons explain why payroll is far more than an administrative task. Payroll sits at the intersection of Human Resources and Finance, making it one of the most collaborative functions within an organisation. While every business structure is different, payroll professionals regularly work with both departments to ensure employees are paid accurately, statutory obligations are met, and financial reporting remains accurate. There is no universal reporting structure for payroll. In some organisations, payroll reports directly to HR, while in others it reports to Finance. Increasingly, larger organisations may establish payroll as a standalone function due to its complexity and strategic importance. Payroll reporting to HR is common where payroll is viewed primarily as part of the employee lifecycle. HR manages employee onboarding, contract changes, leave management, performance, and terminations, all of which have direct payroll implications. This structure promotes close coordination between employee data management and payroll processing. Payroll reporting to Finance is often seen where payroll is regarded as a significant financial operation. Payroll expenses typically represent one of the largest costs for an organisation, and payroll data feeds directly into budgeting, cash flow management, financial reporting, and compliance obligations. Finance-led payroll functions often focus on financial controls, reconciliations, and risk management. Regardless of the reporting line, successful payroll operations depend on strong collaboration between all three functions. Let us look at how payroll and HR work together. HR is responsible for managing employee information, policies, and workplace compliance, while payroll is responsible for translating that information into accurate employee payments. Examples of HR and payroll collaboration include new employee onboarding and setup; employment contracts and remuneration changes; leave approvals and leave management; employee classifications and awards; terminations and final payments; and workplace policies that affect pay entitlements. When communication between HR and payroll is effective, employee data remains accurate and payroll processing is more efficient. Poor communication can result in underpayments, overpayments, compliance breaches, and employee dissatisfaction. Now let us look at payroll and Finance. Finance relies heavily on payroll data to support organisational decision-making and statutory compliance. Key areas of collaboration include payroll costing and general ledger allocations; budgeting and workforce planning; cash flow forecasting; payroll tax, superannuation, and other statutory liabilities; financial reporting and auditing; and reconciliations and internal controls. Payroll provides critical information that enables Finance to accurately report labour costs and meet regulatory obligations. Modern payroll is far more than a transactional process. Payroll professionals must understand employment legislation, taxation, superannuation, workplace relations, technology, and financial controls. As organisations navigate increasing regulatory requirements, including initiatives such as Payday Super and wage compliance reforms, payroll’s role continues to expand. Whether payroll reports to HR, Finance, or operates independently, its success depends on effective partnerships with both functions. Together, Payroll, HR, and Finance create a framework that supports employee trust, organisational compliance, and informed business decision-making. It is also important that HR and Finance professionals understand payroll. HR decisions directly affect payroll outcomes. Changes to employment contracts, classifications, working arrangements, leave entitlements, and terminations all have payroll implications. A sound understanding of payroll requirements helps HR professionals make informed decisions, minimise compliance risks, and ensure accurate employee records. Similarly, Finance professionals benefit from understanding payroll legislation, award obligations, taxation, superannuation, and payroll reporting requirements. Payroll represents one of an organisation’s largest expenses, and accurate payroll data is essential for budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, and risk management. When HR and Finance understand the complexities of payroll, communication improves, errors are reduced, compliance is strengthened, and employees receive a better experience. Payroll should not be viewed as a standalone function. Rather, it is a shared responsibility that requires collaboration and mutual understanding across all three disciplines. A payroll professional is responsible for ensuring that employees are paid correctly and on time, while also ensuring compliance with relevant tax and employment laws. There are nine important characteristics of a payroll professional. The first is attention to detail. Payroll professionals must be highly detail-oriented, as even small errors in pay calculations or deductions can have significant consequences. The second is mathematical and analytical skills. An understanding of basic mathematics is essential for calculating wages, overtime, deductions, and taxes accurately. The third is strong knowledge of tax and employment laws. Payroll professionals must have a solid understanding of employment laws, tax laws, and other relevant legislation to ensure compliance in payroll processing. The fourth quality is confidentiality. Payroll professionals handle sensitive employee information, including employee salaries, tax details, and bank account information. Maintaining confidentiality and ensuring the privacy of this information is crucial. The fifth is organisational skills. Payroll professionals must manage multiple tasks and deadlines effectively. Organisational skills are necessary for keeping track of payroll cycles, deadlines, and compliance requirements. The sixth is time management. Payroll professionals often work with strict payroll deadlines, for example weekly, fortnightly or monthly, and must prioritise tasks to ensure timely and accurate processing of payroll. The seventh quality is communication skills. Payroll professionals need strong communication skills to explain payroll details and issues to employees, answer queries, and collaborate with other departments such as HR and Finance. The employees of a business are essentially a payroll professional’s customers, so a customer service mindset is essential. The eighth is problem-solving skills. Payroll professionals may encounter issues such as discrepancies in employee records or payroll errors. Being able to quickly identify, investigate, and resolve problems is important to avoid delays in payment or compliance issues. The ninth is knowledge of payroll systems and software. Proficiency in payroll software and systems is essential. Payroll professionals should be comfortable using payroll systems to process payments, track employee hours, and generate reports. Let us check your understanding of lesson one. Here is the question. A business processes some of its payroll internally and engages an external vendor to handle the remainder. Which payroll delivery model is this? Option A, in-house payroll. Option B, outsourced payroll. Option C, a combination model. Option D, a standalone payroll function. Take a moment to decide on your answer. The correct answer is C, a combination model. A combination model involves some of the payroll processing being managed in-house, with parts of the process outsourced, depending on the agreement between the company and the outsourced payroll provider. In-house payroll would mean the entire payroll is processed by an internal team, and outsourced payroll would mean an external vendor processes the whole payroll. A standalone payroll function refers to a reporting structure, not to who does the processing. That completes lesson one. In lesson two we turn to payroll obligations. Modern payroll is far more than a transactional process. Payroll professionals must understand employment legislation, taxation, superannuation, workplace relations, technology, and financial controls. As organisations navigate increasing regulatory requirements, including initiatives such as Payday Super and wage compliance reforms, payroll’s role continues to expand. Whether payroll reports to HR, Finance, or operates independently, its success depends on effective partnerships with both functions. Together, Payroll, HR, and Finance create a framework that supports employee trust, organisational compliance, and informed business decision-making. It is also important that HR and Finance professionals understand payroll. HR decisions directly affect payroll outcomes. Changes to employment contracts, classifications, working arrangements, leave entitlements, and terminations all have payroll implications. A sound understanding of payroll requirements helps HR professionals make informed decisions, minimise compliance risks, and ensure accurate employee records. Similarly, Finance professionals benefit from understanding payroll legislation, award obligations, taxation, superannuation, and payroll reporting requirements. Payroll represents one of an organisation’s largest expenses, and accurate payroll data is essential for budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, and risk management. When HR and Finance understand the complexities of payroll, communication improves, errors are reduced, compliance is strengthened, and employees receive a better experience. Payroll should not be viewed as a standalone function. Rather, it is a shared responsibility that requires collaboration and mutual understanding across all three disciplines.', 'c7a53c5e61c96b0dbc3b2eb2f7ddaf98dee18dacf6aaaa70c2076f20483350dc', 12958, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:36.36+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:36.382017+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:36.36+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('5bf3ef55-baa8-456a-a353-249cec0f37b1', 'Long service leave usually belongs to one employer, but a handful of industries run portable schemes instead, where entitlement follows the worker between employers. In this video we will cover which industries have portable schemes, how they are funded and paid, and how portability also works inside the public service. Portable long service leave is legislated by states and territories and is available in industries where workers commonly move between employers: building and construction, security, stevedoring, contract cleaning, the social and community services sector, and children''s services. Rather than accruing leave with a single employer, service accrues across the whole industry. The leave is typically funded by a central scheme. Current and past employers pay levies or contributions into that scheme, and once the worker has accrued enough recognised service, the scheme itself pays out the leave, not the employer the worker happens to be with at the time. Portability also exists outside these industry schemes, within state public services, and between different levels of government, both intra state and interstate, meaning service can carry across federal, state and local government employment. Payroll''s role shifts from calculator to administrator. You typically need to register the business with the relevant scheme authority, enrol eligible workers, report hours or service periods each return period, and remit the correct levy or contribution rate on those wages. Levy rates and reporting cycles differ by scheme and by state, so confirm the specific authority for your industry rather than assuming they all work the same way. For payroll, the practical takeaway is to check whether your industry runs a portable scheme before assuming standard state LSL rules apply. If a portable scheme applies, service is usually tracked and paid by the scheme administrator, and your obligation shifts to registering the worker and remitting the correct levy, not calculating and paying the leave entitlement yourself.', '9cbc06ada15c0ad8c4c389a9c292a4f0535e0ec4787b9bf8613e91f2723a6a60', 2042, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:52:58.165+00', '2026-07-30 08:52:58.298939+00', '2026-07-30 08:52:58.165+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('99cc92d9-4a28-4e71-866a-4c1612453b53', 'Part time employees who are paid an annual salary are paid on what is referred to as a ''pro-ra-ta'' basis. This means that the annual salary amount they will receive will be a proportionate amount of the full time employees salary. For example: If a Full time employee works 38 hours per week and a Part time employee works 22.8 hours per week, The part time employee works a full time equivalent (FTE) of 0.60 (60% of the FTE being 22 point 8 divided by 38 is equal to 0.60). Therefore, if the FTE salary is $70,000, then the part time employee will be paid: $70,000 times 0.60 is equal to $42,000, for working 22.8 hours per week.', 'd5be8a3fd4b039888614aee31acb9441868ef85effca9676ad0fe0e91f95754b', 631, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:53:12.333+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:12.46188+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:12.333+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('40ff6eda-3991-4973-82b7-c2cb2fb995af', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers the protected earnings amount, the part of a pay that a child support deduction is never allowed to touch. Child support deductions are enforceable and taken after tax, but they are not unlimited. The protected earnings amount, or PEA, is the portion of an employee''s or contractor''s pay that stays exempt from child support deductions no matter what the notice says. It exists so a deduction can never leave someone without enough to live on. The PEA is indexed every year on 1 January. For pay dates from 1 January to 31 December 2026, the weekly figure is $544.88, giving a daily rate of $77.84. From there it scales up with the pay cycle, fortnightly is double the weekly figure, four weekly is four times it, and monthly is the daily rate multiplied by 30.4375 days, the average days in a month. In practice, payroll compares the amount the child support notice asks for against what is left once the protected earnings amount is set aside. If deducting the full notified amount would cut into the protected earnings amount, the deduction is capped so the employee keeps at least that amount. Because the figure is indexed on 1 January each year, check for an updated amount at the start of every calendar year before applying it. Keep the current protected earnings amount on file for each pay cycle you run, apply it automatically alongside every child support deduction, and update it as soon as the new calendar year''s figure is published. Getting this one number right protects the employee''s basic living income while still meeting a legally enforceable obligation.', '99af55a195b63d5faf967b516086ff38afeab9394a1541bfbb131242ba4ba16e', 1625, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:53:19.509+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:19.641338+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:19.509+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('52b2921e-9ffb-4e41-aed8-e9bc7d56c4d7', 'Welcome to Payroll tax, rates, thresholds and levies. This video looks at payroll tax rates and thresholds, how they vary between jurisdictions, and the extra levies that some states add on top. Start with the threshold, the amount of wages you can pay before any payroll tax applies. Most states set an annual threshold and simply divide it by twelve for a monthly figure. But New South Wales and Tasmania are different: they apportion the annual threshold by the number of days in each month. So a thirty one day month gives a slightly higher monthly threshold than a twenty eight day month, and the figures shift again in a leap year. The tax rate also differs in every jurisdiction. New South Wales, for example, applies five point four five percent above its threshold, and importantly it never takes the threshold away, no matter how high the wages climb. Other states reduce or remove the threshold as wages grow, which we cover separately. Two states do something different again: instead of changing the threshold, they change the rate as wages rise. Tasmania has a lower rate on wages in its first band and a higher rate above two million. South Australia uses a sliding rate from nil up to its top rate. And from the first of July twenty twenty six, the ACT adds a higher rate for large employers. The threshold concept is the same, it is the rate that varies. On top of the rate, some states add levies. Victoria has a mental health and wellbeing surcharge: an extra half a percent once Australia wide wages exceed ten million, and another half a percent above one hundred million, so up to one percent more. Victoria also has a COVID debt levy. Queensland has its own mental health levy on a similar pattern, a quarter of a percent above ten million and another half a percent above one hundred million. So when you work out a Victorian or Queensland liability for a large employer, you are layering the levy on top of the base payroll tax. Picture it as two parts: the base tax on the taxable wages, plus the surcharge on the wages above each levy threshold. It feels like paying roughly one percent extra at the top end, but you calculate each piece separately so the figures are defensible. The practical takeaway: never assume a rate or threshold from memory. Confirm the current figure for the specific state, check whether a day based threshold applies, and check whether a levy is in play. A small difference in rate across a large payroll is a large difference in dollars.', 'f4d756a739a27ea89f943dab50a1682efab9099660650909f34e702d1d960852', 2493, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:53:49.548+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:50.121525+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:49.548+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6c221919-1707-4828-91ec-c5bbb64e9596', 'When a travel allowance is paid above the ATO''s reasonable amount, only the excess needs to be taxed, and the mechanics of splitting that calculation correctly deserve their own close look. In this video we''ll walk through exactly how to isolate and tax the excess. Start from the reasonable amount published by the ATO for the relevant year, role and destination. That figure represents what the ATO accepts as a genuine, unsubstantiated daily cost of accommodation, meals and incidentals for someone travelling for work to that location. If the employer pays exactly that reasonable amount or less, the whole allowance is untaxed and unreported, full stop. If the employer pays more, the calculation splits cleanly into two pieces: the reasonable portion, still untaxed, and the excess, the amount paid above the reasonable rate, which is treated as ordinary assessable income and taxed accordingly. This means you never tax the whole allowance just because part of it exceeds the reasonable rate, only the excess portion is added to taxable income and has PAYG withheld, the reasonable component underneath it stays completely untouched. Get the reasonable rate wrong, using an outdated year''s figure, or the wrong destination or role category, and this calculation goes wrong in either direction: overtaxing an employee on money that should have been tax-free, or undertaxing on an amount that should have been assessable. Always pull the current rate from the ATO for the specific year and destination before doing this calculation. When a travel allowance exceeds the reasonable rate, tax only the excess above that rate, not the whole payment, and always confirm you''re using the correct year''s published reasonable amount for the specific destination and role before running the split.', '332e89197cff9514963fd7bf468df73c72a270ea37f3884d4729331f648d0b84', 1793, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:54:22.535+00', '2026-07-30 08:54:22.664089+00', '2026-07-30 08:54:22.535+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8fe35dc8-7c8a-4762-8816-b58410e9bf2a', 'Our fourth section covers one of the most common areas where employers get things wrong. The difference between an employee and an independent contractor. Click Start to Begin. Distinguishing between an employee and an independent contractor is one of the most common areas where employers make mistakes. When we say contractor here, we mean an independent contractor. That is an entity, which might be an individual, a partnership, a trust or a company, that agrees to produce a designated result for an agreed price. They generally operate under their own Australian Business Number and issue invoices for their work. Here is why it matters for payroll. Someone treated as an independent contractor may actually be an employee in the eyes of the law. If that happens, you have superannuation obligations and payroll tax obligations that have not been met. Engaging someone as a contractor when they are really an employee, even at their own request, can amount to sham contracting, which carries significant penalties. In most organisations payroll is not the team making the decision. A contractor might be engaged by an operations manager at a site, sign an agreement, and invoice accounts payable, with payroll never seeing it. That is exactly the risk. You do not need to determine the answer yourself, but there should be a process in place so that payroll knows the answer, because payroll carries the liability. Let''s work through the factors used to decide whether someone is an employee or an independent contractor. The first and most significant is the measure of control. An employer usually has the right to control how, when and where a worker performs their duties, and tasks are performed at the employer''s request. A contractor works at their own initiative to achieve a stated result. They keep discretion and flexibility over how the work is completed, although the contract may specify some terms about materials or methods. Second is exclusivity. An employee usually works exclusively for one employer. A contractor is free to provide services to multiple clients at the same time. Third is payment. An employee is generally paid for their time, whether that is hourly, weekly or as an annual salary. A contractor is generally paid for providing services or completing units of work, and usually issues tax invoices. Payment is a useful signal. If you are paying someone for every hour they work rather than for a completed piece of work, that points towards an employment relationship. The next three factors are delegation, risk and hours of work. An employee is personally engaged to perform their role and has no inherent right to delegate it to someone else, unless the employer authorises it. You cannot decide one morning to send a friend in to do your job for you. A contractor may delegate all or some of the tasks to another person, and may employ others to perform the services, although the contract may require the payer''s consent. Risk is a strong indicator. An employee bears little or no responsibility to rectify poor work. The employer is responsible to others for the poor work of an employee. A contractor must rectify poor work at their own cost and effort, and carries the commercial risk of any loss the payer suffers because of it. So ask yourself, if something went badly wrong, who would be liable? If the answer is the business rather than the worker, that points towards employment. Hours of work follow the same logic. An employee has their hours set by the employer. A contractor can set their own hours, as long as the services are performed. A web designer might choose to work in the middle of the night, and that is entirely their call. The last three factors are tools and equipment, method of engagement, and whether the worker is part of the business. Employees generally perform their work using tools and equipment provided by the employer, at the employer''s place of work. They arrive and are given a computer or a phone if the role requires one. A contractor generally provides their own tools and equipment. A painter brings their own ladder and brushes. Method of engagement is a useful test because it is easy to check. Employees are always personally engaged. If an individual is engaged through a trust, a partnership or a company, that usually indicates a contractor relationship. Finally, being part of the business. The work of an employee is usually essential to the business carried on by the employer. The employee is working in the business of the employer. A contractor carries on their own business, independently of and distinct from the employer''s business. One last and very important point. These factors do not carry equal weight. They may be weighted differently depending on the individual facts of each case, so you look at the whole picture rather than counting boxes. Once you know whether someone is an employee or a contractor, the withholding treatment follows. If a worker is an employee, the payer must withhold an amount from any salary, wages, commissions, bonuses or allowances paid to them. That is straightforward. If a worker is an independent contractor, a payer is only required to withhold in three situations. First, where the contractor has entered into a voluntary agreement with the payer to have amounts withheld. Second, where the contractor provides their work or services for a client of the payer under a labour hire arrangement. And third, where the contractor has not quoted their Australian Business Number to the payer. There is one more obligation to keep in mind. In some cases the superannuation guarantee laws may apply to payments for work or services performed by an independent contractor. So even where you are not withholding tax, super may still be payable. That is one of the main reasons payroll needs visibility over contractor engagements.', '68764a08db0ed2d684d3a54fa1f586310bdcbf5efa8e6cdd5e117b216ee1b28f', 5868, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:04.801+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:04.812418+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:04.801+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('de321f9d-3aaf-4c76-8b02-f87084ff7e6f', 'We will begin with the Fair Work system itself. This is the framework that decides who gets what, how much they are paid, and how much leave they accrue. Click the start button to begin learning. Most employment relationships in Australia are covered by the national workplace relations system, which we usually call the Fair Work system. It rests on two pieces of law. The Fair Work Act 2009 and the Fair Work Regulations 2009. Together they create a safety net of minimum entitlements that an employer cannot go below. It is worth knowing where the boundary sits between the two big regulators you will deal with in payroll. Fair Work tells you what to pay, who to pay it to, and what leave someone accrues. The Australian Taxation Office tells you how to tax it and how much superannuation to pay. They are separate bodies with separate rules, and they do not always line up. Keeping that distinction clear in your head will save you a lot of confusion later in the course. The Fair Work Act covers most Australian workplaces, but there are exceptions, and we will look at those next. So who is actually in the national system? The starting point is that most employees in Australia are covered by the Fair Work Act. In the Australian Capital Territory and the Northern Territory, coverage is complete. Every employer and every employee is in the national system. In Victoria, almost everyone is covered as well. The exception is state government employees working in sectors that provide essential services or core government functions, such as state infrastructure services like electricity and gas. Everyone else in Victoria sits in the national system. Western Australia works differently again, and we will come to that shortly. The practical point for payroll is that you need to check. Contact the relevant state or territory industrial relations department, or ask your human resources team, so you know with certainty which system your employer sits under. If the employer is in a state system, the National Employment Standards do not apply. That state will have its own standards which are similar but not identical. Let''s look at the exceptions more closely. In New South Wales, South Australia and Queensland, state public sector and local government employees are not in the national system. They remain under their state system. Tasmania is slightly different. State public sector employees stay under the state system, but local government employees are covered nationally. There is an important qualifier across all of these. Some state public sector and local government employers have registered agreements in the national system, and the employees covered by those agreements are inside the national system. So the answer is not always determined by the sector alone. Western Australia has its own list. Sole traders, partnerships, other unincorporated entities, non trading corporations and local governments are not covered by the national system. Those businesses and their employees fall under the Western Australian state system, along with state public sector and local government employees. From a payroll perspective, the Fair Work system has four key features worth committing to memory. The first is the twelve National Employment Standards, or N E S. These are the minimum entitlements that apply to every employee in the national system, no matter what industry they work in or who they work for. We cover these in detail in the next module. The second is modern awards, which apply nationally to specific industries and occupations. The third is registered agreements, which apply to a particular business or group of businesses. The fourth is the national minimum wage, which is reviewed and set every year. Fair Work also sets the high income threshold, which is one hundred and ninety thousand one hundred dollars for the year commencing the first of July 2026. That threshold sits mostly on the human resources side. For payroll, the thing to know is that an employee guaranteed to earn above it cannot bring an unfair dismissal claim. The national minimum wage is the safety net. It sets the minimum rate of pay for employees in the national system who are not covered by an award or an agreement. For the first full pay period on or after the first of July 2026, the national minimum wage is one thousand and four dollars and ninety cents per week before tax, or 26 dollars and 44 cents per hour. That is the adult rate. It is not the rate for everyone. The Minimum Wage Panel of Fair Work Australia also sets two special national minimum wages for employees with disability, and special rates for trainees, apprentices and juniors. So if you see an hourly rate below 26 dollars and 44 cents, that is a prompt to check. It may be perfectly correct for a junior or an apprentice, or it may be an underpayment. Fair Work also sets the high income threshold, currently one hundred and ninety thousand one hundred dollars, and that amount is indexed each year.', 'dfbf23f1705da0b0dc2573efa66f39d06ed3f2c6797dccc09d0173126ccd3a91', 4998, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:21.96+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:21.981708+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:21.96+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('1baaef30-a81b-415d-9504-4314bcf21f31', 'An employee who is on unpaid leave on both the day before and the day after a public holiday generally won''t be paid for that public holiday, and understanding exactly why avoids a lot of confusion at payroll. In this video we''ll cover the logic behind this NES rule and where the edge cases sit. The NES entitles employees to be paid for public holidays that fall on a day they''d normally be rostered to work. But that entitlement is built around the idea of an ordinary working day, if the employee wasn''t going to be at work anyway because they''re on a period of unpaid leave surrounding that day, there''s no ordinary working day for the public holiday to attach to. The specific test that trips people up: it''s not just about the public holiday itself, it''s about whether the employee is on unpaid leave on both sides of it, the working day immediately before, and the working day immediately after. If they''re on unpaid leave surrounding the holiday on both sides, they generally don''t get paid for the holiday itself. Compare that to an employee who returns from unpaid leave the day after the public holiday but was actually rostered to work, or paid, on the day before it. In that case, the public holiday still falls within their period of ordinary employment, and payment obligations look different, this is where you always check the specific facts rather than assuming a blanket rule for anyone near unpaid leave. This rule exists because the public holiday entitlement isn''t a stand-alone benefit that attaches to the calendar date regardless of circumstances, it''s tied to the ordinary working pattern the employee would otherwise have had. Take that ordinary working pattern away on both sides, through unpaid leave, and the public holiday payment has nothing to attach itself to. Before processing pay around a public holiday, always check what surrounds it for each employee: were they on paid leave, unpaid leave, or ordinarily rostered on both the day before and the day after? Get the surrounding days right, and the public holiday payment decision follows naturally from there.', '2c12c7f77967dc3036c32322ff775c3cc33382fdc2fa109b51eb0b9c69b51f36', 2098, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:53:33.326+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:33.458337+00', '2026-07-30 08:53:33.329+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('0dfa020c-2bb2-46fc-bae5-82231e03641a', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers record keeping obligations, what payroll must keep, for how long, and why it matters. Record keeping starts with why it matters. Accurate records prove compliance to a Fair Work Inspector, resolve pay disputes quickly, and support super, tax and workers compensation claims. Poor records leave a business exposed, with the burden of proof falling on the employer, not the employee, when a dispute arises. Every employee record must meet five standards. It must be readily accessible to a Fair Work Inspector, legible and in plain English, kept for seven years, never altered except to correct an error, and never false or misleading to the employer''s knowledge. General records cover the basics, the employee''s name, start date and whether they are full time, part time or casual. This is the foundation every other record builds on. Pay records must show the rate of pay, gross and net amounts, and any deductions, loadings, penalty rates, bonuses or other separately identifiable entitlements paid. Hours of work records capture when an employee actually worked, including overtime, so pay can be verified against time and attendance data at any point. Leave records track balances, leave taken, and any agreement to cash out leave. Superannuation contribution records show what was paid, when, and into which fund, alongside records confirming the employee was offered a choice of fund where required. Termination records round out the file, capturing the reason for termination, the notice provided, and who terminated the employment. Together, these categories form a complete, defensible employee record. Getting record keeping right is not paperwork for its own sake, it is what lets payroll answer any question, from an employee, an auditor, or a court, with confidence and evidence.', '13efcf0fbba31866873b92443483fbf44d43a628329cc945805f5267839bffa7', 1840, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:54:39.585+00', '2026-07-30 08:54:39.718832+00', '2026-07-30 08:54:39.585+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('19fdc914-f119-4850-9486-d73a4b813bf6', 'Once an overpayment has already had tax withheld and remitted to the ATO, recovering it from the employee gets genuinely more complicated, because the employee didn''t receive that portion at all, it''s already gone to the government. In this video we''ll cover how this actually plays out. When an employee is overpaid, PAYG withholding is calculated and remitted on the full overpaid amount at the time, just like any other payment. That means the employee only ever received the net amount in their bank account, the gross overpayment minus tax that''s already sitting with the ATO. This creates an awkward practical question: if the employer wants to recover the overpayment, do they recover the net amount the employee actually received, or the full gross figure, some of which the employee never actually got their hands on? Recovering the full gross amount from someone''s net pay effectively asks them to repay money they never received. The correct approach generally involves adjusting future reporting so the employee can claim back the tax that was withheld on an amount they''re repaying, through their own tax return, rather than the employer trying to directly claw back tax already sent to the ATO. The employer recovers the net overpayment from the employee, and the employee separately recovers the excess tax through the ATO at tax time. This is exactly why timing matters so much when an overpayment is caught. The sooner it''s identified and corrected, ideally before the next BAS or STP finalisation, the simpler this process is. The longer it runs, potentially across a financial year boundary, the more this becomes a multi-step correction involving the employee''s own tax return. Never assume you can just deduct the full gross overpayment from an employee''s next pay, because part of that gross figure was never in their hands, it went straight to the ATO as withholding. Work out the net amount actually received, agree that repayment with the employee, and let the tax component genuinely resolve at tax time through their return.', '4824a502a94765b5538b42aa7588b69a7ed3c2d75ec677110f0bbf6b42ee6975', 2051, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:55:39.388+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:39.529145+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:39.388+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('2fd40e22-7d06-459d-90ba-84d88bfae2a8', 'Before we get to how redundancy payments are taxed, we need the NES rules for what an employee is actually owed: notice of termination and redundancy pay. Both are based on continuous service, and both have a small business exemption most payroll teams forget to check. In this video we will cover the notice period scale, in lieu of notice, what happens when an employee doesn''t work their notice, and the redundancy pay scale. Only full time and part time employees receive notice, casuals are covered by their casual loading instead. Continuous service for notice purposes excludes casual periods, unless they were regular and systematic, and excludes unauthorised leave. Employers can either have the employee work their notice, or pay it out as payment in lieu of notice, valued at what the employee would have received if they''d kept working, including loadings, rostered overtime and penalty rates. It''s also the only termination payment that must attract superannuation. The NES notice scale: up to 1 year of continuous service, 1 week. Over 1 year up to 3 years, 2 weeks. Over 3 years up to 5 years, 3 weeks. Over 5 years, 4 weeks. Add one more week if the employee is over 45 with at least 2 years of continuous service. Watch contracts that simply say ''one month''s notice'', that''s 4.3333 weeks, which can underpay an employee who is entitled to 5 full weeks under the NES. No notice is payable for a fixed term contract ending on schedule, end of a traineeship or apprenticeship, or dismissal for serious misconduct. If an employee resigns and simply doesn''t work their notice, you generally cannot deduct the shortfall from other wages unless an award specifically allows it, and even then only from wages actually owed, never from leave entitlements like annual or long service leave, which are not wages. Some awards allow a deduction of up to one week''s pay from an adult employee''s wages if they haven''t provided proper notice, but you can only deduct from wages still owing, not from leave payouts. Redundancy pay follows its own NES scale, based on continuous service: 1 to 2 years, 4 weeks. 2 to 3, 6 weeks. 3 to 4, 7 weeks. 4 to 5, 8 weeks. 5 to 6, 10 weeks. 6 to 7, 11 weeks. 7 to 8, 13 weeks. 8 to 9, 14 weeks. 9 to 10, 16 weeks. At 10 or more years it drops to 12 weeks, because long service leave is expected to have kicked in by then in most states. Awards and agreements can be more generous, never less. Small businesses, fewer than 15 employees, are exempt from paying redundancy pay altogether. No redundancy pay is owed at the end of a fixed term contract, or the end of a traineeship or apprenticeship either. Get this right before you look at tax: confirm continuous service for notice and redundancy purposes, apply the correct NES scale, check whether the small business exemption applies, and remember payment in lieu of notice always attracts super, no matter how the rest of the payment is taxed.', '6628113aea2f2bee8628fa4f4e5b6b256452a6b42994fd427a421a3f0062329d', 2933, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:55:46.969+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:47.098449+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:46.969+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('4ee5ba1c-08fa-48f1-9980-82e8e553b840', 'Redundancy, or severance, pay under the NES scales with length of continuous service, and there''s a specific extra entitlement that kicks in based on the employee''s age too. In this video we''ll walk through the scale and a worked comparison. Severance or redundancy pay is based on the length of the employee''s continuous service, more years generally means more weeks of pay, following the NES scale from as little as a few weeks after one year, up through a maximum band for longer tenures. On top of the service-based scale, an additional entitlement applies once the employee is over 45 years of age with at least two years of continuous service, they receive an extra week of redundancy pay on top of what the standard scale already provides for their tenure. This means two employees with identical years of service can end up with different redundancy payouts if one is over 45 and the other isn''t, it''s a genuine age-based addition, not just a rounding difference. Always check both the length of continuous service and the employee''s age before finalising a redundancy calculation. Take an employee who is 57 years old, a strong early signal to check the extra week eligibility immediately given they clearly clear the age 45 threshold, then confirm their continuous service against the standard scale to find the base weeks owed, and add the extra week on top if both service and age conditions are met. Work redundancy pay in two clear steps: apply the NES service-based scale first to get the base weeks, then check age and service against the over-45 test to see if an extra week applies. Skipping the age check is one of the easiest ways to underpay a genuinely eligible employee.', '3abe2ea001620d65ea76efc56dc7dc98b28a295f1a73dfd596828ef8a738706b', 1694, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:55:59.804+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:59.933924+00', '2026-07-30 08:55:59.804+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('86cbf754-978c-4da8-a2f7-6d39e1fc984f', 'Welcome to this training series on the Australian Industrial Relations System and the General Retail Award. Over this series of four videos, we''ll cover everything payroll professionals need to know — from the structure of modern awards through to individual flexibility arrangements. In this first video, we''re focusing on the big picture: how the award system works and what it means for your payroll obligations. Let''s get started. Across these videos we''ll be covering: the Australian Industrial Relations System overview; definitions and award coverage; types of employment — full-time, part-time, and casual; minimum wages and allowances; payment of wages; ordinary hours, shift penalties, and overtime; rostering and breaks; leave entitlements; superannuation as it appears in the award; award variations and how to stay on top of changes; and finally, individual flexibility arrangements. There''s a lot to get through, so let''s make it as practical as possible. The General Retail Award is a modern award that sets out the minimum terms and conditions of employment, sitting on top of the National Employment Standards — or N.E.S. The N.E.S came into effect on the 1st of January 2010. The General Retail Award is an industry award, and it applies to employers and employees who perform work covered by the award. Every modern award has information about who is covered. It outlines terms and conditions such as pay, hours of work, rosters, breaks, allowances, penalty rates, and overtime. Think of the NES as the foundation — the absolute minimum. Employees cannot be paid less than the National Employment Standards. The NES covers things like the maximum 38 ordinary hours per week, annual leave, community leave, and long service leave. Modern awards then build on top of the NES, adding detail on base rates, allowances, penalty rates, and overtime. Some employees are also covered by an enterprise agreement — but here''s the critical rule: an agreement cannot pay less than the relevant modern award. If the agreement rate falls below the award rate, the employee automatically receives the award rate. The agreement conditions still apply for allowances and other entitlements — but not the pay rate. And of course, neither the award nor the agreement can undercut the NES. To work out which award applies, we always start with the coverage clause — usually clause four of the award. If there are no exclusions in the coverage clause, we move to the job classification, which is typically found in a pay clause or a schedule. Now — if a business is covered by a registered agreement, the modern award conditions are generally no longer relevant. However, the base rate of pay under the agreement cannot fall below the relevant modern award rate. Some employees won''t be covered by any award or agreement — they''re referred to as award-free or agreement-free. This can include managers or high-income earners — but be careful. Don''t automatically exclude all managers or high-income employees from award coverage. Even if they earn above the high-income threshold, they may still be within the jurisdiction of the Fair Work Commission and covered by the NES. So to recap Video 1: the General Retail Award is a modern award that sits above the NES. Awards and agreements cannot undercut the NES, and agreements cannot undercut the award. Determining coverage always starts with the coverage clause, then moves to classification. In Video 2, we''ll go deeper into who is actually covered by the General Retail Award — including some practical examples. See you there.', '8703b0e1c28d9cadaf3f78d7ad413d0ecedb83a452e9ab1ceecf5309ca6ecc98', 3578, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:56:22.347+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:22.477594+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:22.347+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('9d713793-9be6-4205-92af-9627c54a1799', 'A shift worker is not a fourth type of employee alongside full time, part time and casual, it''s simply a work pattern. In this video we''ll clarify exactly what makes someone a shift worker, how that changes what they''re paid without changing their employment status, and why the ordinary hourly rate never moves. Full time, part time and casual describe the employment relationship: how much certainty of hours an employee has. Shift worker describes something completely different: when those hours are worked. An employee can be full time and a shift worker, part time and a shift worker, or casual and a shift worker, all at once. A shift worker is simply someone who may work their ordinary hours outside the span of ordinary hours set out in their award or agreement. If the Clerks Award says ordinary hours normally sit within a defined daytime window, an employee rostered on regularly outside that window is working shift work, even though their classification, their leave entitlements, and their base rate haven''t changed. This is exactly why shift penalties exist: to compensate for working outside that normal span, evenings, nights, weekends, whatever the award defines. The penalty attaches to the shift, not to the person. If that same employee is later rostered back into standard hours, the penalty disappears, because it was never a permanent feature of their employment, it''s tied to when the hours fall. On a payslip, this looks like three columns for three totally different employees: one on ordinary hours at the base rate, one working an afternoon shift getting the base rate plus a shift loading, and one working nights getting the base rate plus a larger loading. All three have the exact same ordinary hourly rate underneath. It doesn''t matter if you''re a shift worker or a casual, your ordinary rate doesn''t move, only the loading on top of it does. Shift work can also unlock extra NES entitlements in some cases, most notably an additional week of annual leave for genuine shift workers who meet specific criteria. But the starting point is always this: shift work is a pattern of when hours are worked, not a category of employee, and it changes what''s paid on top of the ordinary rate, never the ordinary rate itself. Keep these two ideas separate: employment type tells you the certainty of hours and base entitlements; shift work tells you when those hours fall and what penalty, if any, applies on top.', 'f57d89cfabf5dc73c8d378ec1a84e5503a8498fe8eaee76ae813f395f1e672d1', 2437, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:56:34.668+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:34.802935+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:34.668+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('fdaf5b4f-d8a5-4f6c-b73e-d26b4d2ee891', 'When a new employee doesn''t nominate a super fund, choosing their own or returning the form, you can''t just leave them without a fund, you need to actively find their stapled fund through the ATO. In this video we''ll walk through that process step by step. If a new employee hasn''t returned a choice of fund form, indicating either their own fund or that they want to use the default fund, the employer''s obligation doesn''t disappear, it shifts. You need to go into the ATO business portal and identify the employee''s stapled super fund, an existing fund already linked to them from earlier employment. To find it, you go into the ATO''s system and search using a couple of key identifiers, the employee''s TFN and their date of birth. The TFN is unique to each individual, which makes it the primary reliable identifier, date of birth alone isn''t unique, especially across a larger workforce, so both pieces of information are needed together to correctly match the employee. Once you''ve submitted that request, the ATO determines whether the employee has an existing stapled fund from prior employment and returns that information to you. If they do have one, you pay their superannuation guarantee contributions into that fund, rather than defaulting them into a new fund of your choosing. This process exists specifically to reduce the number of duplicate, low-balance super accounts an individual accumulates over their working life, by keeping them attached to one fund as they move between jobs, rather than a new default fund starting up every time they change employer. Before defaulting any new employee into your organisation''s default fund, confirm they''ve genuinely been given the chance to choose, and if they haven''t nominated one, go through the stapled fund process in the ATO portal using their TFN and date of birth, rather than skipping straight to your default option.', 'd1d99f2a49f2694110ce5c617270e15a7f5cbefa80387fd261646dfc36a0832e', 1887, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:57:44.682+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:44.816723+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:44.682+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('90a2a7d5-3573-4d30-9bea-464473528fb7', 'Welcome to Payroll Advanced. This video covers STP Phase 2 disaggregation of gross, why the ATO wants pay broken into components, and how the main categories work. STP Phase 2 requires gross payments to be split into specific components rather than reported as a single lump gross figure, so the ATO and Services Australia can see the makeup of pay without asking the employer directly. Ordinary hours, shift and public holiday penalties, and casual loading are reported as Gross. Overtime, call back payments, and the identifiable overtime component of an annualised salary are reported as Overtime. Directors'' fees get their own category entirely. Leave payments split further. Annual, long service, personal and similar leave taken is Other paid leave, type O. Community service, defence and jury duty leave is Ancillary and defence leave, type A. Leave cashed out while still employed is type C. Parental leave, government or employer paid, is type P. Allowances split by nature. Working condition allowances like first aid or confined spaces are task allowances, code KN. Qualification and certification allowances are code QN. Tools and equipment supplied by the employee are code TD. Cents per kilometre within or above the ATO rate is code CD, while private travel like home to work is folded into other allowances, code OD. Backpay accrued less than 12 months before payment is reported in whatever category the underlying payment matches. Backpay accrued more than 12 months before is Lump Sum E instead. Ordinary bonuses and commissions get their own category, but a bonus that entices someone back to work, ends industrial action, or withdraws a resignation is a separate return to work payment, Lump Sum W. For example, Louisa works 38 ordinary hours at $40, receives a $300 motor vehicle allowance and a $114 leading hand allowance, and works 3 hours overtime at double time worth $249. This reports as Gross $1,520, Overtime $249, Allowance KN $114, Allowance OD $300, OTE $1,934, and Super $232.08. The same payment reported as ordinary hours becomes an allowance, while the identical payment made during overtime hours is reported as overtime instead. Getting disaggregation right isn''t just a compliance box tick, it directly determines the accuracy of every employee''s income statement, and what Centrelink and child support assessments see about their income.', 'e1634d96705e12f52820dac0286815e2390c18435a99b46aac02bafeaf9eff4f', 2379, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:58:58.853+00', '2026-07-30 08:58:58.98941+00', '2026-07-30 08:58:58.853+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('efdcf8d4-9a90-4e0e-9dc8-6cabd23ab017', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This video covers superannuation: who gets it, what you pay it on, when it is due, and the big change arriving in twenty twenty six. Most employees are entitled to super, currently twelve percent, and that can include eligible contractors whose work is mainly their labour. There are narrow exceptions. An employee under eighteen who works thirty hours a week or less does not get super, though if that same person works thirty hours or more in the school holidays, super applies for those weeks. The other exception is private or domestic work, a nanny or gardener for a household rather than a business, working thirty hours a week or less. Almost everyone else is in. What do you pay super on? The answer is ordinary time earnings, or OTE. That means ordinary hours of work, plus the things attached to ordinary hours: shift loadings, most allowances, bonuses and commissions. What it does not include is overtime. So if an all purpose allowance is paid on overtime hours, that part becomes overtime and carries no super. Annual leave loading is a special case: it is generally subject to super, unless you can show it is there to compensate for lost overtime, in which case it is not. Watch for entitlements that look like they should not attract super but might. Government paid parental leave carries no super, because it is not the employer''s money. Employer funded parental leave and workers compensation are not OTE under the ruling, but many awards add a clause requiring super on any paid leave or while on workers compensation. So always check the award. And unused leave paid out on termination never attracts super, with the single exception of a payment in lieu of notice. When is super due? Right now, contributions must reach the employee''s fund by the twenty eighth day of the month after the end of each quarter. This is the big one to flag: from the first of July twenty twenty six, that changes to Payday Super, where super must be paid at the same time as wages. If you pay it late, short, or to the wrong fund, you become liable for the super guarantee charge, which is painful to deal with, so accuracy matters. Two practical points to finish. If a new employee does not return their choice of fund form, you do not just use the default. You look up their stapled fund in the ATO portal, using their tax file number as the key, and only use the default fund if there is no stapled one. And for salary sacrifice to super, document it properly before it starts: the amount, what it applies to, when it begins, and when the contributions will be sent. It cannot be applied retrospectively. Get super right, because it is the entitlement the ATO watches most closely.', '97fe781b847f8c4e6354be2df0580a44cc5420100c8a75189675b1bd38a85b80', 2718, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:59:20.089+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:20.252877+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:20.089+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d9a9ad75-42a6-433f-81d0-810beb6245a7', 'Getting superannuation wrong doesn''t just mean a late payment, it triggers the superannuation guarantee charge, a penalty regime with three separate components plus a growing list of other ATO penalties. In this video we will unpack the SGC, the stapled super fund process, contractor obligations, and why now is the time to tighten every one of these processes before Payday Super arrives. The superannuation guarantee charge applies whenever you underpay the 12% minimum, miss a contribution altogether, pay into the wrong fund, or pay after the cutoff date, even if the delay was your clearing house''s fault. It has three components: a $20 per employee per quarter admin fee, nominal interest, currently 10% and compounding, and the guarantee shortfall itself, which, importantly, is currently calculated on salary and wages, not ordinary time earnings, meaning items you never normally pay super on, like overtime, get caught in a shortfall calculation. There is good news coming with Payday Super: the shortfall calculation moves to ordinary time earnings instead of salary and wages, and the SGC becomes tax deductible, it currently is not. Two genuine wins buried inside an otherwise more demanding compliance regime. Beyond the SGC itself, the ATO can apply a choice liability penalty for failing to provide a standard choice of fund form, director penalties that make directors personally liable for unpaid super, which cannot be absorbed by the business, general interest charge, administration penalties, and penalties for failing to keep records or lodge SGC statements, sometimes going back years if an audit uncovers a historical underpayment. Since 2021, new employees who don''t choose a fund go through ''stapled super'': you pay them regardless, which creates an ATO-visible employment relationship via STP, then you request their stapled fund through ATO online services using their name, TFN, date of birth and address. The ATO determines the stapled fund using its own history, and notifies the employee via myGov, you don''t have to tell them yourself. Stapled super has real gaps: the ATO can hand you a fund that isn''t complying with your business, and won''t check that for you, and an employee''s stapled fund may lack industry-specific insurance their role actually needs, such as in construction. If a stapled contribution bounces back after the quarterly cutoff, the SGC still applies, even though you followed the process correctly, so process contributions early enough to have room to recover from exactly this kind of failure. Contractors paid wholly or principally for their labour, more than half the contract''s dollar value, are treated as employees for super purposes, even with an ABN and invoice, and super must go to a complying fund in their name, never added to the invoice itself. Contractors engaged to achieve a stated result, and those operating through a company, trust or partnership, are not owed super directly. The ATO has resources to help you tell the difference, and getting it wrong is exactly the kind of error Payday Super will surface faster.', '8367569d5b4dbf629a9a22a5c627bc6a5f90231123e70861bf84ff403e259b29', 3094, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:01:03.4+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:03.564528+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:03.4+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('3e101a6b-5d86-49be-8f90-1440757a4127', 'This video explains Single Touch Payroll: what it sends to the ATO every pay run, what the data is used for, and the real reason it exists. Every time you run a pay run, the data is sent to the ATO automatically, through what is called Standard Business Reporting. You will not see the file itself before it goes, though your software can usually produce a report of what was sent. On the other side, employees can log into myGov at any time and see their income statement, with year to date earnings, because that data comes straight from your reports. Picture the file as a simple workbook with two sheets. The first sheet holds the employer information: the ABN, a contact, and the business management system identifier, which is a unique code for your payroll software. It also carries the totals for that one pay run, the gross and the tax withheld. The second sheet lists every employee, with the same details that used to sit on the paper tax declaration, which is why those triplicate forms are no longer sent in. The employee figures are year to date, not just the current pay. They are rolling totals that grow each pay run. That is why, if you need to correct something from earlier in the same financial year, you can simply make the adjustment and the next report will bring everything back into line. By, say, the middle of the year, an employee''s income statement should show their correct year to date gross and tax. What is the data used for? Three main things. It pre-populates the business activity statement, the W1 and W2 fields, which bookkeepers rely on. It produces each employee''s end of year income statement, replacing the old payment summaries. And it feeds other agencies, for example providing the information that used to require a separation certificate for Services Australia. Now the real reason it exists, which is not administrative convenience. A review of superannuation found the government would have far less to fund retirees than expected, because some employers were not paying super. Showing super on a payslip never meant it reached the fund. Returned cheques would sit in drawers. The ATO had no way to see whether employers were paying enough super, to the right fund, at all. So two streams of data are now matched. From your Single Touch Payroll report, the ATO reads the gross, leave and allowance columns, works out the twelve percent that should be paid, and compares it to the super guarantee you report. Separately, the funds report what they actually received through SuperStream. If your reported super looks short, or if the fund says it received nothing while your report says it was calculated, you will hear about it, often as a super guarantee charge. That data matching is the whole point of Single Touch Payroll.', '78aceee7667d604fcfe6565b7d070a62a1a83e5d4692b916fbd48d0dacdee122', 2775, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:56:59.861+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:59.990916+00', '2026-07-30 08:56:59.861+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('000c0915-e0e3-4e3a-9423-740fd571f175', 'Compliance for superannuation isn''t just paying the right amount, it''s reporting it correctly and running the right choice-of-fund process. In this video we will cover STP reporting requirements for super, offering choice of fund, the new Member Verification Request service, and the super stapling process end to end. Employers must report both YTD employer super liability, field L, and YTD qualifying earnings, capped at the MCB, through STP for every employee. Where a figure is genuinely zero, report zero. Where an employee''s contributions exceed the minimum and can''t be separately identified, report the higher combined figure. For defined benefit fund members, report the YTD amount shown on their payslip, or zero if that figure isn''t available. From 1 July 2027, STP reports will be rejected if they don''t include both the YTD super liability and YTD qualifying earnings. Salary sacrifice amounts get their own reporting types: contributions sacrificed to superannuation are Salary Sacrifice type S, while contributions sacrificed to other benefits, like a novated lease or not-for-profit salary packaging, are type O. Getting the type right keeps these amounts out of ordinary gross payments. Every eligible employee must be offered a standard choice form within 28 days of starting. Beyond that, a fresh standard choice form is required whenever an existing employee requests one, their chosen fund can no longer accept contributions, or the employer''s default fund changes. Once an employee nominates a fund, contributions must start within two months, and employees may request a fund change at any time, but employers are only required to actually action one change per employee in any 12 month period. A new Member Verification Request service, part of the SuperStream changes under Payday Super, lets payroll or software check that an employee''s fund details are valid before a contribution is sent. Use it before a first-time contribution to a fund, after a change in employee details like a name change, or after a contribution has been rejected, it isn''t intended for routine checks before every regular pay run. Where a new employee doesn''t choose a fund, request their stapled fund details from the ATO before defaulting anywhere. If the ATO has no stapled fund on record, only then can contributions go to the employer''s own default fund. Build these into a standing compliance routine: offer choice within 28 days, verify new or changed fund details through MVR at the right moments, request stapled details before defaulting, and report both YTD super liability and YTD qualifying earnings correctly every pay cycle, ahead of the 1 July 2027 hard requirement.', '37515bccb4712f6870fcdbe2ef9cedcd9aa742f9a87ea6d960ddc18f2f822e9c', 2685, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:00:28.776+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:28.901905+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:28.776+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('824c4582-0c03-4f6e-9bff-97a944f27624', 'Getting superannuation wrong doesn''t just mean a late payment, it triggers the superannuation guarantee charge, a penalty regime with three separate components plus a growing list of other ATO penalties. In this video we will unpack the SGC, the stapled super fund process, contractor obligations, and why now is the time to tighten every one of these processes before Payday Super arrives. The superannuation guarantee charge applies whenever you underpay the 12% minimum, miss a contribution altogether, pay into the wrong fund, or pay after the cutoff date, even if the delay was your clearing house''s fault. It has three components: a $20 per employee per quarter admin fee, nominal interest, currently 10% and compounding, and the guarantee shortfall itself, which, importantly, is currently calculated on salary and wages, not ordinary time earnings, meaning items you never normally pay super on, like overtime, get caught in a shortfall calculation. There is good news coming with Payday Super: the shortfall calculation moves to ordinary time earnings instead of salary and wages, and the SGC becomes tax deductible, it currently is not. Two genuine wins buried inside an otherwise more demanding compliance regime. Beyond the SGC itself, the ATO can apply a choice liability penalty for failing to provide a standard choice of fund form, director penalties that make directors personally liable for unpaid super, which cannot be absorbed by the business, general interest charge, administration penalties, and penalties for failing to keep records or lodge SGC statements, sometimes going back years if an audit uncovers a historical underpayment. Since 2021, new employees who don''t choose a fund go through ''stapled super'': you pay them regardless, which creates an ATO-visible employment relationship via STP, then you request their stapled fund through ATO online services using their name, TFN, date of birth and address. The ATO determines the stapled fund using its own history, and notifies the employee via myGov, you don''t have to tell them yourself. Stapled super has real gaps: the ATO can hand you a fund that isn''t complying with your business, and won''t check that for you, and an employee''s stapled fund may lack industry-specific insurance their role actually needs, such as in construction. If a stapled contribution bounces back after the quarterly cutoff, the SGC still applies, even though you followed the process correctly, so process contributions early enough to have room to recover from exactly this kind of failure. Contractors paid wholly or principally for their labour, more than half the contract''s dollar value, are treated as employees for super purposes, even with an ABN and invoice, and super must go to a complying fund in their name, never added to the invoice itself. Contractors engaged to achieve a stated result, and those operating through a company, trust or partnership, are not owed super directly. The ATO has resources to help you tell the difference, and getting it wrong is exactly the kind of error Payday Super will surface faster.', '8367569d5b4dbf629a9a22a5c627bc6a5f90231123e70861bf84ff403e259b29', 3094, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:01:18.851+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:18.982165+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:18.851+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('992df73a-82ef-4980-8831-6afe4a53d5f6', 'Whether a payment is superable comes down to one question: is it ordinary time earnings? Most codes are obvious, but a handful sit in genuine grey areas, and getting them wrong across your whole employee base adds up fast. In this video we will work through leave loading, workers compensation, allowances, and bonuses, the areas the ATO checks most closely. Leave loading is the classic grey area. It was originally introduced to compensate for a lost opportunity to work overtime while on leave, and payments clearly linked to that lost overtime opportunity are not ordinary time earnings. But the ATO has confirmed that leave loading paid simply because an award or agreement provides it, with no real connection to lost overtime, is ordinary time earnings and is superable. Two employees on the same leave loading clause can have different super outcomes, depending on whether they actually work overtime. Workers compensation paid by an insurer, whether it goes through the employer or directly to the employee, is not ordinary time earnings under the Superannuation Guarantee Act. But awards can override that: several awards, including the Clerks Award, require super to continue for up to 52 weeks of work-related injury or illness while the employee remains employed, regardless of who is actually paying them. That means you may need two separate workers compensation codes: one that attracts super, one that doesn''t, depending on which award applies. Paid leave generally attracts super, but company paid parental leave does not, unless your award, agreement, or company policy says otherwise, and jury duty pay doesn''t either under the SG Act, though again, some awards specifically require it. Always check the award before assuming the SG Act''s default position applies. For allowances, working condition allowances like crib, danger and first aid attract super, as does any non-deductible allowance, one the employee can''t claim at tax time, because the ATO treats it as additional salary. Deductible allowances like tools or a genuinely work-related car allowance do not attract super, and neither do on-call allowances outside ordinary hours, cents per kilometre, laundry allowances, or overtime meal allowances. On-call allowances during ordinary hours, like a nurse or doctor''s on-call roster, do attract super. Bonuses attract super in the vast majority of cases, sign-on, performance, and Christmas bonuses are all ordinary time earnings. The single exception is a bonus paid specifically in connection with overtime already worked, for example a one-off bonus for coming in on a weekend to do overtime work, which follows overtime''s own exemption. Employers can choose to pay 12% on an employee''s full ordinary time earnings, or cap contributions at the maximum quarterly contribution base, currently $270,830 of ordinary time earnings per annum It''s the employer''s choice, not the employee''s. Ideally let your system apply the cap automatically pay period by pay period, since a fixed monthly amount can quietly overpay or underpay super if an employee terminates mid-quarter or takes a chunk of unpaid leave. Reportable superannuation contributions, RESC, must be shown on an employee''s income statement because they affect child support, HECS repayments and Medicare levy calculations, even though they don''t change take-home pay. A contribution is RESC when two things are both true: it''s more than 12%, and the employee had influence over it. Pre-tax salary sacrifice above 12% is the classic example, so is an employee who negotiated a higher super rate as part of their job offer. An EBA that simply sets a flat 14% for everyone is not RESC, because the employee had no individual influence over that rate. Before Payday Super lands, run a full review of your superannuation codes: leave loading needs a real test of whether it connects to lost overtime, workers compensation may need a second code depending on your awards, and allowances need sorting into deductible and non-deductible categories. These are exactly the errors that a faster, more frequent super cycle will surface much sooner than the current quarterly cycle does.', '1485014b343ba0d7719b222c8d23980532d9af4605dfee12778a6dada115f6b8', 4162, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:04:44.656+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:44.793357+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:44.656+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('922a0034-1dbd-4833-9e63-e297b3dda3ad', 'Flexible start times, like an employee starting early, breaking for a school pickup, then returning later in the day, are increasingly common, and they can be paid correctly without ever becoming overtime, as long as they''re handled properly. In this video we''ll cover how to document a flexible arrangement so it works for both payroll and the employee. Picture an employee who starts at eight thirty, works until midday, takes a break to do a school pickup, then comes back and works an hour or two more in the afternoon. As long as this pattern is agreed between employer and employee, and reflects their actual ordinary hours for the day, it''s simply their agreed working pattern, not overtime and not a broken shift penalty, provided the award doesn''t say otherwise. The key word is agreed. This only works smoothly when there''s a genuine, documented understanding between employer and employee about the hours being worked and when. Without that documentation, you''re relying on memory when a dispute or an audit comes up, and informal arrangements are exactly where payroll gets caught out. Every flexible arrangement should record: the standard agreed hours, what happens if those hours change on a given day, and whether that change was employee-initiated or employer-directed. This matters because whether extra or shifted hours are ordinary or attract a penalty can come down to exactly this kind of documentation. None of this changes the total hours owed under the award or NES, it just changes how those hours are distributed across the day. A flexible arrangement is a scheduling tool, not a way to avoid entitlements, the same daily and weekly maximums, breaks, and any applicable penalties still apply on top of it. Document every flexible arrangement in writing, keep it updated when the pattern changes, and make sure both employer and employee have signed off on what ''ordinary hours'' actually means for that specific person''s day. It protects the employee''s pay, and it protects payroll if anyone ever asks how a particular day was calculated.', '0f7c9e420d2206b493c1b9cea83731e1b8a7ca17e662f6f3bcaa5bbf25fc8342', 2064, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:57:10.092+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:10.220596+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:10.092+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('193fb78d-10ea-407a-ae4c-c3dcde8df996', 'Flexible start times, like an employee starting early, breaking for a school pickup, then returning later in the day, are increasingly common, and they can be paid correctly without ever becoming overtime, as long as they''re handled properly. In this video we''ll cover how to document a flexible arrangement so it works for both payroll and the employee. Picture an employee who starts at eight thirty, works until midday, takes a break to do a school pickup, then comes back and works an hour or two more in the afternoon. As long as this pattern is agreed between employer and employee, and reflects their actual ordinary hours for the day, it''s simply their agreed working pattern, not overtime and not a broken shift penalty, provided the award doesn''t say otherwise. The key word is agreed. This only works smoothly when there''s a genuine, documented understanding between employer and employee about the hours being worked and when. Without that documentation, you''re relying on memory when a dispute or an audit comes up, and informal arrangements are exactly where payroll gets caught out. Every flexible arrangement should record: the standard agreed hours, what happens if those hours change on a given day, and whether that change was employee-initiated or employer-directed. This matters because whether extra or shifted hours are ordinary or attract a penalty can come down to exactly this kind of documentation. None of this changes the total hours owed under the award or NES, it just changes how those hours are distributed across the day. A flexible arrangement is a scheduling tool, not a way to avoid entitlements, the same daily and weekly maximums, breaks, and any applicable penalties still apply on top of it. Document every flexible arrangement in writing, keep it updated when the pattern changes, and make sure both employer and employee have signed off on what ''ordinary hours'' actually means for that specific person''s day. It protects the employee''s pay, and it protects payroll if anyone ever asks how a particular day was calculated.', '0f7c9e420d2206b493c1b9cea83731e1b8a7ca17e662f6f3bcaa5bbf25fc8342', 2064, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:57:17.988+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:18.144316+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:17.988+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('0abd5400-d5ec-4b8e-b316-dd08c4e4211d', 'When a new employee doesn''t nominate a super fund, choosing their own or returning the form, you can''t just leave them without a fund, you need to actively find their stapled fund through the ATO. In this video we''ll walk through that process step by step. If a new employee hasn''t returned a choice of fund form, indicating either their own fund or that they want to use the default fund, the employer''s obligation doesn''t disappear, it shifts. You need to go into the ATO business portal and identify the employee''s stapled super fund, an existing fund already linked to them from earlier employment. To find it, you go into the ATO''s system and search using a couple of key identifiers, the employee''s TFN and their date of birth. The TFN is unique to each individual, which makes it the primary reliable identifier, date of birth alone isn''t unique, especially across a larger workforce, so both pieces of information are needed together to correctly match the employee. Once you''ve submitted that request, the ATO determines whether the employee has an existing stapled fund from prior employment and returns that information to you. If they do have one, you pay their superannuation guarantee contributions into that fund, rather than defaulting them into a new fund of your choosing. This process exists specifically to reduce the number of duplicate, low-balance super accounts an individual accumulates over their working life, by keeping them attached to one fund as they move between jobs, rather than a new default fund starting up every time they change employer. Before defaulting any new employee into your organisation''s default fund, confirm they''ve genuinely been given the chance to choose, and if they haven''t nominated one, go through the stapled fund process in the ATO portal using their TFN and date of birth, rather than skipping straight to your default option.', 'd1d99f2a49f2694110ce5c617270e15a7f5cbefa80387fd261646dfc36a0832e', 1887, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:57:58.06+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:58.188519+00', '2026-07-30 08:57:58.06+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('2b604d0a-2f4e-4e6c-8acb-6c31ee997a5c', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials Superannuation. This video covers superannuation: who gets it, what you pay it on, when it is due, and the big change arriving in twenty twenty six. Most employees are entitled to super, currently twelve percent, and that can include eligible contractors whose work is mainly their labour. There are narrow exceptions. An employee under eighteen who works thirty hours a week or less does not get super, though if that same person works thirty hours or more in the school holidays, super applies for those weeks. The other exception is private or domestic work, a nanny or gardener for a household rather than a business, working thirty hours a week or less. Almost everyone else is in. What do you pay super on? The answer is ordinary time earnings, or OTE. That means ordinary hours of work, plus the things attached to ordinary hours: shift loadings, most allowances, bonuses and commissions. What it does not include is overtime. So if an all purpose allowance is paid on overtime hours, that part becomes overtime and carries no super. Annual leave loading is a special case: it is generally subject to super, unless you can show it is there to compensate for lost overtime, in which case it is not. Watch for entitlements that look like they should not attract super but might. Government paid parental leave carries no super, because it is not the employer''s money. Employer funded parental leave and workers compensation are not OTE under the ruling, but many awards add a clause requiring super on any paid leave or while on workers compensation. So always check the award. And unused leave paid out on termination never attracts super, with the single exception of a payment in lieu of notice. When is super due? Right now, contributions must reach the employee''s fund by the twenty eighth day of the month after the end of each quarter. This is the big one to flag: from the first of July twenty twenty six, that changes to Payday Super, where super must be paid at the same time as wages. If you pay it late, short, or to the wrong fund, you become liable for the super guarantee charge, which is painful to deal with, so accuracy matters. Two practical points to finish. If a new employee does not return their choice of fund form, you do not just use the default. You look up their stapled fund in the ATO portal, using their tax file number as the key, and only use the default fund if there is no stapled one. And for salary sacrifice to super, document it properly before it starts: the amount, what it applies to, when it begins, and when the contributions will be sent. It cannot be applied retrospectively. Get super right, because it is the entitlement the ATO watches most closely.', '0093b6f8df4cc4a25efc94dc616af54bc95545f507678059f7d183075603f458', 2733, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:59:31.624+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:31.750723+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:31.624+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7c5dc4dd-42e8-4375-af77-ac4c1d68edd3', 'The current superannuation guarantee rate is 12% from the first of July 2025. This means that the employer has to contribute 12% of the employees ordinary time earnings or OTE to the employees superannuation fund. For example, if an employee was paid 2 and a half $1000 in a week of ordinary time earnings, then 12% of that would have to be contributed to the employees superannuation fund, which would be $300. There are some pay elements however that are typically not OTE, like overtime. For example, if you had an employee who had ordinary pay of $1500 and also had some overtime that they had worked and been paid for $300.00, their total pay for that would be $1800. But over time does not have superannuation. So we would only calculate the 12 percent on the $1500 ordinary, which means for this. There will be $180 in super contributed to the Employees Superannuation Fund.', '271a276a0b15c757b514b85b0bc2cccf0d20711f5aad2b6eb042fc8899ed62c0', 881, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 08:59:54.692+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:54.832635+00', '2026-07-30 08:59:54.692+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('043f5534-4e31-40d3-ac75-edaff3c4a71c', 'Every new employee has a right to choose their own super fund, and that obligation sits alongside, but separate from, the tax file declaration process. In this video we''ll cover what an employer must actually offer, and when. Alongside the tax file declaration, every new employee must be given the opportunity to nominate their own superannuation fund, this is a distinct legal obligation, not an optional extra tied to the tax paperwork. The employer must provide the standard choice form, or an approved equivalent, as part of onboarding. If the employee completes it and nominates their own fund, that''s where the super guarantee contributions go. If they don''t return it, the employer''s obligation doesn''t end there, it moves into checking for a stapled fund through the ATO before defaulting to the employer''s chosen fund. This isn''t a one-time event locked to the day someone starts. Employees can generally change their nominated fund later, and when they do, the employer needs a clear process to update contributions from the correct date, not retroactively adjusting past contributions that were correctly paid under the earlier nomination. Getting choice of fund wrong isn''t just a paperwork issue, it can mean super contributions land in the wrong account for months before anyone notices, and correcting that after the fact is far messier than getting the initial nomination right. Treat choice of fund as a standing onboarding obligation, distinct from the tax file declaration, track whether a nomination was actually made, and have a clear process both for the stapled fund fallback and for handling an employee who changes funds partway through their employment.', '60607393997f75e4533aac938a28c2406e71018f34cbe5418a89d831243a0aea', 1680, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:00:05.701+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:05.829235+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:05.701+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e2ec4106-d156-4cf7-8d8b-4820158d6f6d', 'Salary sacrifice arrangements go wrong more often through timing than intent. In this video we will cover what makes a salary sacrifice arrangement effective, two common examples of arrangements that fail, voluntary post-tax contributions and their award-driven deadlines, and how reportable employer super contributions, RESC, get identified and reported. To be effective, a salary sacrifice arrangement must be entered into before the employee becomes entitled to the income, documented as an effective salary sacrifice arrangement, and involve contributions to a complying super fund. It''s recommended that every arrangement be clearly documented and agreed to in writing between employer and employee, under Tax Ruling 2001/10. An effective arrangement can''t include salary and wages, leave entitlements, bonuses or commissions the employee has already accrued or earned before the arrangement started. If the arrangement doesn''t meet these requirements, the amount is simply taxed as assessable income at the time it''s received, the sacrifice doesn''t work. Two common failures: an employee advised on 1 July of a bonus for the previous 12 months'' work can''t then ask to sacrifice it, the entitlement already existed before the arrangement was made. Same with an employee who resigns and asks to sacrifice their accrued annual leave balance, they''d have needed the arrangement in place from when they started accruing it. And employment termination payments can never be salary sacrificed at all, they aren''t future earnings, they''re a payment in consequence of termination. Voluntary employee contributions are post-tax and have their own award-driven deadlines. Under the Clerks Private Sector Award 2020, clause 20.3(c), post-tax contributions must reach the fund no later than 28 days after the end of the month the deduction was made, for example, $300 deducted across June is due at the fund no later than 28 July. Reportable employer superannuation contributions, RESC, must be reported separately through STP whenever the employee has capacity to influence the contribution, salary sacrifice being the clearest example, or where employer contributions exceed the applicable minimum under super guarantee law, an industrial agreement, a fund''s trust deed, or other legislation. Capacity to influence looks at the employee''s relationship with the employer, involvement in negotiations, contribution size, and any non-arm''s-length dealings. Get the arrangement documented before entitlement arises, respect award-driven timing on post-tax contributions, and correctly separate RESC from ordinary employer contributions.', '1674a8f8c38a6c28de00646fa4cb89031e940883fe5aa67494a005716410a7b9', 2628, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:01:45.393+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:45.522518+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:45.393+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('2aa4aadb-9260-4bde-bed8-fcd6601121b4', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. The National Employment Standards are the minimum entitlements that sit behind every pay. In this video we get oriented on all twelve, then focus on the part payroll touches every day: hours of work. The National Employment Standards are the minimum entitlements that apply to every employee in the national system. There are now twelve, it was ten until a couple of years ago. It helps to group them. The first three deal with hours and arrangements: maximum weekly hours, the right to request flexible working, and casual employment. The next group is leave: parental, annual, personal and carer''s, compassionate, family and domestic violence, community service, and long service leave, plus public holidays. The last group covers superannuation, notice of termination and redundancy, and the Fair Work Information Statement. In this video, we focus on the first group: hours and arrangements. The first standard sets the maximum ordinary hours of work at 38 a week. Can an employee work more? Of course, but anything beyond their ordinary hours is overtime. So if a contract says a 40 hour week, it really should read 38 ordinary hours plus two hours of reasonable overtime, because your ordinary hours cannot exceed 38. Hold that thought, because the line between ordinary hours and overtime is the single most important distinction in payroll. Here is why it matters so much. Ordinary hours carry leave accruals and superannuation; overtime hours generally do not. And the ordinary hourly rate should be the same for everyone: day worker, shift worker, casual. Penalties and loadings, like a shift loading or a casual loading, attach on top of that ordinary rate; they do not change it. The only time the hourly rate itself changes is for overtime. If you bury a loading into someone''s base rate, you will distort overtime, leave accruals and long service leave down the track, so keep ordinary hours and overtime cleanly separated. When overtime starts depends entirely on the award. Take two part time employees working the same days. Under some awards, the hospitality and nurses awards for instance, a part timer can work additional hours up to 38 before overtime applies. Under the Clerks Award, any hours beyond their agreed contract hours are overtime straight away. Same hours, different result, because the award says so. The National Employment Standards only give you the 38 hour ceiling; for the trigger point you must read the relevant award or agreement. Let us make this concrete. An afternoon shift worker on, say, a 15 percent loading should show ordinary hours at the base rate, with the shift loading as a separate line on top. The ordinary rate itself is unchanged. A casual is the same: show the ordinary rate and the casual loading separately. The danger is mapping a loaded rate as the base rate, because overtime is usually calculated on the ordinary rate without the loading. Get that wrong, and you will overpay overtime, or worse, distort entitlements that depend on the true ordinary rate. Some awards let you average an employee''s ordinary hours over a roster cycle, say over two, three or four weeks. So a full timer might work 42 hours one week and 34 the next without triggering overtime in the heavy week, as long as the average stays within the limit. Two conditions apply: the award must allow it, and the employee must agree. And remember the flip side, you can ask an employee to work reasonable additional hours, but if it is unreasonable for them, given safety or personal circumstances, they can decline. The second standard gives certain employees the right to request flexible working arrangements: carers, people with a disability, parents of school age or younger children, employees over 55, and those experiencing family and domestic violence. A request might be to change hours, change the pattern of work, or change location. But it is a right to request, not an automatic right to receive: the arrangement still has to meet the needs of the business, and the employer must respond, with reasons, within 21 days. That timeframe matters, there have been cases lost purely because the employer did not respond in time. The third standard covers casual conversion, now called the employee choice pathway. A casual who has been with a large employer for six months, or a small employer for twelve, can request to become permanent. A small employer here means fewer than 15 employees, counting associated entities, not just the one company. Note the shift: the onus is now on the employee to make the request, and the employer must respond within 21 days. They can decline on reasonable grounds, for example, the role genuinely will not continue. And payroll often gets pulled in to provide the hours data that supports that decision. Those are the three standards covering hours and arrangements: maximum weekly hours, flexible working, and casual employment.', '9304b58a12443531c3df3dca6c129c2851696400c2330b794134d73c967c5087', 4939, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:03:20.829+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:20.962824+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:20.829+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6bb410ec-a500-4890-ad41-423ee6195c2d', 'The whole of income cap is the one that catches payroll teams out, because unlike the ETP cap, it''s personal to each termination, and it doesn''t index. In this video we''ll define it precisely, work through two full examples, and cover the rare pre-1983 service tax-free rule. The whole of income cap only ever applies to non-excluded ETPs, code O. It starts at $180,000 for everyone, and that figure has not changed in many years, it does not index like the ETP cap does. From that $180,000, you subtract the employee''s year to date gross earnings, then subtract any annual leave or long service leave being paid out on termination, then subtract any outstanding final wages. What''s left is that employee''s personal whole of income cap for this termination. Because it''s reduced by earnings already received this financial year, no two employees have the same whole of income cap, it depends entirely on their individual circumstances. A high income earner who has already earned $190,000 this year effectively has no whole of income cap left at all, meaning any non-excluded ETP they receive is taxed at 47% from the first dollar. Take George: 63, 12 years'' service, $130,000 year to date, $4,000 long service leave and $3,500 final pay on termination, and $6,000 in lieu of notice on resignation, a non-excluded ETP, code O. Whole of income cap: $180,000 minus $130,000 minus $4,000 minus $3,500 equals $42,500. That''s lower than the $260,000 ETP cap, so it applies. His $6,000 payment is under that cap, and he''s over preservation age, so it''s taxed at 17%, giving $1,020 in tax. Take Roberto: 61, retired, receiving a $50,000 gratuity, non-excluded because retirement alone doesn''t meet the genuine redundancy conditions. His year to date is $100,000, no leave or outstanding wages mentioned. Whole of income cap: $180,000 minus $100,000 equals $80,000, again lower than the ETP cap, so it applies. His $50,000 is under that $80,000 cap, and he''s over preservation age, so 17% tax applies: $8,500. And a genuinely excluded example for contrast: Courtney received $150,000 as compensation for unfair dismissal, harassment and discrimination, which sits in the top, excluded section of Schedule 11, so only the ETP cap of $260,000 applies, the whole of income cap is irrelevant here. At 45, she''s under preservation age, and $150,000 is under the ETP cap, so it''s taxed at 32%, giving $48,000 in tax. There''s one more rare tax-free rule: where an ETP relates to service, most often a long service gratuity, and part of that service occurred before 1 July 1983, the portion of the payment relating to pre-1983 service is entirely tax-free. Calculate it as: payment amount divided by total years of service, multiplied by years of service before 1 July 1983. An employee hired 1 July 1980, terminating 30 June 2025 with a $50,000 payment: 45 years of total service, 3 of them before 1983, so $50,000 divided by 45 times 3 equals $3,333.33 tax-free, with the remaining $46,666.67 taxed as a normal ETP. The whole of income cap starts at $180,000, never indexes, and is reduced by year to date earnings, leave paid on termination, and outstanding wages, making it unique to every employee. It only applies to non-excluded ETPs. Always calculate both the ETP cap and the whole of income cap, and apply whichever is smaller. And remember the rare pre-1983 service rule for anyone with genuinely long tenure.', '6c80ab969095c3dd7fd711cdea1da485510ffcbc96d590d9d8fc170050b500f8', 3403, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:05:20.836+00', '2026-07-30 09:05:20.957045+00', '2026-07-30 09:05:20.836+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8777f41e-d1be-40bd-afda-2156d9cc4fc7', 'Every new employee has a right to choose their own super fund, and that obligation sits alongside, but separate from, the tax file declaration process. In this video we''ll cover what an employer must actually offer, and when. Alongside the tax file declaration, every new employee must be given the opportunity to nominate their own superannuation fund, this is a distinct legal obligation, not an optional extra tied to the tax paperwork. The employer must provide the standard choice form, or an approved equivalent, as part of onboarding. If the employee completes it and nominates their own fund, that''s where the super guarantee contributions go. If they don''t return it, the employer''s obligation doesn''t end there, it moves into checking for a stapled fund through the ATO before defaulting to the employer''s chosen fund. This isn''t a one-time event locked to the day someone starts. Employees can generally change their nominated fund later, and when they do, the employer needs a clear process to update contributions from the correct date, not retroactively adjusting past contributions that were correctly paid under the earlier nomination. Getting choice of fund wrong isn''t just a paperwork issue, it can mean super contributions land in the wrong account for months before anyone notices, and correcting that after the fact is far messier than getting the initial nomination right. Treat choice of fund as a standing onboarding obligation, distinct from the tax file declaration, track whether a nomination was actually made, and have a clear process both for the stapled fund fallback and for handling an employee who changes funds partway through their employment.', '60607393997f75e4533aac938a28c2406e71018f34cbe5418a89d831243a0aea', 1680, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:00:17.524+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:17.652364+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:17.524+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('5f579867-4c48-47d1-b730-250b4b2d34aa', 'Welcome to Payroll tax, what is liable. This video covers what is actually liable for payroll tax: the components of remuneration, how allowances are treated, and the payments that are exempt. Most of what you pay is liable. Ordinary pay, including shift loadings and overtime, is liable. So are superannuation, including salary sacrifice, bonuses and commissions, director''s fees, and employee shares. The key habit is to categorise correctly: if someone earns one hundred thousand and salary sacrifices ten thousand to super, you would report ninety thousand as ordinary pay and the sacrificed ten thousand within superannuation, alongside the employer contribution. Fringe benefits are liable too, but you do not recalculate them. Finance works out the fringe benefits value in the FBT return, and for payroll tax you report the grossed up value using the lower, type two factor, regardless of whether a benefit is type one or type two. A subtle trap with novated leases: even when a post tax contribution reduces the FBT payable to nil, a benefit is still being provided, so the grossed up value is still reported. Allowances are liable unless they fall into an exempt category, and there are two main ones. The first is a travel or accommodation allowance, where the employee stays away overnight. Up to the ATO''s reasonable amount, set each year, it is exempt; only the excess is liable. This year the lowest capital city figure is around three hundred and twenty three dollars a night, split across accommodation, meals and incidentals. The second is the motor vehicle allowance. The exempt portion is the business kilometres travelled multiplied by an exempt rate. One catch worth remembering: this calculation uses the prior year''s rate, not the current one. So while pay as you go uses the current cents per kilometre figure, the payroll tax exempt rate lags a year behind. Anything above the exempt portion is liable. Some payments are exempt outright. Employer funded parental leave, up to fourteen weeks at full pay or twenty eight weeks at half pay, is exempt, and in most jurisdictions so is the super paid on it. The tax free part of a genuine redundancy is exempt. Workers compensation where no work is performed is exempt, including the first weeks the employer funds. And government paid parental leave is exempt, because it is not the employer''s money. So the rule of thumb: treat almost all remuneration as liable, then apply the specific exemptions. Categorise each component correctly, report fringe benefits from the FBT figures grossed up by the type two factor, and only carve out the allowance amounts and the exempt payments the legislation actually allows.', '0fa94407c850683de3dc04e9f305cafeac4f5fbf0496a628a00f5b296f062d1d', 2685, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:04:14.523+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:14.685467+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:14.523+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e63e6c14-9ba8-40ee-ae8e-aae5d81efdad', 'Welcome to what is payroll tax. This video introduces payroll tax: what it is, who has to pay it, and why it works differently in every state and territory. Payroll tax is a little like pay as you go withholding in one respect: it is calculated on what an employer pays its people. But the similarities end there. Pay as you go is a federal tax withheld from the employee. Payroll tax is a state and territory tax, and it is the employer''s own liability, not the employee''s. Each state and territory imposes its own levy on the remuneration a business pays, and the money goes to that state or territory, not to the ATO. A key word here is remuneration, not salaries and wages. Payroll tax is much broader than what shows on a payslip. It captures superannuation, the value of fringe benefits, employee shares, allowances, and often payments to contractors. So when you think payroll tax, think total remuneration from an employer to a worker, not just ordinary pay. Not every business pays it. You only become liable once your Australia wide wages exceed the relevant threshold for the state or territory. A small standalone business with a few employees, well under the threshold and not part of any group, will not pay payroll tax at all. But once your wages cross the threshold, you must register and lodge. There is no single payroll tax. There are eight, one for each state and territory, each with its own revenue office, its own rate, its own threshold, and its own view of what is liable. They are broadly similar, but not identical, so you always confirm the rules for the specific jurisdiction you are lodging in. Back in 2007 the jurisdictions harmonised some things to make life easier. They broadly aligned how to treat motor vehicle and accommodation allowances, how to value fringe benefits, how to handle employee share schemes, superannuation for non working directors, and the grouping of businesses. What they did not align are the rates, the thresholds, and exactly what is liable. Those still differ. Finally, the rhythm. You generally lodge a return each month, then a reconciliation return after year end, around the twenty first of July. The exact lodging dates were meant to be aligned, but in practice they drift a little between jurisdictions, so check the due date for each one you lodge.', '0ed2d66ed9b3ac63f96d34d97b7b1c1a96ceb9b3e0726a03f98df3263ca5a50a', 2317, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:04:17.177+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:17.332313+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:17.177+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6b2710a6-9e8e-4fb7-854b-dd27011e7c57', 'An ABN doesn''t automatically mean a contractor is exempt from super. In this video we will cover when contractors are owed superannuation guarantee, how to calculate it on the labour component of a contract, why adding 12% to the invoice isn''t compliant, and how contractors get a choice of fund too. Contractors who are paid wholly or principally for their labour are considered employees for superannuation guarantee purposes, even if they quote an ABN. Three conditions need to all be met: the contract is wholly or principally for labour, meaning more than half the contract value relates to labour, the individual is paid for personal labour and skills rather than to achieve a specific result, and the work must be performed personally, it cannot be delegated. Where a contract is entered into with an entity such as a company, trust or partnership, rather than the individual performing the work, superannuation obligations generally do not apply. This is the key structural difference: who is actually a party to the contract. Super is calculated on the labour component of the contract, not the whole invoice. If the contract doesn''t clearly separate labour from non-labour components, a reasonable allocation must be worked out, and the ATO accepts market value assessments and standard industry practice for that split. Simply increasing a contractor''s payment by an additional 12% does not satisfy your SG obligation. Contributions must go to a complying super fund, paid on the same day the invoice is paid, not added into the invoice amount for the contractor to arrange themselves. That timing requirement means payroll and accounts payable need to coordinate. Options include having AP pay contractors the same day payroll runs, with contractors set up in the payroll system just for the super component; asking your clearing house or software provider about compliant alternate payment paths; or building components in payroll to pay both the contractor invoice and the super together, instead of through AP. Contractors are entitled to a choice of fund too. If they don''t provide details, employers can request their stapled fund using the ATO''s independent contractor stapled super fund request form, NAT75404. Get the labour test right first, calculate on the labour component, pay on invoice day, and treat contractor choice of fund the same way you would for any employee.', '4bfce0b3b6420b9b592a73f7b2abf40c316b8f9552783f0dc5301dadcee452a9', 2394, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:00:41.887+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:42.011241+00', '2026-07-30 09:00:41.887+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('c1bbbe2d-e2b1-4cb1-a61d-82c46415feaa', 'The whole of income cap is the one that catches payroll teams out, because unlike the ETP cap, it''s personal to each termination, and it doesn''t index. In this video we''ll define it precisely, work through two full examples, and cover the rare pre-1983 service tax-free rule. The whole of income cap only ever applies to non-excluded ETPs, code O. It starts at $180,000 for everyone, and that figure has not changed in many years, it does not index like the ETP cap does. From that $180,000, you subtract the employee''s year to date gross earnings, then subtract any annual leave or long service leave being paid out on termination, then subtract any outstanding final wages. What''s left is that employee''s personal whole of income cap for this termination. Because it''s reduced by earnings already received this financial year, no two employees have the same whole of income cap, it depends entirely on their individual circumstances. A high income earner who has already earned $190,000 this year effectively has no whole of income cap left at all, meaning any non-excluded ETP they receive is taxed at 47% from the first dollar. Take George: 63, 12 years'' service, $130,000 year to date, $4,000 long service leave and $3,500 final pay on termination, and $6,000 in lieu of notice on resignation, a non-excluded ETP, code O. Whole of income cap: $180,000 minus $130,000 minus $4,000 minus $3,500 equals $42,500. That''s lower than the $260,000 ETP cap, so it applies. His $6,000 payment is under that cap, and he''s over preservation age, so it''s taxed at 17%, giving $1,020 in tax. Take Roberto: 61, retired, receiving a $50,000 gratuity, non-excluded because retirement alone doesn''t meet the genuine redundancy conditions. His year to date is $100,000, no leave or outstanding wages mentioned. Whole of income cap: $180,000 minus $100,000 equals $80,000, again lower than the ETP cap, so it applies. His $50,000 is under that $80,000 cap, and he''s over preservation age, so 17% tax applies: $8,500. And a genuinely excluded example for contrast: Courtney received $150,000 as compensation for unfair dismissal, harassment and discrimination, which sits in the top, excluded section of Schedule 11, so only the ETP cap of $260,000 applies, the whole of income cap is irrelevant here. At 45, she''s under preservation age, and $150,000 is under the ETP cap, so it''s taxed at 32%, giving $48,000 in tax. There''s one more rare tax-free rule: where an ETP relates to service, most often a long service gratuity, and part of that service occurred before 1 July 1983, the portion of the payment relating to pre-1983 service is entirely tax-free. Calculate it as: payment amount divided by total years of service, multiplied by years of service before 1 July 1983. An employee hired 1 July 1980, terminating 30 June 2025 with a $50,000 payment: 45 years of total service, 3 of them before 1983, so $50,000 divided by 45 times 3 equals $3,333.33 tax-free, with the remaining $46,666.67 taxed as a normal ETP. The whole of income cap starts at $180,000, never indexes, and is reduced by year to date earnings, leave paid on termination, and outstanding wages, making it unique to every employee. It only applies to non-excluded ETPs. Always calculate both the ETP cap and the whole of income cap, and apply whichever is smaller. And remember the rare pre-1983 service rule for anyone with genuinely long tenure.', '6c80ab969095c3dd7fd711cdea1da485510ffcbc96d590d9d8fc170050b500f8', 3403, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:04:55.657+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:55.780494+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:55.657+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('899a8aea-374b-4de4-b758-9dbd4e147508', 'Welcome to tax on back pay and lump sum payments. A back pay or a bonus cannot just be taxed like a normal week, or you will take far too much. This video shows how the ATO''s lump sum method works, using a worked example. First, what counts. Schedule 5 of the tax rules covers lump sum payments: bonuses, commissions, cashed out leave and back pays. They are all taxed the same way. There are three methods. Method A and Method B2 can be used for any of these, in any year. Method B1 is only for a back pay that relates to specific pay periods in the current financial year. We will work through Method A, then compare it with Method B1. The idea behind Method A is simple, even if the steps look fiddly. A lump sum is spread, or apportioned, across the whole year, so the employee is taxed as if their normal pay had been a little higher all year, rather than being hit with one big taxed amount in a single week. That avoids massively over taxing them. Here is the worked example. Charlie works thirty eight hours a week, was being paid forty dollars an hour, but should have been on fifty five, a fifteen dollar increase from six weeks ago. Step one, the normal gross this period at the correct rate: fifty five by thirty eight is two thousand and ninety. Step two, the tax on that is four hundred and ninety three. Step three, the back pay is fifteen dollars by thirty eight hours by six weeks, three thousand four hundred and twenty, divided by fifty two pay periods, which is sixty five. Step four, add that sixty five to the gross: two thousand one hundred and fifty five. Step five, the tax on that is five hundred and thirteen. Step six, five hundred and thirteen minus four hundred and ninety three is twenty. Step seven, multiply that twenty by fifty two: one thousand and forty. That is the tax to withhold from the back pay. Steps eight to ten are a safety check and the payslip. Step eight works out forty seven percent of the back pay, sixteen hundred and seven dollars, because nobody should be taxed above the top marginal rate. Step nine says use the lower of step seven and step eight, so one thousand and forty stands. Step ten is just how it lands on the payslip if you pay it this cycle: ordinary hours of two thousand and ninety, a separate back pay line of three thousand four hundred and twenty, and total tax of four hundred and ninety three plus one thousand and forty, which is fifteen hundred and thirty three. Method B1 is more accurate but more work. Instead of apportioning, you recalculate each affected pay period: what the employee was actually paid, against what they should have been paid, and you sum the difference in tax. For Charlie that comes to one thousand and ninety eight, a little more than Method A''s one thousand and forty. Method B1 only works if your system is date driven, so it can go back and recalculate each period. If it is not, you use Method A. The takeaway: a lump sum is never taxed like a normal pay. Pick a method your system supports, apply it consistently, and remember the same approach covers bonuses, commissions and cashed out leave, not just back pays. The ATO is not fussy about which method you use, as long as you do not over withhold.', 'a26fa8e26f48ac737fb3716bfa18d8d7c6891980e65d8533831a1e97d3687faa', 3208, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:01:57.408+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:57.553221+00', '2026-07-30 09:01:57.408+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('aee5a686-4c32-4f19-b398-18149be5c135', 'Every termination looks unique on the surface, but payroll really only deals with a handful of underlying reasons, and each one drives what we pay and how we tax it. In this video we will map termination reasons to payments, cover the NES rule on unused annual leave and leave loading, and walk through the seven step marginal rate method most terminations rely on. Your system might store hundreds of termination reasons, but for payroll purposes they collapse into a few groups. Resignation, retirement, transfer, dismissal, contract expiry and abandonment of employment are all taxed the same way. Invalidity, where an employee is medically unfit for their specific role, can unlock a tax-free portion. Genuine redundancy, where the role itself disappears, can also unlock a tax-free portion. And an early retirement scheme, a rare, ATO-approved offer to a selected group nearing retirement, can too. On termination, full time and part time employees are typically paid unused annual leave, plus leave loading if it applied while working, unused long service leave, sometimes unused sick leave or RDOs where the award requires it, payment in lieu of notice if the employer chooses not to have them work it, and any other employment termination payments like a gratuity or golden handshake, plus redundancy pay if applicable. Under the NES, unused annual leave must be paid at the same amount the employee would have received had they taken it during employment. If they received leave loading, allowances, or penalty rates while on leave, you must pay those on termination too, even if the award or contract tries to say otherwise. Awards and contracts can be more generous than the NES, never less. Where an award pays the greater of a flat leave loading percentage or the applicable penalty rate, don''t apply both, and don''t calculate day by day, look at the whole period of leave being taken. Take an employee on $25 an hour: five days a week including one Saturday at a 25% penalty. A full week off, base pay is $625; 17.5% loading is $109.38, versus a 25% penalty on the Saturday alone worth only $31.25, so loading wins. Take only the Saturday off, and the 25% penalty wins instead. The comparison depends entirely on which days fall inside the leave period. Marginal rate is what most terminations use, since resignations outnumber redundancies. The method: 1) find the tax on the employee''s normal pay for a single period, 2) divide the lump sum (leave payout) by the number of pay periods in a year, 3) drop the cents, 4) add that to normal gross pay for one period, 5) find the tax on that new combined figure, 6) subtract the step 1 tax from the step 5 tax, 7) multiply that difference by the number of pay periods in a year. That final figure is the tax on the lump sum. HECS or STSL debts are ignored throughout. Worked example: a weekly employee on $1,650, receiving $3,864 of annual leave on resignation. Step 1: tax on $1,650 is $352. Step 2: $3,864 / 52 = $74.30. Step 3: drop cents, $74. Step 4: $1,650 + $74 = $1,724. Step 5: tax on $1,724 is $375. Step 6: $375 minus $352 = $23. Step 7: $23 times 52 = $1,196. That $1,196 is the tax withheld on the $3,864 annual leave payout.', 'b3d77db6a5b5be6e543efd61287fd85fb0a27b2398a8930692ef1a928f406339', 3199, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:02:05.263+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:05.394101+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:05.263+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7545c2b1-f1da-4b3c-97d8-13ad909ab8ed', 'A travel allowance is designed to reimburse an employee for the genuine cost of being away from home for work, and understanding its tax treatment saves a lot of confusion when payments look unusually large. In this video we''ll cover what travel allowances actually cover and how they interact with a reasonable daily rate. A travel allowance exists to compensate an employee for the extra costs of travelling for work: accommodation, meals, and incidentals while away from their usual place of residence. In most instances, provided it''s paid at a reasonable level, you won''t have to tax it, it''s not taxable and not reportable. That last point deserves emphasis: unlike ordinary salary, a genuine travel allowance paid at or below a reasonable amount doesn''t need PAYG withheld from it, and it doesn''t need to be reported the way regular income is. This is exactly why a travel allowance can appear as a large, separate line item on a payslip without inflating the employee''s taxable income. The ATO publishes reasonable travel allowance amounts each year, varying by destination and role, and if an employer pays more than that reasonable amount, only the excess above the reasonable rate needs to be taxed, not the whole payment. Get the reasonable rate for the relevant location and pay period, then split the payment: reasonable portion untaxed, excess portion taxed as income. Take an employee travelling to a capital city for work who receives an allowance covering accommodation, meals and incidentals for each day away. If that daily rate sits within the ATO''s published reasonable amount for that destination, the whole thing is untaxed. If the employer pays above that rate, only the difference between what''s paid and the reasonable amount gets taxed. Always check the current ATO reasonable travel allowance rates for the relevant year and destination before assuming a travel payment is fully tax-free, they update periodically and vary by location. And keep clear records showing what the allowance was paid for, so if it''s ever queried, you can show the reasonable amount that applied at the time.', '934137307deae2e5ed74ed9a17f64dbebcf28ef2e543fdedf75b01cfffc7a107', 2114, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:03:39.13+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:39.274102+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:39.13+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('71611e95-b1db-4993-9291-e8b17a06baac', 'By now you know the individual pieces: excluded ETPs, non-excluded ETPs, genuine redundancy, the caps. This video puts them together into one decision path, so you can walk any termination from first question to final tax code, and we will run a real example that mixes an excluded and a non-excluded payment in the one termination. The path always starts with the same question: has there been a termination of employment? If not, there is no ETP, full stop, no matter what the payment is called. From there: is the payment for unused annual leave, sick leave, or outstanding salary and wages? Those are never ETPs, they are taxed and reported in their own categories. Next ask why the termination happened. Resignation, dismissal or normal retirement generally produce no ETP unless the amount is above what the employee would have received had they simply resigned, in which case it becomes a non-excluded ETP. Genuine redundancy and approved early retirement scheme payments get their own tax-free portion calculated first, with anything remaining becoming an excluded ETP. Invalidity and compensation for unfair dismissal are also excluded ETPs. Death payments are their own category entirely. Once you know excluded or non-excluded, the steps are the same shape: total the payments, work out any tax-free component for invalidity or pre-1983 service if it applies, then tax what''s left, excluded ETPs against the ETP cap alone, non-excluded ETPs against the smaller of the ETP cap or the whole of income cap. Worked example: Jason, 45, five years'' service, $25,000 other taxable income, $5,000 long service leave and $15,000 final wages. He receives $187,270 in redundancy. The tax-free genuine redundancy amount is $47,603, leaving an ETP portion of $139,667, taxed at 32% since he''s under preservation age, that''s $44,693 of tax, reported as ETP code R. Jason is also paid $140,000 in unused RDOs, a non-excluded payment. Because the excluded portion already used $139,667 of the ETP cap, the new ETP cap is $130,333. His whole of income cap starts at $180,000 and is reduced by his $25,000 YTD, $5,000 LSL and $15,000 final wages, leaving $135,000. The ETP cap of $130,333 is now the smaller cap, so $130,333 is taxed at 32% and the remaining $9,667 at 47%, both reported as ETP code O. The path is always the same: confirm a termination has occurred, rule out unused leave and wages, work out why the termination happened to classify excluded versus non-excluded, calculate any tax-free portion first, then apply the correct cap to what remains. When a termination mixes payment types, work through excluded payments first, they reduce the ETP cap available to the non-excluded payments that follow.', 'd2f574ec1c6dfe0b5c23514ea312ca03fd27b361269a1d158df28def24256102', 2709, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:02:19.685+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:19.813129+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:19.685+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6bc30875-6f5b-4588-99ca-c9bce946c6c0', 'The tax file declaration form looks simple, but a couple of small ticked boxes drive a large chunk of an employee''s PAYG withholding, and getting them wrong is one of the most common new-starter errors. In this video we''ll walk through the form and the mistakes that show up most often. Whether it arrives on paper, through an online form, or via an API integration, every new employee needs to complete a tax file declaration, giving the employer their TFN and the key details needed to withhold tax correctly. Alongside it, they also need to provide a superannuation choice of fund, a related but separate document. The form only needs the basics: name, date of birth, and a handful of tick-box questions. There''s no need for a full address on the newer version, it''s been deliberately simplified. But those tick-box questions matter enormously, especially the one asking whether the employee wants to claim the tax-free threshold. One of the most common errors is an employee ticking the wrong box on that question, sometimes without realising the consequence. Claiming the tax-free threshold at the wrong employer, or not claiming it where they should, changes how much tax is withheld from every single pay, and the employee often doesn''t notice until their tax return doesn''t reconcile the way they expected. Payroll''s job isn''t to give tax advice on which box an employee should tick, that''s genuinely up to their own circumstances, especially if they have more than one job. But payroll''s job is to process the form exactly as completed, and to have a process for catching obviously incomplete or contradictory declarations before they go live in the system. Treat the tax file declaration as more than paperwork to file away, it''s the direct input to weekly, fortnightly or monthly withholding for that employee. Read every field, check the tax-free threshold box specifically, and confirm the superannuation choice of fund form was submitted alongside it, since the two are meant to travel together.', '56d5a893eb2d39a5b16bfa3a843a95325a881c862cf1903da4ff1d99f3111f15', 2009, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:03:05.569+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:05.70168+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:05.569+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('5143c2a5-5b92-48e5-9db7-0cd1a0ebfb5a', 'Welcome to unused leave on termination and overpayments. This video covers two end of cycle tasks: taxing unused leave when someone leaves, and correcting an overpayment, where the fix depends entirely on the financial year. When an employee resigns or is dismissed, their unused annual and long service leave is paid as a lump sum, and taxed using the marginal rate calculation, Schedule 7. If you have done a back pay, this will feel familiar, because it apportions the lump sum across the year in the same way. There are two differences. First, there is no forty seven percent cap, no buffer step. If the result lands above that, it stands. Second, a study loan like HELP is not factored into this calculation, so an employee with a HELP debt will often end up with a tax bill, and their accountant may call you about it. Here is the example. Mark resigns and is owed eleven thousand five hundred and fifty dollars of unused leave. His normal fortnightly gross is three thousand eight hundred and fifty, and he claims the tax free threshold. Step one, tax on his normal pay is eight hundred and eighty. Step two, the lump sum divided by twenty six pay periods is four hundred and forty four. Step four, add that to his normal pay, four thousand two hundred and ninety four. Step five, tax on that is one thousand and twenty two. Step six, the difference from step one is one hundred and forty two. Step seven, times twenty six pay periods, three thousand six hundred and ninety two. That is the tax to withhold from the leave payout. Now overpayments. The first thing to know is what does not matter: when the employee repays. They might repay next week or in two years, and it makes no difference to how you fix it in the system. And you cannot simply claw an overpayment back in the next pay. The only thing that decides the fix is the financial year in which the overpayment is identified: the current year, or a prior one. If the overpayment is found in the same financial year, you only recover the net, the amount that actually reached the employee. Say you paid one thousand too much: three hundred went to the ATO as tax, and the employee pocketed seven hundred. You ask the employee for the seven hundred. Then in the system you reduce their year to date gross by the thousand and the tax withheld by the three hundred. You recover that three hundred automatically, because your next pay run''s PAYG payment is reduced by it. If the overpayment relates to a prior financial year, it is trickier, and you recover the full gross from the employee. You reopen, or unfinalise, last year''s Single Touch Payroll, reduce the gross by the overpayment, and crucially do not touch the tax, then re finalise. Because the gross drops but the tax does not, the ATO sees the employee as having overpaid tax and refunds it to them when they lodge. So the employee gets the net back at the time, plus the tax back from the ATO, and together that returns the full gross to you. The year is closed for the employer, so the tax can only come back through the employee.', 'fa2d03f703b8dd9d84a59f1eae74d3247e09dae3ce87d77734c802839319c6bc', 3060, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:03:51.447+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:51.57417+00', '2026-07-30 09:03:51.447+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('96dd9c49-1d2a-4aa9-a4e7-a697cfc9c18f', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials, tax file declarations and PAYG withholding. This video covers two things you handle when an employee starts and every time you pay them: the tax file number declaration, and how pay as you go withholding is worked out. Part of onboarding is giving a new employee a tax file number declaration, along with a superannuation choice of fund form. The employee has twenty eight days to return the declaration. Whether you collect it on paper or electronically through your onboarding system does not matter, but always use the latest version, because the form changes and old questions disappear. Sometimes the tax file number field is not a number. There are four code shortcuts. Nine ones means the employee has applied and is waiting for their number, and you tax them as normal in the meantime. If twenty eight days pass with no number, you change it to nine zeros, which taxes them at the top marginal rate. Nine threes means they are under eighteen and will not earn enough to pay tax. And nine fours means they receive an Australian government pension and are not required to provide a number. When an employee needs to change what they declared, for example they ticked the wrong box on the tax free threshold, they do not fill out a whole new declaration. They complete a withholding declaration instead, which just updates the change. An employee can also simply ask, by email, for more tax to be withheld, perhaps because they have other income like rent and want to avoid a bill at year end. Withholding more is always fine. Withholding less is different. An employee cannot just ask you to reduce their tax. They must apply to the ATO online for a downward variation. The ATO assesses it and, if approved, writes to the employer. You cannot act on the employee''s word, or even on their copy of the approval. You wait for the ATO''s letter, addressed to you, because it binds only your organisation. If the employee changes employer, they have to apply again. Beyond the standard weekly, fortnightly and monthly tax tables, there are extra ones you will meet. There are tables for study loans like HELP. There is Schedule 5 for lump sums such as bonuses, commissions and cashed out leave. There are separate schedules for unused leave on termination and for employment termination payments. And there is a schedule for working holiday makers, who are taxed at flat rates rather than the usual brackets. In practice you will not look up paper tables, you will use the ATO tax withheld calculator. You enter the pay frequency and the gross amount, with no cents, and answer the same questions that are on the declaration: tax free threshold, residency, study loan. For most employees the only thing that changes is the tax free threshold. One rule to remember: a non resident cannot claim the tax free threshold, and your system should not let you apply it.', 'd55b4cf15c700f42b402043fceb0125bb731664148322500790e6daa2a1df90b', 2892, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:02:46.195+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:46.322432+00', '2026-07-30 09:02:46.195+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('152c7b19-1f9c-4820-8820-9738d8f46d32', 'Redundancy pay is a genuine NES entitlement, but it only applies when a role is actually redundant, not simply when someone is being let go. In this video we''ll cover exactly what that means and where it gets complicated. Redundancy happens when an employee''s role is no longer required to be done by anyone, the tasks either disappear entirely or get absorbed elsewhere, restructured, automated, or the position is genuinely no longer needed by the business. It also happens where the employer becomes bankrupt or insolvent. That insolvency scenario deserves its own note: when a business becomes bankrupt or insolvent, redundancy pay may technically be owed, but employees can have real trouble actually receiving it, since there may not be funds available to pay it out. This is different to a healthy business simply choosing to restructure and being able to fund entitlements as normal. The test is about the role, not the person. If a role still exists and is simply being given to someone else, that''s not redundancy, no matter how it''s described internally. Genuine redundancy requires that nobody is doing that job anymore, the position itself has ended. This distinction matters immediately for payroll, because genuine redundancy unlocks different entitlements and different tax treatment than an ordinary termination. Getting the classification wrong, treating an ordinary termination as a redundancy, or vice versa, flows straight through into what''s paid and how it''s taxed. Before processing any termination as a redundancy, confirm with HR or the business that the role itself is ending, not just the person in it. Ask directly: is this position being backfilled by anyone else? If the answer is yes, in almost all cases, this isn''t a genuine redundancy.', 'd358963cf88bd9ffecf712169847e5cec0501ffe756710093411c0b74937fcda', 1770, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-30 09:04:33.38+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:33.505313+00', '2026-07-30 09:04:33.38+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6de6609f-4ad2-4192-84b8-9937b85748a4', 'Drowning in payroll complexities? You''re not alone. Australian payroll professionals are constantly chasing answers to award interpretation, super, leave and termination rules, and ever changing legislation. Meet Beryl! Australia''s smartest AI payroll assistant. Powered by Australian Payroll Association, Beryl blends advanced tech with decades of trusted payroll expertise to give you accurate, jargon free answers, instantly! Whether it''s Fair Work compliance, tax, leave, or award interpretation, Beryl has you covered 24 7. Pulling directly from APA''s extensive knowledge base. Stop wasting hours on research. Get instant, expert support instead. Be the first to experience the future of payroll. Beryl! Payroll expertise, at your fingertips.', '4dd82720b52eacbba54f8377aad4cbc5177206873133bf686018b15c7b89c4af', 747, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-31 07:50:05.843+00', '2026-07-31 07:50:05.895399+00', '2026-07-31 07:50:05.843+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e0f97e3e-6a3e-4509-8148-9aa31f7bd489', 'Welcome, everyone! I''m excited to introduce the Payroll Assurance Scheme, or PAS, developed by the Australian Payroll Association. PAS enables Payroll Operations across Australia to showcase payroll excellence, supporting accuracy through rigorous standards and best practices. This scheme allows payroll operations to demonstrate a gold standard for payroll excellence, showcasing a strong focus on payroll accuracy and integrity. In addition, it showcases a commitment to the payroll profession with a strong focus on continuous improvement. The PAS accreditation process includes: Documentation submission Assessment of payroll processes Evaluation of the payroll team Report highlighting findings PAS certification In summary, the PAS is the gold standard for payroll excellence, enhancing efficiency, compliance, and integrity. ore information about PAS can be found here – website. And if you would like to start the accreditation process you can click here and enter your details. Once entered one of the APA Team will be in touch.', '749a7cfc0875ee16174eeb8fbff43f56eaa60154340e16847fe2f2d16aed5b3a', 1038, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-07-31 07:50:09.474+00', '2026-07-31 07:50:09.529628+00', '2026-07-31 07:50:09.474+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('2e665cf9-771d-46fe-b7eb-7e7c1421f18d', 'The fourth and last obligation is timing. Under Payday Super, contributions have to reach the employee''s fund within seven business days of pay day. That is a considerable change from the old quarterly cut off, and it means the definition of a business day matters. Click start to begin Pay day, also referred to as QE day, is the date that an employer makes a qualifying earnings payment to an employee, which is generally when an employee is paid their salaries and wages. When employees are paid, the employer is required to report those payments to the ATO on or before pay day via an STP lodgement, and the ATO then uses the data reported in the STP file to determine when super payments are due. So your STP reporting is what starts the clock. Now the definition of a business day. A business day is any day other than a Saturday or Sunday, or a day that is a public holiday for the whole of any Australian state or territory. This means if there is a state, territory or nationwide public holiday, that day is not a business day for the purposes of Payday Super. If a public holiday applies to only part of a state or territory, for example Brisbane Show Day which applies only to the Brisbane area and not all of Queensland, that day is still a business day for Payday Super purposes. Superannuation guarantee contributions are to be paid to the employee''s fund within seven business days of pay day. And paid to the employee''s fund means the fund must receive, and be able to allocate, the contributions to the employee''s fund and account within seven business days following QE day or pay day. Receiving the money is not enough on its own. It has to be allocatable. There are three exceptions to the seven business day rule. First, new employees, and returned contributions due to changes to the employee''s fund details such as a fund merger. The extended due date is twenty business days after pay day. Second, out of cycle payments, for example bonus pay runs and adjustment pay runs. The extended due date is seven business days following the next scheduled pay day or QE day. Third, exceptional circumstances affecting multiple employers. If an exceptional circumstance determination covers the employer and the current pay day, contributions must be received by super funds before the later of twenty business days after the current QE day, or twenty business days after the day that the determination is made. Two further notes from the workbook. Where an extended due date overlaps the next due date, and where the extended due date for new employees applies, that extension applies to subsequent due dates that fall within the twenty business days.', '1b5f45c79b3ac369959060aec740983ce36897e6b6c926f00af4f66ddee123ad', 2666, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:25.824+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:25.847795+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:25.824+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('01018d36-0614-4f22-9319-2f6281296820', 'The third obligation is paying the contribution to the employee''s nominated fund. That means offering a choice of fund, acting on the employee''s choice, and knowing what to do when no choice comes back. We will work through the Standard Choice Form section by section, then the stapled super fund process. Click start to begin An employer is generally required to offer employees a choice of super fund and act on the employee''s choice. Employers must keep records to show this has been done. As we saw a moment ago, where an independent contractor is deemed an employee for superannuation guarantee purposes and is therefore entitled to contributions, they are generally eligible to choose their own super fund, and employers must provide a Standard Choice Form within twenty eight days of the contractor''s commencement. The Standard Choice Form has four sections. Section A is the employee''s details and the choice they are exercising. Section B is where the employee chooses their own APRA regulated fund. Section C is where the employee chooses the employer''s default fund. And Section D is where the employee chooses a self managed superannuation fund. We will take each in turn. Section A is the employee''s details. The employee completes their full name, their tax file number, and what choice they are exercising, which is whether they want to choose their own APRA fund, go with the employer''s default fund, or use their own self managed fund. Section B applies where the employee chooses their existing superannuation fund. If the employee has indicated they wish for the employer to send contributions to their existing fund, they must provide the fund name, the ABN or USI, and their member or account number. They must also provide a complying fund letter from their super fund, stating that it is a complying fund and will accept contributions from the employer. That letter is easy to overlook and it is the piece that most often holds up the setup. Section C is where the employee chooses the employer''s default fund. While most employees can choose a super fund, or may have a stapled super fund, employers must have a nominated default fund. The superannuation fund that the employer nominates as their default fund must be a complying fund, that is one that meets specific requirements and obligations under super law. And it must be registered by the Australian Prudential Regulation Authority and offer a MySuper product. MySuper products are cost effective superannuation products with a basic set of features. Employers may need to use multiple default funds to align with different industry awards, agreements and employee requirements. However, employers can only offer one default fund to each employee. The employer must complete this section before giving the employee the form. Section D is where the employee elects a self managed superannuation fund. When nominating a self managed fund, the employee must provide the fund name. The fund ABN. The employee''s full name. Bank account details, including BSB, account number and account name. An electronic service address. And a document confirming the self managed fund is an ATO regulated super fund. A word on the electronic service address. An ESA is a digital address used to deliver SuperStream messages to the self managed super fund, typically through an administrator or messaging provider. SuperStream is the electronic process by which contributions are remitted to superannuation funds. If the electronic service address provided is inactive or incorrect, the employer will receive an error message and will be prevented from making a contribution to the self managed fund. That delays contributions and may expose the employer to penalties for not meeting their obligations. So verify the ESA before the first contribution is due, not after. Where an employee does not return their superannuation choice of fund form, the employer is required to undertake the stapled super process. You log into ATO online services, go to Employees, then Request stapled fund. You enter the employee''s tax file number, or an exemption code, along with their full name and date of birth, and their address if there is no tax file number. You then declare and submit, and you can add more employees using the checkbox. Here is the summary logic. Has the employee chosen their own super fund or your default fund? If yes, the employer pays super into the employee''s chosen fund, or the employer''s own default fund. If no, does the employee have a stapled fund? If yes, the employer pays super into their stapled fund. If no, is the employee covered by an industrial instrument that stipulates a super fund? If yes, the employer pays super into that fund. If no, the employer pays super into their default fund.', '232884ded1c85e5f893a14440af9a24debc8af1f1bbf8b607fbe190b0bf32c22', 4782, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:32.247+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:32.272439+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:32.247+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('48bbbef9-1d28-49e1-85a0-f92007a75ccf', 'Now to qualifying earnings, which is what you apply the twelve per cent to. This is the part of superannuation that changed most under Payday Super, and it is where the errors are. We will look at what qualifying earnings are made up of, what counts as ordinary time earnings and what does not, the change to commissions, salary sacrifice, contractors, and the maximum contributions base. Click start to begin Qualifying earnings are the types of payments made to employees that are used to calculate an employer''s superannuation guarantee liability. Qualifying earnings include four things. Ordinary time earnings, plus all commissions paid to an employee, plus superannuation salary sacrifice amounts that would qualify as qualifying earnings had they not been sacrificed to superannuation, plus earnings paid to workers who fall under the expanded definition of employee, including payments to independent contractors paid mainly for their labour. The legislative instrument that determines which payments are qualifying earnings is Law Companion Ruling LCR 2026 slash D1, Payday Super, Qualifying Earnings. The ATO also publishes a page called What payments are qualifying earnings, which is the practical reference you will use most often. We will take each of these four components in turn. Ordinary time earnings are generally what your employees earn for their ordinary hours of work. Employers must use ordinary time earnings as defined in the superannuation guarantee law to calculate the minimum contributions required for eligible employees. Payments for ordinary hours of work are qualifying earnings, and this includes certain types of paid leave, some allowances, bonuses and lump sum payments. Ordinary time earnings include salary or wage payments. Over award payments. Higher duties payments. Bonuses. Commissions. Allowances, including danger, dirt, site, first aid and all purpose allowances. Shift penalties. And leave payments, being annual leave, personal leave, long service leave and family and domestic violence leave. Notice that shift penalties are in, and paid leave is in. Those two catch people out because they feel like extras rather than ordinary time. Payments excluded from ordinary time earnings include the following. Lump sum payments on termination, except payments in lieu of notice. Payments in lieu of unused annual leave, long service leave or sick leave. Overtime hours. Expense allowances expected to be fully expended. Reimbursements. Payments for unfair dismissal. Workers compensation payments where no work is performed. Parental leave payments. Ancillary leave, for example jury duty and defence force reserves leave. Leave loading that relates to a notional loss of overtime. Benefits subject to fringe benefits tax. Bonuses paid in respect of overtime worked. And dividends. Two of these are worth holding on to. Payment in lieu of notice is the exception within the termination exclusion, so it is qualifying earnings. And leave loading is only excluded where it is demonstrably referable to a lost opportunity to work overtime. Where that is not demonstrable, leave loading is ordinary time earnings. The second component of qualifying earnings is commissions, and this is one of the changes brought in by Payday Super. Prior to the first of July 2026, only commission payments made in respect of an employee''s ordinary hours of work were included. From the first of July 2026, all commission payments, whether in relation to ordinary hours of work or not, are qualifying earnings and subject to superannuation. That includes commission payments earned during a period of overtime. So the general rule that overtime is not qualifying earnings does not carry across to commission earned while working overtime. The commission is still qualifying earnings. The third component is salary sacrifice to superannuation. An effective salary sacrifice arrangement allows employees to sacrifice future earnings in place of a benefit. Examples include salary sacrifice to superannuation, novated leases and laptops. A salary sacrifice arrangement reduces the amount of PAYG withheld on the employee''s salaries and wages. The rule for qualifying earnings is this. Amounts salary sacrificed to superannuation under an effective salary sacrifice arrangement are qualifying earnings. Amounts sacrificed to anything other than superannuation are not. So a salary sacrifice arrangement to superannuation does not affect the employee''s qualifying earnings, while a salary sacrifice arrangement to any other benefit reduces them. Here is the workbook example, which makes the difference concrete. Mary earns one thousand dollars a week and enters into an effective salary sacrifice arrangement of one hundred dollars. In both cases Mary pays tax on nine hundred dollars. In the first case the one hundred dollars goes to superannuation. Mary''s qualifying earnings are made up of nine hundred dollars in salaries and wages plus one hundred dollars in salary sacrifice contributions, so one thousand dollars in total. The employer is liable for twelve per cent of one thousand dollars, which is one hundred and twenty dollars. In the second case the one hundred dollars goes towards a benefit such as a laptop or a novated lease. Mary''s qualifying earnings are made up of nine hundred dollars in salaries and wages only, because the salary sacrifice to a benefit is not qualifying earnings. The employer is liable for twelve per cent of nine hundred dollars, which is one hundred and eight dollars. Same gross, same taxable income, different superannuation liability. The destination of the sacrifice is what decides it. The fourth component of qualifying earnings is earnings paid to workers who fall under the expanded definition of employee. If an employer pays independent contractors mainly for their labour, they are employees for superannuation guarantee purposes. It does not matter if the independent contractor has an Australian business number. Where an independent contractor is deemed an employee for superannuation guarantee purposes and is therefore entitled to contributions, they are generally eligible to choose their own super fund. Employers must provide a Standard Choice Form within twenty eight days of the contractor''s commencement. So the test is what the contractor is being paid for. If it is mainly their labour, superannuation applies, and the choice of fund obligations apply along with it. An employer is only liable for superannuation guarantee contributions up to the maximum contributions base. The maximum contributions base is calculated as the concessional cap divided by the superannuation guarantee rate, rounded to the nearest ten dollars. For the 2026 to 2027 financial year, the concessional cap is thirty two thousand five hundred dollars and the superannuation guarantee rate is twelve per cent. Thirty two thousand five hundred divided by twelve per cent gives two hundred and seventy thousand eight hundred and thirty dollars, rounded to the nearest ten. So the maximum contributions base for the 2026 to 2027 financial year is two hundred and seventy thousand eight hundred and thirty dollars. Above that level of qualifying earnings, the employer has no further superannuation guarantee liability. Because the base is derived from the concessional cap and the rate, it changes whenever either of those changes.', '72a4bd20f26ae1d37ab03181444252c099221f62601395759828c39979a8ff4d', 7393, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:37.301+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:37.326696+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:37.301+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('c2d790fd-a2cb-457a-8d20-91a41bf04adb', 'Welcome to Module 4 of Payroll Essentials. This module covers superannuation. Superannuation is a mandatory retirement savings system. Under it, employers pay a percentage of an employee''s earnings, the superannuation guarantee, into a designated fund to grow and provide income during retirement. Payday Super commenced on the first of July 2026, and it changed both what you calculate super on and when it has to reach the fund. We will work through the employer''s four obligations in order. Calculate the superannuation guarantee rate, apply it to qualifying earnings up to the maximum contributions base, pay it to the employee''s nominated fund, and make sure it is received within seven business days of pay day. There is one practice activity at the end. Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this module. You will know who is eligible for the superannuation guarantee and who is exempt. You will know the current rate and how it has moved. You will be able to identify which payments are qualifying earnings and which are not, including the change to commission payments from the first of July 2026. You will be able to calculate the maximum contributions base and explain why it exists. You will understand the choice of fund process, each section of the Standard Choice Form, and the stapled super fund process where no choice is returned. And you will know when contributions have to reach the fund under Payday Super, and the situations where an extended due date applies. We start with the superannuation guarantee itself. Who has to be paid super, who is exempt, what the rate is, and the four things Payday Super requires an employer to do. Lets get started. Superannuation, commonly known as super, is a mandatory retirement savings system. Under this system, employers are required to pay a percentage of an employee''s earnings, the superannuation guarantee, into a designated fund to grow and provide income during retirement. Employers in Australia must pay the minimum superannuation guarantee contribution based on the current super guarantee percentage of an employee''s qualifying earnings, up to the maximum contributions base, by the relevant cut off date. Generally, all employees are eligible for the superannuation guarantee. It does not matter whether employees are full time, part time or casual. There are two exceptions to that. An employee under eighteen years old, where the employer is only liable for contributions if the employee works more than thirty hours in a week. And an employee paid to do work of a private or domestic nature for a non business employer, where again the employer is only liable if the employee works more than thirty hours in a week. Temporary residents are also generally eligible, with some exceptions. Employers need to set up their business to pay super into eligible employees'' chosen superannuation funds, or their stapled super fund where no choice has been made. Employers are not required to provide superannuation guarantee support to the following employees. Employees under eighteen years of age and not working more than thirty hours per week. Persons paid to do private or domestic work for not more than thirty hours per week for a non business employer. And employees who already have vested benefits in excess of their pension reasonable benefit limit and have elected not to receive any more employer contributions. The under eighteen exemption is the one you will meet most often, and it is assessed week by week. Take a sixteen year old at school who works eight hours a week at a retailer on weekends. You are not paying super for those weeks. If during the school holidays they work thirty five hours in a week, you are paying super for that week. It is the hours in each week that decide it, not the employee''s usual pattern. Here is how the superannuation guarantee rate has moved. In 2020 to 2021 it was nine point five per cent. In 2021 to 2022, ten per cent. In 2022 to 2023, ten point five per cent. In 2023 to 2024, eleven per cent. In 2024 to 2025, eleven and a half per cent. In 2025 to 2026, twelve per cent. And in 2026 to 2027, twelve per cent. The current superannuation guarantee rate, as of the first of July 2026, is twelve per cent. That is the figure to use for every calculation in this module. Payday Super commenced on the first of July 2026. Under the Payday Super legislation, employers are required to do four things. First, calculate the superannuation guarantee rate on. Second, qualifying earnings, up to the maximum contributions base, to the. Third, employee''s nominated fund, within. Fourth, seven days of qualifying earnings day or pay day, and exceptions apply. Read as one sentence, that is the whole obligation. Calculate the rate on qualifying earnings up to the maximum contributions base, pay it to the employee''s nominated fund, within seven business days of pay day. The rest of this module takes each of those four in turn.', '2038af4016bd203011627989a0708191f2d8c87eee170e653c2b1df497c4928b', 4957, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:40.76+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:40.772308+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:40.76+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('4bb71798-a81c-4119-8495-95535793857c', 'The last part of this module covers record keeping and pay slips. These obligations sit under the Fair Work Act rather than with the ATO, and they are the first thing a Fair Work Inspector asks to see. Click the start button to begin Employers must issue pay slips to each employee within one working day of their pay day, even when the employee is on leave. The pay slip may be issued in electronic or hard copy. Information which must be on the pay slip includes the name of the employer. The Australian Business Number, if any, of the employer. The employee''s name. The date of payment. The pay period. The gross and net amount of pay. Any loadings, monetary allowances, bonuses, incentive based payments, penalty rates or other entitlements paid that can be singled out. If the employee is paid an hourly rate, the ordinary hourly pay rate, the number of hours worked at that rate, and the amount of pay at that rate. If the employee is paid an annual rate, that is a salary, the rate as at the last day in the pay period. And any deductions made from the employee''s pay, including the amount and details of each deduction. If you are required to pay superannuation contributions for your employee''s benefit, you should also include the amount of each superannuation contribution made during the period to which the pay slip relates, or the amounts of contributions that you are liable to make, and the name, or the name and number, of the superannuation fund you put or will put those contributions into. If employers issue electronic pay slips, they must list the same information as hard copy pay slips, and they must be in a format which is easily printed and distributed confidentially. Now record keeping. Employee records must be in a form that is readily accessible to a Fair Work Inspector. They must be in a legible form and in English, preferably in plain, simple English. They must be kept for seven years. They must not be altered unless for the purposes of correcting an error. And they must not be false or misleading to the employer''s knowledge. Note that the seven years runs from the date the record was created, not seven years after the employee''s termination. Employee records which must be kept include general records such as the employee''s name, commencement date and basis of employment. Pay records, including the rate of pay, the gross and net amounts and any deductions, and details of any loadings, penalty rates, bonuses or other monetary allowance or separately identifiable entitlement paid. Hours of work records. Leave records, including balances, leave taken and agreements to cash out leave. Superannuation contribution records. Individual flexibility arrangement records. And termination records, including the reason for termination, notice provided, and the name of the person who terminated the employment.', 'acf7561bfd7efb3fb9fe2ea6a40dcb28624ddb5171aebe59feea2be1214df25d', 2850, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:45.427+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:45.438035+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:45.427+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('3d634ea8-f2e3-49fe-b405-e4955350f183', 'Next, deductions and the recovery of overpayments. Deductions are tightly controlled by the Fair Work Act, and overpayments have their own reporting rules that depend entirely on which financial year the overpayment relates to. Getting that wrong creates a problem with the ATO as well as with the employee. Click start to begin Generally speaking, an employer is allowed to make a deduction from an employee''s pay if one of the following applies. The employee agreed in writing and the deduction is principally for the employee''s benefit. Or it is allowed by a law, a court order, or by the Fair Work Commission. Or it is allowed under the employee''s award. Or it is allowed under the employee''s registered agreement and the employee agrees to it. Examples of permitted deductions include salary sacrifice arrangements and voluntary contributions into an employee''s super fund. Note the difference between awards and agreements here. Some awards have a clause that allows an employer to deduct money from an employee''s pay without the employee''s agreement. If a registered agreement allows the deduction, the employee must still agree to it. A common example of an award deduction is where an employee has been allowed to go into a negative annual leave balance, and on termination is still negative. That amount can be deducted from the final pay where the award allows it. An employer cannot deduct money if it benefits the employer directly or indirectly and is unreasonable in the circumstances. Or if the employee is under eighteen years of age and their parent or guardian has not agreed in writing. That second one applies even if the deduction is made in accordance with an award, registered agreement or contract. There are, however, some reasonable deductions that benefit employers. Notice of termination not given, as per award terms, and note that this can only be deducted from wages owed under the award, not from unused leave payments on termination. Business goods and services deductions. And private use of property deductions, for example personal items bought by an employee using a work credit card. From the thirtieth of December 2023, employees are able to authorise recurring salary deductions by their employer for amounts that vary from time to time. For example, employees may wish to establish payroll deductions for union fees or other expenses that may vary. An employee can make a single written authorisation for the deduction, which can be withdrawn by the employee in writing at any time. Before this change, a written authority had to specify a dollar amount, which meant a new authority was needed every time the amount changed, for example when union fees increased each year. Now one authorisation covers the deduction even as the amount varies. These changes do not weaken the rules that govern employer initiated wage deductions. The Protected Earnings Amount is the part of an employee''s or contractor''s pay that is exempt from child support deductions. This amount is indexed on the first of January each year. The following amounts apply to pay dates from the first of January 2026 to the thirty first of December 2026. Weekly, five hundred and forty four dollars eighty eight. Daily, five hundred and forty four dollars eighty eight divided by seven days, which is seventy seven dollars eighty four. Fortnightly, five hundred and forty four dollars eighty eight times two, which is one thousand and eighty nine dollars seventy six. Four weekly, five hundred and forty four dollars eighty eight times four, which is two thousand one hundred and seventy nine dollars fifty two. And monthly, seventy seven dollars eighty four times thirty point four three seven five days per month, which is two thousand three hundred and sixty nine dollars twenty six. Because this is indexed every January, check the current figure rather than carrying last year''s forward. Overpayments can happen when an employer mistakenly believes an employee is entitled to the pay, or because of a payroll error. Employers cannot take money out of an employee''s pay to fix up a mistake or overpayment. Instead, the employer and employee should discuss and agree on a repayment arrangement. If the employee agrees to repay the money, a written agreement has to be made setting out the reason for the overpayment, the amount of money overpaid, and the way repayments will be made, for example cash, cheque or electronic transfer, and how often. That has to be reasonable. If the repayment cannot be agreed, the employer should get legal advice. Where an overpayment is identified in the same financial year as it is paid, the employee will only need to repay the net amount, which is the amount actually received by the payee. Repayments should come from the employee''s after tax income. Details of the overpaid amount should not be included on the employee''s income statement or reported through Single Touch Payroll. The overpaid amount may be repaid in the same financial year or a subsequent financial year. Here is the workbook example. An employee normally receives wages of three thousand two hundred dollars per month, but you incorrectly paid them four thousand two hundred dollars in March 2027, an overpayment of one thousand dollars. Three hundred dollars was withheld in tax from the overpayment, with the employee receiving the remaining seven hundred dollars. They agree to repay in two instalments of three hundred and fifty dollars, in June and September 2027. The employee is only required to repay seven hundred dollars, and the income statements do not reflect the overpaid or repaid amounts. You need to reverse the overpayment amount, both gross and tax, in the 2026 to 27 financial year, before you finalise, even if the employee has not yet repaid the full amount. Where an overpayment occurs in one financial year and the overpaid amounts are discovered in a later financial year, an employer may not recover the PAYG amount withheld from the payment from the ATO. If the employee is required to repay the overpaid amount, they will need to repay the gross amount. The gross amount is the overpaid amount actually received by the payee plus any tax amount withheld, and the employee repays that gross amount from after tax income. Here is the workbook example. An employee normally receives three thousand two hundred dollars per month, but was incorrectly paid four thousand two hundred dollars in March 2026, an overpayment of one thousand dollars. The overpayment was not identified until August 2026. The employee is required to repay the gross amount of one thousand dollars and agrees to repay in September 2026. You are required to un finalise the employee for the 2025 to 26 financial year and adjust the gross by the amount of the overpayment, one thousand dollars. Do not adjust the tax. You then re finalise the employee for that financial year. On reporting, if the overpayment is discovered in a later year you should submit an update event through STP to advise of the amounts the employee should have received in the relevant financial year. Do not adjust the amount of tax withheld. Provide an update event for each financial year an overpayment occurred in. If the overpayment relates to a payment you did not report through STP, provide the employee with an amended payment summary and submit an amended payment summary annual report to the ATO.', '9bc36174a7b7c5d9eac77d44df8122cedc20883b11dee23b57af3d6f93464ea1', 7404, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:51.509+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:51.538205+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:51.509+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('0ca97ce0-8aac-489d-82b7-1ed65f755529', 'Now we move from lump sums paid during employment to lump sums paid on termination, specifically unused leave. A normal termination covers resignation, normal retirement, dismissal, the end of a fixed term contract and abandonment of employment. There is no special treatment for retirement, it is simply a resignation where the employee does not intend to work afterwards. Click Start to begin On a normal termination, what are we paying? Unused annual leave. All unused annual leave will always be paid, including purchased and salary sacrifice leave. Leave loading, which is paid out on termination where the employee receives loading when they take leave. And unused long service leave. The rules for long service leave vary from state to state, and individual awards, agreements, contracts and company policy may have different provisions. Generally, everyone has access to long service leave after ten years of service, and no one has access before five years of service. The tax table for calculating the withholding on these payments is Schedule 7, the tax table for unused leave payments on termination of employment. As per Schedule 7 there are differing rates of withholding, depending on the reason for termination. For a standard termination relating to a resignation, normal retirement or dismissal, the withholding rate to use in most circumstances is marginal rates. Marginal rates are a notional calculation. That is, the extra tax the person would pay on top of their normal pay if they received their normal pay every pay period for a full year, and they got a portion of this termination amount each pay for a whole year instead of being paid in a lump sum. Here are the seven steps. Step one, using the relevant PAYG withholding tax table, work out the amount to withhold from the payee''s normal gross earnings for a regular pay period. Step two, divide the amount of the payments by the number of normal pay periods in twelve months, that is twelve monthly, twenty six fortnightly or fifty two weekly. Step three, disregard any cents. Step four, add the amount at step three to the normal gross earnings for a single pay period. Step five, use the same PAYG withholding tax tables used at step one to work out the amount to withhold from the amount at step four. Step six, subtract the amount at step one from the amount at step five. Step seven, multiply the amount obtained at step six by the number of normal pay periods in twelve months. One important note. Do not withhold any amount for study and training support loan debts on these payments. Your payroll system should perform this calculation automatically, but it is worth knowing how to do it manually, because systems occasionally get it wrong and you should be able to confirm the figure when an employee questions it.', '75c8acf8f0de3a52a012ad3b593f0eb071171a8d28fdd60cd8d43d97080af058', 2803, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:18:55.979+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:55.990304+00', '2026-09-09 23:18:55.979+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6d7202b6-9da7-45ab-b3b9-6c8f3e63e21b', 'Calculating the tax on a back payment is only half the job. The other half is reporting it correctly, and how you report it depends on when the payment accrued. We will work through the three cases and then look at Lump Sum E. Click start to begin How a back payment is reported depends on when it accrued. For payments accrued in the current financial year, include the total gross amount of all payments at gross payments or allowances, and the amount withheld at total tax withheld. Under STP Phase 2, rather than a single gross bucket, you report the payment type that matches the payment. If it is a back payment of overtime you report it as overtime. If it is a back payment of leave you report it as leave. If it covers several things you split it across the relevant buckets. For salary and wage income, including allowances, accrued before the current financial year but not more than twelve months before the date of payment, the treatment is the same. Include the gross amount of the payment at gross payments or allowances, or under Phase 2 the matching payment type, and the amount withheld at total tax withheld. For each payment accrued more than twelve months before the date of payment, include the gross amount of the payment, including allowances, at Lump Sum E, and the amount withheld at total tax withheld. And for each payment that accrued both before and after that twelve month point, apportion the payment between the two periods and apply the rules above for each portion. There are also other amounts accrued before the current financial year, described in the schedule as other payments you should use this schedule for. For each of those accrued in a prior financial year, include the gross amount at Lump Sum E and the amount withheld at total tax withheld. A word on Lump Sum E. Under STP Phase 1, if you report an amount at Lump Sum E you must also issue the employee with a letter specifying the financial years over which the amount was accrued, as well as the gross amount of the payment that accrued in each financial year. Under STP Phase 2, there is no requirement for a Lump Sum E letter. The information is completed as part of STP reporting, and it appears on the employee''s income statement. Here is the workbook example. Brett''s employer identifies on the seventh of October 2025 that Brett has not claimed some overtime he worked in the prior financial year, the first of July 2023 to the thirtieth of June 2024, due to a technical issue. The payment accrued more than twelve months before the date the employer was going to pay the back pay, so the payment must be reported as Lump Sum E. Under STP Phase 2 the amounts are reported as 2023 Lump Sum E, ten hours of overtime, five hundred and twenty nine dollars seventy six, and 2024 Lump Sum E, eight hours of overtime, five hundred and thirteen dollars zero four. Brett''s employer does not need to provide him with a letter, because they have included the amount for each relevant financial year in their STP reporting.', 'b382f73915e63ee1d9e2296e250b32fb975b01200141505a70433bf93c6befaf', 3017, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:00.727+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:00.753618+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:00.727+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('a7b178ea-e49a-4afc-ba46-a4a788600017', 'Now to irregular payments, which is where the calculations start. Back payments, commissions and bonuses are not taxed the same way as a normal pay, because taxing a lump sum in a single pay period would over tax the employee. We will look at what counts as an irregular payment, the methods available, the definitions you need, and then work through both methods step by step. Click start to begin Irregular payments include back payments, including lump sum payments in arrears, bonuses and commissions, and other similar lump sum payments. The tax table applicable to these payments is Schedule 5, the tax table for back payments, commissions, bonuses and similar payments. This tax table does not apply to working holiday makers. There is one important exclusion to understand. If you normally process payments in a pay period later than when the work is performed, for example overtime paid with a time lag of one pay period, those are not considered back payments. They are treated as part of the normal pay cycle when paid, and withholding is calculated on total earnings for that period. An overtime payment is only considered a back payment if it was meant to have been made in a prior pay period. There is no exhaustive list of what a similar payment is, but in practice it covers a lump sum paid during employment that is not a normal part of the pay. That includes a cash out of annual leave, a cash out of long service leave in the states that permit it, a cash out of rostered days off, and a cash out of time in lieu. What it does not include is any lump sum paid on termination, which is dealt with separately. To work out the amount to withhold from an additional payment you can use one of three methods. Method A calculates withholding by apportioning additional payments made in the current pay period over the number of pay periods in a financial year, and applying that average amount to the gross earnings in the current pay period. These additional payments can be current or prior financial year payments. Method B one is used for any back payments applied to specific periods in the current financial year. Method B two uses complex calculations and is best suited to employers who use computerised payroll systems, such as date driven payroll software. It calculates withholding by averaging all additional payments made in the current financial year over the number of pay periods in a financial year, and applying that to the average total earnings to date. It is up to the employer which method to use, and they will give different results. The ATO accepts either. In practice most payroll systems are configured to use Method B, and Method A is the one used for manual calculations because you can follow the steps in a spreadsheet. There are limits. Method A is all purpose, so you can use it for any of these lump sum payments, for back payments relating to any financial year, and for bonuses, commissions and leave cash outs. Method B one is limited to back payments relating to specific periods in the current financial year. Method B two can be used for back payments relating to a prior financial year, and for other additional payments such as commissions and bonuses. Three definitions to hold on to before we work through the steps. Additional payments include back payments, including lump sum payments in arrears, commissions, bonuses and similar payments. That is the lump sum you are making. Normal earnings are gross taxable earnings, and include all salary and wage income, taxable allowances, and overtime earnings for the current financial year. This includes any back payments previously made using Method B one. At the start of a financial year, a payee''s normal earnings can be based on the last full pay period worked in the previous financial year. If a payee''s pay fluctuates significantly, you can use an average of gross taxable earnings for the current financial year, or if applicable the previous financial year. If a payee has no current or past normal earnings, for example because they are newly employed, you can include expected future earnings, based on their contracted or expected salary for the financial year. For the purposes of lump sum payments, normal earnings do not include employment termination payments or unused leave payments made on termination of employment. Average total earnings are the sum of all normal earnings paid in the current financial year, including the current pay, plus any current year back payments if Method B one is used, divided by the number of pay periods to date including the current pay period. Here are the ten steps for Method A, which applies to a current or prior financial year payment. Step one, work out your payee''s gross earnings excluding any additional payments for the current pay period, and ignore any cents. Step two, use the relevant tax table to find the amount to be withheld from those gross earnings. Step three, add any additional payments to be made in the current pay period together and divide the total by the number of pay periods in the financial year, that is fifty two weekly, twenty six fortnightly or twelve monthly. Ignore any cents. Step four, add the amount at step three to the gross earnings at step one. Step five, use the relevant tax table to find the amount to be withheld from the amount at step four. Step six, subtract the amount at step two from the amount at step five. Step seven, multiply the amount at step six by the number of pay periods used in step three. Step eight, multiply the additional payment being made in the current pay period by forty seven per cent. Step nine, use the lesser amount of step seven and step eight for the withholding on the additional payment, ignoring any cents. Step ten, work out the total PAYG withholding for the current pay period by adding the withholding on the additional payment at step nine to the withholding on the gross earnings at step two. One variation worth knowing. If you are paying a commission, bonus or similar payment for a defined period of less than twelve months, you can choose to use the number of pay periods the payment relates to at step three. For example, if a commission relates to four weeks and the employee is paid weekly, you divide by four pay periods rather than fifty two. Here are the seven steps for Method B one, which applies to back payments applied to specific periods in the current financial year. Step one, work out how much of the back payment applied to each earlier pay period in the current financial year. Step two, for the first affected pay period, add the back payment relevant to that period to the normal earnings previously paid, to get total earnings for that period. Step three, use the relevant tax table to find the amount to be withheld from the total earnings for that period. Step four, subtract the amount previously withheld for the period from the amount at step three. Step five, repeat steps two to four for each pay period affected, and total the amounts calculated in step four for each pay period, for the withholding on the back payment. Step six, use the relevant tax table to find the amount to be withheld from your payee''s gross earnings, excluding additional payments, for the current pay period. Step seven, work out the total PAYG withholding for the current pay period by adding the withholding on the back payment at step five to the withholding on the gross earnings at step six. So Method A averages the lump sum across the year, while Method B one goes back and recalculates what should have been withheld at the time.', '549eed1ae6b5c29a1b02e3810112d2a8bf8df667223f0dcc066546ab6e7348e6', 7595, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:06.295+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:06.320359+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:06.295+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('9d673445-64dd-4fe0-918c-c85dc508fe6e', 'Now allowances. Allowances generate more payroll questions than almost anything else, because the treatment does not follow the name of the allowance. It follows the purpose. We will look at what allowances are, how they differ from reimbursements, and then work through the ATO''s withholding tables one by one. Click start to begin. Allowances are separately identified payments made to an employee. Typically these fall into three groups in the workbook. First, payments to compensate employees based on working conditions. This includes working in harsh or remote environments. Examples include danger allowance, site allowance, dirt allowance and height allowance. Second, payments made based on skills, qualifications, or for extended hours of work or special duties. Examples include first aid allowance, safety officer allowance, shift allowance and call out allowance. Third, payments made to employees expected to incur expenses while carrying out their duties. Examples include tool allowance, vehicle allowance, uniform allowance and laundry allowance. These are subject to PAYG, but are reported as separately identifiable allowances in STP with the applicable allowance code. In practice it helps to split that third group in two. Expense allowances where the employee can claim a tax deduction, such as tools or a compulsory uniform, and expense allowances where they cannot, such as a flat rate car allowance for travel that is not work related. The most important point on this slide is this. It is not the name of an allowance that determines its treatment. It is what the allowance is for. It is worth being precise about the difference between an allowance and a reimbursement. Allowances are amounts paid to cover anticipated costs, or as compensation for conditions of employment, and are paid regardless of whether the employee incurs an expense. Allowances are assessable income to the employee and are generally included as income in their tax return, and the employee may be entitled to claim a deduction. Reimbursements, by contrast, are exact compensation for actual expenses already incurred. That distinction is the reason employees sometimes arrive at payroll saying an allowance is not taxable because their accountant told them they can claim a deduction for it. Both things are true at once. From an employment perspective the allowance is taxable, and separately, the employee may claim a deduction when they lodge their tax return. Table 1A in your workbook lists the types of allowance an employee might receive. The categories are working conditions, qualifications or special duties, which covers crib, danger, dirt, height, site, shift or travelling time allowances and trade, first aid certificate or safety officer allowances. Non deductible expenses, which covers part day travel with no overnight absence from the employee''s ordinary place of residence, meals other than award overtime meal allowance or overnight travel allowance, and motor vehicle allowances for non deductible travel such as home to work, including cents per kilometre payments. Deductible expenses, which covers tools, compulsory uniform or dry cleaning, motor vehicle for work related travel including cents per kilometre payments in excess of the ATO rate, and overseas accommodation for deductible travel. And on call allowances, both within and outside ordinary hours. The critical note sits underneath the table. You are required to withhold from all allowances listed in Table 1A. Every one of them. That includes the deductible expense allowances, which is where most of the questions come from. Table 2A covers cents per kilometre car expense payments using the approved rate. There are three rows. Payments made by applying the approved rate, or a lower rate, to the number of kilometres travelled up to five thousand business kilometres. You are not required to withhold, and it is reportable. Payments made by applying the approved rate, or a lower rate, to kilometres travelled in excess of five thousand business kilometres. You are required to withhold from payments for the excess over five thousand kilometres, and it is reportable. And payments made at a rate above the approved rate for distances travelled up to five thousand business kilometres. You are required to withhold from the amount which relates to the excess over the approved rate, and it is reportable. To put that in practice. The ATO does not care what rate you pay your employees. That rate comes from the award, the agreement, or the contract. What the ATO cares about is how you tax it. Up to the approved rate, no withholding. Above the approved rate, the excess is taxable. And past five thousand kilometres, payments for the excess kilometres are taxable. That five thousand kilometre limit applies per car, not per employee in total. Table 3A covers award transport payments. Award transport payments that are deductible transport expenses. No withholding required, and reportable. Award transport payments that are non deductible transport expenses. Withhold from the total payment, and reportable. Award transport payments are rare. They only apply where the payment can be traced back to an industrial instrument that was in force under Australian law on the twenty ninth of October 1986. Table 4A covers laundry allowances, not dry cleaning, for deductible clothing. Up to the threshold amount, no withholding, and reportable. Over the threshold amount, withhold from the excess over the threshold amount, and reportable. Dry cleaning allowances should have an amount withheld in accordance with Table 1A. Table 5A covers award overtime meal allowances. Up to the reasonable allowances amount, no withholding, and not reportable. Over the reasonable allowances amount, and the allowance must be paid under an industrial instrument in connection with overtime worked, withhold from the excess over the reasonable allowances amount, and report the total allowance. Table 6A covers domestic or overseas travel allowances involving an overnight absence from the employee''s ordinary place of residence. Up to the reasonable allowances amount, no withholding, and not reportable. Over the reasonable allowances amount, withhold from the excess over the reasonable allowances amount, and report the total allowance. Overseas accommodation, withhold, and report the total allowance. Notice the pattern in these two tables. Where the allowance is within the reasonable amount, it is neither withheld from nor reported. Once it exceeds the reasonable amount, you withhold from the excess but you report the whole allowance, not just the excess. Here are the numbers that sit behind those tables. The approved rate is ninety one cents per kilometre for the year commencing the first of July 2026. An award transport payment is a transport payment covering particular travel that was paid under an industrial instrument, that is an award, order, determination or industrial agreement, that was in force under Australian law on the twenty ninth of October 1986. The current threshold amount for laundry expenses is one hundred and fifty dollars, and dry cleaning allowances should have an amount withheld in accordance with Table 1A. Each year the ATO publishes a determination setting out the reasonable amounts for overtime meal allowance expenses, domestic travel allowance expenses, travel allowance expenses for employee truck drivers, and overseas travel allowance expenses. For the 2026 to 2027 income year, those amounts are in Taxation Determination TD 2026 slash 4. These figures change, most of them on the first of July, so check the current determination rather than relying on the number you used last year.', '399edd268a18308854981a8c9bb793a1c985b955f778fc12779cbdec8fe40127', 7717, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:11.22+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:11.242853+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:11.22+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bbdb26d6-e4b0-4f99-b0ad-7a35b0e9a60f', 'Next, Single Touch Payroll. STP changed payroll reporting completely when it arrived, and it is now simply part of the process that happens each time you run a pay. We will look at what it is, what you must report and when, how superannuation fits in, and what happens with out of cycle payments. Click the start button to begin. Single Touch Payroll is an Australian Government initiative to reduce employers'' reporting burdens to government agencies. With STP, you report employees'' payroll information to the ATO each time you pay them, through STP enabled software. Payroll information includes salaries and wages, PAYG withholding, and superannuation. STP started on the first of July 2018 for employers with twenty or more employees, and the first of July 2019 for employers with nineteen or fewer employees, and it is a mandatory obligation. For anyone who worked before 2018, this replaced the end of year payment summary process entirely. Instead of reporting a whole year at the end of the year, you now report every pay. One of the key purposes of STP was superannuation. The ATO data matches what the employer reports as super paid against what the funds report as super received, and follows up employers where those figures do not line up. Now what you must report. You report payments from your regular pay cycle, which is the pay event, and out of cycle pays. You are required to report a pay event to the ATO on or before the pay day. The pay day is either the payment date stipulated in the electronic transaction to your financial institution or, if you did not stipulate a date, the date you intend to make the payment into the employee''s bank account. If you include out of cycle payments in your regular pay event, report the date of your regular pay day. The report must include, at a minimum, each employee with an amount subject to withholding in that regular pay cycle, and it may include information for other employees. You must report the year to date values of gross salary or wages, allowances or other payments, deductions and PAYG withholding for each employee included in that pay event. Those year to date amounts may be less than a previous report, for example on recovery of a current year overpayment, but they cannot be negative. A pay event must contain at least one employee record, and can only include one record per employee per payroll identifier. If you have established two payroll records for an individual, you can report both within the same pay event using unique payroll identifiers, reporting separate year to date amounts for each. Where an employee is paid more than once on a particular day, you may provide a single report for that employee with the latest year to date figures. You may lodge multiple pay event files for the same day, and the time stamp identifies the latest record for each employee. STP also carries the superannuation and identity information. You must report year to date employer superannuation liability or ordinary time earnings amounts for each employee in that pay event. If you pay above the minimum super guarantee liability, report the higher amount if you cannot separately identify these in your payroll solution. If your year to date employer superannuation liability is zero, report zero. If your employee is a member of a defined benefit fund and you make contributions for them, report that amount, otherwise report zero as the super liability amount. This would usually correspond to the year to date amount shown on the employee''s pay slip. Where you cannot report superannuation liability you must report the year to date ordinary time earnings amount, and you can report both if your payroll solution allows. On identity, all payees must have either a tax file number or an Australian business number reported. Where you report a payment and withholding to a contractor under a voluntary agreement, you must provide the contractor''s ABN, and the contractor''s TFN is not required. Reporting those payments through STP is voluntary. Where a TFN has not been provided you must use the TFN exemption codes. And a payee may be both a contractor and an employee with the same payroll identifier within the same financial year, in which case both the TFN and the ABN must be reported if payments are made under both a voluntary agreement and any other gross payment. You may make payments other than as part of the employee''s regular pay cycle, such as commissions, bonuses, payments in advance or back payments. These may be reported in one of two ways. You can submit a pay event on or before the pay day you made the payment. Or you can include the out of cycle payment in the next regular pay event you lodge, as long as that is in the same financial year. If the next regular pay cycle falls in the following financial year, you must report the payment by the thirtieth of June in the year the payment was made. So the one time of year you cannot roll an off cycle payment into the next pay event is at the end of June. Related to that, at the end of each financial year you must finalise your STP. You submit a finalisation declaration to tell the ATO those are the final figures for the year, and that needs to be done by the fourteenth of July. That is what makes the employee''s income statement final in myGov so they can lodge their tax return.', 'f56aa2175d5dd2975b26e57bdad9c1c5faeae0561b6cf931c55f9f123e6d36c5', 5344, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:15.357+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:15.370257+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:15.357+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('3b5d511a-d47c-466b-abc0-3463aa5e0a13', 'Now to the tax tables themselves and to PAYG withholding generally. There are a lot of tax tables, and choosing the right one is most of the work. We will look at which tables exist, when each applies, and what kinds of payment PAYG is withheld from. Then you will have a practice activity calculating gross, tax and net. Click start to begin. Tax tables are used to help work out how much PAYG to withhold from payments to employees or other payees, based on the frequency of payment and individual circumstances. The most commonly used tables are the weekly table, NAT one zero zero five, the fortnightly table, NAT one zero zero six, and the monthly table, NAT one zero zero seven. Other tables available include withholding for workers who have answered yes to having a HELP, SSL, VSL, FS or TSL debt, the table for individuals employed in the horticultural or shearing industry, NAT one zero one three, and the table for actors, variety artists and other entertainers, NAT one zero two three. Other commonly used tables are NAT three three four eight, the tax table for back payments, commissions, bonuses and similar payments, and NAT three three five one, the tax table for unused leave payments on termination of employment. NAT seven zero nine eight zero provides instructions on how to calculate employment termination payments. NAT codes are simply the descriptions used to identify and order forms on the ATO website. The appropriate table depends on the pay frequency, unless the employee has a study or training support loan, in which case use the STSL tables, or has provided a Medicare levy variation declaration, in which case use the Medicare levy adjustment tables. You also need to consider whether the employee is paying the Medicare levy and whether they have claimed the tax free threshold for your employment. A full list of tables, and an online tax withheld calculator, are on the ATO website. One practical note. Some employers pay bimonthly, that is twice a month, which gives twenty four pays a year rather than twenty six. There is no bimonthly tax table, so you take the monthly table and divide by two. PAYG stands for pay as you go. It is tax withheld as the employee earns, rather than at the end of the year. Here are the types of payment from which PAYG is withheld. Salaries and wages. Allowances, with some exceptions we will work through shortly. Back payments, commissions, bonuses and similar payments. Unused leave paid on termination. And employment termination payments. All of these are subject to withholding, and each has a table that tells you how much.', 'e9c1789800f54aa8ed94898b9168907d85d30e3d24fc769e9d3743bb03124c9e', 2601, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:19.084+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:19.096911+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:19.084+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e33ffa84-7787-4a45-89f8-ecbf3a0430da', 'The eleventh National Employment Standard covers notice of termination and redundancy pay. This is the standard that most often gets checked by an auditor after the fact, because it is the one where a mistake turns into an underpayment on the final pay. We will work through notice periods, payment in lieu of notice, redundancy pay, and final pay, and then you have the last practice activity. The written material starts on page 36 of your workbook. A notice period is the amount of notice an employer must give an employee if they plan to terminate the employee''s employment. Under the NES, an employer must provide at least the following notice. For not more than one year of continuous service, one week. For more than one year but not more than three years, two weeks. For more than three years but not more than five years, three weeks. And for more than five years, four weeks. On top of that, the relevant notice period increases by one week if the employee is over 45 years old and has completed at least two years of continuous service with the employer. Both of those conditions have to be met for the extra week. So the maximum notice under the NES is five weeks, being four weeks for more than five years of service, plus the additional week for an employee over 45 with at least two years of service. Remember also that an award, agreement or contract may provide more than the NES, and where it does, the more generous entitlement applies. An employer can pay an employee instead of giving notice. This is usually called payment in lieu of notice. The amount paid to the employee must equal or exceed the total amount at full rate of pay the employee would get had they worked until the end of the notice period. Note the words full rate of pay. This is not the base rate. It is what the employee would actually have received had they worked out the notice, which can include incentive payments, loadings, allowances, overtime and penalty rates and other separately identifiable amounts, depending on the employee''s arrangements. That is a common source of underpayments, because it is easy to default to the base rate when calculating payment in lieu. An employee is entitled to redundancy pay from the employer if the employee is terminated at the employer''s initiative because they no longer require the job to be done by the employee or anyone else, or because of the insolvency or bankruptcy of the employer. The amount of redundancy pay equals the total amount payable to the employee for the redundancy period, calculated at their base rate of pay. Note that redundancy pay is at base rate, whereas payment in lieu of notice is at full rate of pay. Those two are different and it is worth keeping them straight. Here are the redundancy pay periods. At least one year but less than two years, four weeks. At least two but less than three years, six weeks. At least three but less than four years, seven weeks. At least four but less than five years, eight weeks. At least five but less than six years, 10 weeks. At least six but less than seven years, 11 weeks. At least seven but less than eight years, 13 weeks. At least eight but less than nine years, 14 weeks. At least nine but less than 10 years, 16 weeks. And at least 10 years, 12 weeks. That last figure surprises people every time. Redundancy pay peaks at 16 weeks between nine and 10 years of service, then drops back to 12 weeks at 10 years. It is not a typographical error. It reflects the fact that employees with 10 years of service become entitled to long service leave. Final pay is what the employer owes and must pay an employee when the employer ends their employment. The final pay includes outstanding wages, including penalty rates and allowances. Accrued annual leave entitlements. Accrued or pro rated long service leave, if applicable. And redundancy pay entitlements, if applicable. It is best practice to make the final wage payment no later than seven days after the employee''s final day of employment. Most modern awards were amended to include a clause about payment of final pay, and those clauses state that the employer must pay an employee no later than seven days after the day on which the employee''s employment terminates. Be sure to check your applicable award to determine whether that applies. One further point that comes from the redundancy rules rather than the final pay rules. Casual employees have no entitlement to notice of termination or to redundancy pay, and small business employers, meaning those with fewer than 15 employees, are not required to pay redundancy pay under the NES.', '70c2fdd3970780713d1d7d9e83d33ec7842f0aecb4b81fc3457ff62e568ef2a8', 4606, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:31.318+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:31.327474+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:31.318+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('053635cf-c238-4d8e-bfb2-aae9ae2328d4', 'The tenth National Employment Standard is superannuation. This is the newest of the twelve. Superannuation only became an entitlement under the NES from the first of January 2024. The obligation to pay super is not new, of course. What is new is that it now sits inside the NES as well as in the superannuation guarantee laws. click start to begin. Employers have an obligation to pay superannuation contributions for eligible employees under superannuation guarantee laws, and from the first of January 2024 superannuation is an entitlement under the National Employment Standards as well. What difference does that make in practice? It means most employees covered by the NES can take court action under the Fair Work Act to recover unpaid super, unless the Australian Taxation Office has already commenced proceedings in relation to that super. The NES entitlement to super aligns with super laws, so if an employer complies with the superannuation guarantee they will also meet their obligations under the NES. It is worth knowing which agency does what. The Australian Taxation Office gives advice and information about tax and superannuation requirements, and it is the primary enforcement agency for the compulsory super guarantee. The Fair Work Ombudsman gives employers and employees information and advice on workplace rights and obligations, including information on the entitlement to super in the NES and extra terms about super in awards. There is one exception to be aware of. The new NES entitlement does not apply to some employees. Employees who are in the national workplace relations system because their state referred its powers to make workplace laws to the Commonwealth do not have an entitlement to super under the NES. That broadly includes employees in New South Wales, South Australia, Queensland, Tasmania and Victoria who are employed by sole traders, partnerships, other unincorporated entities or non trading corporations. It also includes employees in Victoria employed in the public sector, and employees in Tasmania employed in local government. Those employees may still be entitled to super under the super guarantee, and under super terms in an award or registered agreement. Finally, some awards, enterprise agreements and other registered agreements have extra terms about super, so check the instrument.', '01ad73d71bf3f8de51d3892cec3e2981436523f2d45540c5a2cceb634f80298d', 2344, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:36.231+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:36.257823+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:36.231+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('704399de-349c-4f13-8218-8b357aea2902', 'The ninth National Employment Standard is public holidays. This standard sets out which days are public holidays, who has to be paid for them, and what happens when a public holiday collides with another form of leave. That last part is where most of the questions come from, so we will spend a slide on it. Click start to begin. The following days are public holidays under the National Employment Standards. The first of January, New Year''s Day. The twenty sixth of January, Australia Day. Good Friday. Easter Monday. The twenty fifth of April, Anzac Day. The King''s birthday holiday, on the day on which it is celebrated in a state or territory. The twenty fifth of December, Christmas Day. And the twenty sixth of December, Boxing Day. In addition, public holidays can include any other days or part days stated under state or territory law. For example, regional public holidays that apply only in a specific area, and state public holidays such as Labour Day and Melbourne Cup Day in Victoria. So the NES list is a floor, not a complete list. You always need the state and territory list as well, and for some employees the regional list on top of that. Full time and part time employees are entitled to have paid time off from work on public holidays. If a business is closed on a public holiday, or if a full time or part time employee takes the day off, the employer must pay them at their base rate for the ordinary hours they would have otherwise worked. Employees who do not normally work on the day the public holiday falls do not receive payment. Here is the workbook example. Fred is a full time courier employed for 7.6 hours per day Monday to Friday, and he receives an hourly wage of 25 dollars. The business is closed for the Anzac Day holiday. Even though Fred is not working on Anzac Day, the employer is required to pay Fred''s base rate of pay of 25 dollars per hour for whatever ordinary hours of work he would have worked that day. So the employer must pay Fred 25 dollars times 7.6 hours, which is 190 dollars. Now the other side of it. Under many awards and agreements, an employee who works on a public holiday will receive a penalty rate or loading for the hours they work, or they may be provided with time off instead of payment of penalty rates, or equivalent time added to their annual leave. Refer to the relevant award, agreement or contract of employment to see what additional entitlements apply. And one firm rule. Rosters cannot be changed to avoid the payment of public holiday entitlements and penalties. Now the interaction questions. Are public holidays paid if the employee is on unpaid leave? If an employee is on paid leave either the day before or the day after the public holiday, they will still be entitled to be paid the public holiday. If an employee is on unpaid leave the day before and after a public holiday, the employee is not entitled to be paid for the public holiday. What about public holidays during long service leave? Yes, public holidays are paid in addition to long service leave in New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, the Australian Capital Territory, Western Australia and Tasmania. No, public holidays are not paid in addition to long service leave in South Australia and the Northern Territory. And sick leave on a rostered public holiday. If an employee calls in sick on a normally rostered day of work that is a public holiday, they get paid for that day as their ordinary pay. It is not treated as personal leave, so no sick leave is deducted. If an employee is rostered to work on a public holiday that they do not normally work and they call in sick, they will not get paid for that day.', '44cd9b8242b1042dd09e92356b992179063f7c38e7b0f15684d37d53a52955e5', 3665, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:41.408+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:41.442151+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:41.408+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('a516b05b-5192-4cd0-ad8f-7b3918df1bfe', 'The eighth National Employment Standard is long service leave. Long service leave is unusual among the standards, because the NES itself does not set the entitlement. It points to other instruments, and in most cases that means state and territory legislation. So this is the standard where you cannot answer a question without knowing which state the employee is in. Click start to begin. Long service leave forms part of the National Employment Standards, and as of the first of January 2010 the NES apply to all employees regardless of their industrial instrument or contract of employment. Under the NES, an employee is entitled to long service leave in accordance with their applicable pre modernised award. Modern awards, from the first of January 2010, cannot include terms dealing with long service leave. So from the first of January 2010, if a pre modernised award does not apply to an employee, any entitlement to long service leave is derived from the applicable state or territory long service leave laws. Depending on the relevant state or territory law, or the relevant industrial instrument, an employee may be entitled to long service leave after a period of continuous service ranging from seven to 10 years with the same or a related employer. Untaken long service leave is usually paid on termination, although this can depend on the circumstances of termination, and depending on the relevant law or instrument an employee may be eligible for a pro rata payment on termination after a minimum of five years of continuous service. Here is the entitlement in each state and territory. The Australian Capital Territory gives 6.0667 weeks after seven years of service, then one fifth of a month for each completed year of service. New South Wales gives 8.667 weeks after 10 years, then 4.333 weeks for each additional five years of service. The Northern Territory gives 13 weeks after 10 years, then 6.5 weeks for each additional five years. Queensland gives 8.667 weeks after 10 years, then 4.333 weeks for each additional five years. South Australia gives 13 weeks after 10 years, then 1.3 weeks for each additional year of service. Tasmania gives 8.667 weeks after 10 years, then 4.333 weeks for each additional five years. Victoria gives 6.0667 weeks after seven years, and there is no additional period of service required to take long service leave. And Western Australia gives 8.667 weeks after 10 years, then 4.333 weeks for each additional five years. The pattern to notice is that the Australian Capital Territory and Victoria qualify at seven years, everywhere else at 10, and the Northern Territory and South Australia are the two most generous on the number of weeks. Depending on the reason for termination, a pro rata payment may have to be made to employees who have completed a particular period of service but have not yet reached their entitlement date. Here are those periods. In the Australian Capital Territory and New South Wales, pro rata applies at five years of service. In the Northern Territory, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania, Victoria and Western Australia, it applies at seven years of service. Two cautions here. First, the reason for termination matters. Pro rata long service leave is not automatically payable on every termination, and the qualifying reasons are set out in the relevant state legislation. Second, note that the pro rata thresholds are not the same as the entitlement thresholds we saw on the previous slide. New South Wales, for example, requires 10 years for the full entitlement but only five years for pro rata on termination.', '91f2ed19cdd2ff571da22c9cdd5de6c30a08e250bd5f3206ef45ad24d88bfc74', 3605, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:45.65+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:45.663153+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:45.65+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('1026150f-6d43-4042-8810-354e62515605', 'The seventh National Employment Standard is community service leave. This is a short standard, but it contains one of the few areas in this module where the rules differ from state to state, so it is worth reading carefully. Click start to begin Under the NES there is an entitlement to community service leave. Employees, including casuals, are entitled to take leave to carry out certain community service activities. The first is jury service, including attendance for jury selection. The second is a voluntary emergency management activity, and there are four conditions for that. The activity deals with an emergency or natural disaster, the employee is a volunteer, the employee is a member of a recognised emergency management body, and that body requests the employee to engage in the activity. A recognised emergency management body can include the State Emergency Service, the Country Fire Authority, and the RSPCA in respect of animal rescue during emergencies or natural disasters. It must be a recognised services body. Two important points. There is no limit to the amount of community service leave an employee can take. And with the exception of jury duty, most community service leave is unpaid. Jury duty is where the state by state differences appear, because the payment obligation comes from state legislation rather than from the NES. Here is the position in each jurisdiction. In New South Wales, Queensland, South Australia, Tasmania and the Northern Territory, the employer tops up to the base rate of pay for ordinary hours, and payment is only required for the first 10 days of absence. In the Australian Capital Territory it is the base rate of pay for ordinary hours, again with payment only required for the first 10 days of absence. Victoria is more generous. The employer tops up to what the employee would have been reasonably expected to earn, including penalties, allowances or loading they would normally receive, and that applies for the entirety of the service. Western Australia covers normal wages, being the earnings the employee could reasonably expect to have been paid, for the entirety of the jury service, and employers may then apply to be reimbursed wages if a loss can be substantiated. Two things to take away. Most states cap the obligation at the first 10 days. Victoria and Western Australia cover the whole period.', 'ef8cf51dd8c4fe1fc264a77ef1dd87fa73f4b3b0e22e257ae29fb6ed595a28a0', 2368, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:19:52.067+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:52.087208+00', '2026-09-09 23:19:52.067+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7a54fac4-0b8d-434e-9b5e-e48bbb5459a2', 'The sixth National Employment Standard bundles together three related entitlements. Personal and carer''s leave, compassionate leave, and family and domestic violence leave. They are grouped because they all deal with an employee''s own illness or with caring for and supporting others, but each has its own rules on eligibility, payment and evidence, so we will take them one at a time. Click start to begin Personal and carer''s leave and compassionate leave both form part of the National Employment Standards, and as of the first of January 2010 the NES apply to all employees covered by the national workplace relations system, regardless of the applicable industrial instrument or contract of employment. The NES establish minimum entitlements to paid personal and carer''s leave, unpaid carer''s leave, and paid or unpaid compassionate leave. These forms of leave are designed to help an employee deal with personal illness, caring responsibilities, family emergencies, and the death or serious illness of close family members. The term personal and carer''s leave effectively covers both sick leave and carer''s leave. The minimum entitlement to paid personal and carer''s leave is 10 days each year for full time employees, and a pro rata 10 days each year for part time employees. Casual employees are not entitled to any paid personal and carer''s leave, but they are entitled to unpaid carer''s leave and unpaid compassionate leave. An employee''s entitlement to paid personal and carer''s leave accumulates progressively during a year of service, based on their ordinary hours of work. The entitlement to 10 days can be calculated as approximately one twenty sixth of an employee''s ordinary hours of work in a year, and the accrual rate you use for that is 26.071428. Here is the example. A full time employee working 38 hours divided by 26.071428 accrues 1.45753 hours of personal and carer''s leave for that week. When paid personal and carer''s leave is taken, the minimum requirement is that the employee is paid at their base rate of pay for the ordinary hours they would have worked during the period. That base rate does not include incentive based payments and bonuses, loadings, monetary allowances, overtime penalty rates, or any other separately identifiable amounts. An employee may take personal and carer''s leave if they are unfit for work because of their own personal illness or injury, or to provide care or support to a member of their immediate family or household because of a personal illness, injury or unexpected emergency. One exception to watch. If the period during which an employee takes paid personal and carer''s leave includes a day or part day that is a public holiday, the employee is not on paid personal and carer''s leave on that public holiday. For all periods of personal and carer''s leave or compassionate leave, an employee must give their employer notice of the taking of the leave, and the employer is entitled to request evidence that would substantiate the reason for the leave. An award or agreement may include terms about the kind of evidence an employee must provide in order to be entitled to paid personal and carer''s leave, unpaid carer''s leave or compassionate leave. For example, the employer may request a medical certificate. On cashing out. For employees covered by an award or agreement, cashing out paid personal and carer''s leave is permitted only if all of the following apply. The award or agreement allows the practice. There is a separate written agreement on each occasion. The employee retains a balance of at least 15 days of untaken paid personal and carer''s leave. And the employee is paid at least the full amount that would have been payable had they taken the leave they cashed out. An award or agreement free employee is not able to cash out paid personal and carer''s leave at all. An employee, including a casual employee, is entitled to two days of compassionate leave to spend time with a member of their immediate family or household who has sustained a life threatening illness or injury. Compassionate leave may also be taken after the death of a member of the employee''s immediate family or household. An employee may take compassionate leave for each occasion as a single continuous two day period, as two separate periods of one day each, or as any separate periods the employee and employer agree on. Note the words for each occasion. The two days are per occasion, not per year. On payment, if an employee other than a casual takes a period of compassionate leave, the employer must pay them at their base rate of pay for the ordinary hours they would have worked during the period. Casual employees are not entitled to paid compassionate leave, but they are entitled to unpaid compassionate leave. Because so many of these entitlements turn on the definition of immediate family, it is worth setting it out in full. An employee''s immediate family is their spouse or former spouse, their de facto partner or former de facto partner, their child, their parent, their grandparent, their grandchild, their sibling, or a child, parent, grandparent, grandchild or sibling of the employee''s spouse or de facto partner, including a former spouse or de facto partner. The definitions include step relations as well as adoptive relations. Household is a separate concept and it is broader in one sense and narrower in another. A member of the employee''s household does not have to be related to them at all, but they do have to live with them. So a housemate can be a member of the household, and a sibling who lives interstate is immediate family but not a member of the household. Either one is enough to support a claim for carer''s or compassionate leave. Now family and domestic violence leave. Family and domestic violence means violent, threatening or other abusive behaviour by an employee''s close relative that seeks to coerce or control the employee, or causes them harm or fear. A close relative is the employee''s spouse or former spouse, de facto partner or former de facto partner, child, parent, grandparent, grandchild or sibling, or the same relations of a current or former spouse or de facto partner, or a person related to the employee according to Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander kinship rules. Employees can take the leave if they need to do something to deal with the impact of family and domestic violence and it is impractical to do so outside their ordinary hours of work. That could include making arrangements for their safety or the safety of a close relative, including relocation, attending court hearings, accessing police services, attending counselling, or attending appointments with medical, financial or legal professionals. The leave does not need to be taken all at once and can be taken as single or multiple days. Here is the entitlement. Full time, part time and casual employees are all able to access 10 days of paid family and domestic violence leave in a 12 month period, and it is not pro rated for part time or casual employees. The full 10 day entitlement is available upfront, and it does not accumulate from year to year if it is not used. This is the one paid leave entitlement in the NES that casuals receive, and it is the one that does not accrue, so it behaves differently from everything else in your system. On payment. Full time and part time employees take paid family and domestic violence leave at their full pay rate for the hours they would have worked if they were not on leave. Casual employees are paid at their full pay rate for the hours they were rostered to work in the period they took leave, and a casual is deemed to have been rostered to work if they were offered and accepted work on that day. Note that this is full pay rate, not base rate. An employee''s full pay rate is their base rate plus any incentive based payments and bonuses, loadings, monetary allowances, overtime or penalty rates, and any other separately identifiable amounts. This slide covers the obligation that most often catches payroll teams out. Employers need to keep a record of leave balances and any leave taken by employees. However, pay slips must not mention paid family and domestic violence leave, including any leave taken and any leave balances. An amount paid to an employee for taking paid family and domestic violence leave has to be recorded on a pay slip as ordinary hours of work, or as another kind of payment for performing work, such as an allowance, bonus or overtime payment. If the employee requests it, the employer can record the time as another type of leave on the pay slip, for example annual leave. The guiding principle is that the pay slip should look as close as possible to how it would have looked if the employee had not taken the leave. On evidence. An employer can ask the employee for evidence showing they need to do something to deal with family and domestic violence and that it is not practical to do that outside their hours of work. Evidence can include documents issued by the police service or a court, family violence support service documents, or a statutory declaration. Employers can ask for evidence for as little as one day or less off work. The employer can only use that information to satisfy themselves that the employee is entitled to the leave, unless the employee consents, the employer is required to deal with the information by law, or it is necessary to protect the life, health or safety of the employee or another person. It cannot be used for other purposes, including to take adverse action against the employee. Employers have to take reasonably practicable steps to keep the information confidential.', 'f9e803a0499a5b126fca11f54e69da72cb3b6d56fa8b9ad56ecab633f4ead193', 9690, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:02.973+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:02.995201+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:02.973+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('dfed5f12-9839-4424-8f1f-0d7b56844af9', 'The fifth National Employment Standard is annual leave. This is bread and butter payroll work, and it is also where most accrual errors happen. We will look at the entitlement, how the accrual is calculated, the rules around taking leave, how it has to be paid, and the rules for cashing out. Then you will have a practice activity to work through. Click start to begin. An employee other than a casual is entitled to four weeks of paid annual leave for each year of service with the employer. An employee classified as a shiftworker is entitled to five weeks of paid annual leave. An employee may qualify for the shiftworker entitlement if they are employed in an enterprise where shifts are continually rostered 24 hours a day for seven days a week, they are regularly rostered to work those shifts, and they regularly work Sundays and public holidays. All three of those need to be true. The Fair Work Act 2009 provides that the entitlement accrues progressively during a year of service according to the employee''s ordinary hours of work. So ordinary hours of work are central to determining the entitlement, which is why we spent time on ordinary hours at the start of this module. Recording leave accrual from hour to hour may not be practical, so the regulations require an employer to keep a record that sets out the balance of an employee''s leave entitlement from time to time. Best practice is to record leave accrual at least monthly, or in line with the pay frequency if the employer pays more often than that. One of your options is to calculate how much leave accrues for every single ordinary hour worked, and there are two easy ways to do that. Divide the number of ordinary hours worked over the period by 13.035714. Or multiply the number of ordinary hours worked by 0.076712. Both give the same answer. Here is the worked example. Ordinary hours worked over a year is 365 divided by 7, times 38, which is 1981.4286 hours. Divide that by 13.035714 and you get 152 hours. Multiply it by 0.076712 and you also get 152 hours. And 152 hours is exactly four weeks at 38 hours a week. One caveat worth knowing. These formulas assume there are exactly 52 weeks in a year, so they may give employees a small additional amount each year, up to about an hour. What they guarantee is that the entire minimum NES entitlement of four weeks is accrued by the end of the 52nd week. Annual leave does not have to be taken each year, because the entitlement can accumulate. In certain circumstances an employer can direct an employee to take annual leave, and a requirement to take paid annual leave may be reasonable if the employee has accrued an excessive amount of annual leave, or the company is being shut down for a period, such as between Christmas and New Year. Annual leave may also be taken in advance of accrual by mutual agreement between the employer and the employee. Note the word reasonable in there. The ability to direct an employee to take leave is not unlimited, and what is reasonable will often be spelled out in the applicable award or agreement, so check the instrument before issuing a direction. When annual leave is taken, the minimum requirement is that the employee must be paid at their base rate of pay for the ordinary hours they would have worked during the period. On termination of employment, the employer must pay the employee the value of the untaken annual leave. Separately from that minimum, there is leave loading. Leave loading, typically 17.5 per cent, is a bonus payment intended to compensate workers for extra pay lost when taking leave, such as overtime and other penalty rates that they would have earned had they been at work. Leave loading is not an NES entitlement. It comes from the applicable award, agreement or contract, so whether it applies and at what rate depends on the instrument covering the employee. Cashing out of annual leave is permitted for employees under an award or agreement which allows the practice. In July 2016, most modern awards were amended to incorporate terms for cashing out annual leave. The specific terms are in the relevant modern award, but the following rules apply to all employees who are permitted to cash out annual leave. The employee needs to have at least four weeks of annual leave left over. A written agreement needs to be made each time annual leave is cashed out. An employer cannot force or pressure an employee to cash out annual leave. And the payment for cashed out annual leave has to be the same as what the employee would have been paid if they took the leave. That last rule has a practical consequence. An employee who cashes out annual leave must receive what they would have been entitled to had they taken the leave, so if leave loading would have applied to the leave, leave loading still applies to the cashed out amount.', '6703fa4fb121cfa7020283c0f3e50621a6e4be2ce46e9be9160a11e79e817515', 4838, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:18.33+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:18.350362+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:18.33+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ae0cf9c9-5d93-45e5-89aa-6ddee0e0ab17', 'The fourth National Employment Standard is parental leave and related entitlements. There are really two separate things to keep apart here. Unpaid parental leave, which is the employee''s entitlement under the NES and which the employer administers. And government paid parental leave, which is a separate Commonwealth funded payment administered through Services Australia, and which the employer often pays out through the payroll. We will do unpaid parental leave first, then the paid scheme. Click start to begin The NES entitlement may be exercised by permanent employees after 12 months of continuous service. A parent who is an eligible employee may take a period of up to 12 months of unpaid parental leave in relation to the birth of a child, or the adoption of a child below school age. They may also request up to 12 months of additional leave, and the employer can only refuse that request on reasonable business grounds. Both parents are able to take up to 12 months of unpaid parental leave each, at any time within 24 months of their child''s birth or placement, and both can apply for an extension of up to 12 months beyond the initial 12 month amount. So the practical ceiling for each parent is 12 months as of right, plus up to another 12 months by request. Part of the unpaid parental leave entitlement can be taken flexibly. Flexible unpaid parental leave may be taken as a single continuous period of one or more days, or as separate periods of one or more days each. A pregnant employee can access flexible unpaid parental leave up to six weeks before the expected date of birth of the child. The point of the flexible arrangement is that the leave does not have to be one unbroken block, which matters for employees who want to phase a return to work. From a payroll point of view, single days of unpaid parental leave still need to be recorded as unpaid parental leave, not as some other kind of unpaid absence, because the entitlement is capped and the balance has to be tracked. Now the mechanics of taking the leave. Unpaid parental leave must start, for a pregnant employee, within 24 months of the birth of the child, or up to six weeks before the expected birth, or earlier if the employer agrees. For an employee other than the pregnant employee, the leave has to start and end within 24 months of the birth of the child. And for adoption leave, on the day of the child''s placement. In all cases the leave must end within the 24 month period starting on the date of birth or placement. An employer may direct a pregnant employee to start unpaid parental leave up to six weeks before the expected date of birth if the employer considers, based on medical evidence, that there is a risk to the employee in working in their present circumstances. That risk might arise from a pregnancy related illness or from hazards connected with the employee''s work. An employee is required to give the employer at least 10 weeks'' written notice before starting parental leave, or if that is not practicable, to provide the notice as soon as practicable, which may even be a time after the leave has started. A premature birth is the usual example of when notice cannot be given in advance. Employers may require evidence of the expected date of birth, or the day or expected day of placement for adoption. And when a period of unpaid parental leave ends, the employee is entitled to return to their pre parental leave position. If that position no longer exists, they are entitled to return to an available position for which they are qualified and suited, nearest in status and pay to their pre parental leave position. The NES includes a keeping in touch provision. Keeping in touch days allow an employee who is still on unpaid parental leave to go back to work for a few days. The employee can undertake paid work on a day provided three conditions are met. The purpose of the work is to enable the person to keep in touch with work or to facilitate a return to work. Both the employee and the employer consent to the work being done on that day. And the day is not within 42 days after the child was born. An employee may request to work a keeping in touch day earlier than that, and if a request is made, a keeping in touch day cannot be worked earlier than 14 days after the birth or adoption. An employee is allowed 10 keeping in touch days. This work does count as service, and it attracts the usual employment provisions including pay and leave accrual. If the employee extends their period of unpaid parental leave beyond 12 months, they can take an additional 10 days. Keeping in touch days can be worked as a part day, one day at a time, a few days at a time, or all at once. One important restriction. Employees are not able to work a keeping in touch day on a day they are receiving government paid parental leave. Now we move from the employer''s unpaid entitlement to the government funded scheme. The Paid Parental Leave scheme is a government funded entitlement for working parents who are the primary caregivers of a child born or adopted from the first of January 2011. An eligible family can receive taxable Parental Leave Pay at the rate of the National Minimum Wage. For a child born on or after the first of July 2026, eligible families can receive up to 130 days, or 26 weeks based on a five day working week. Single parents can take the full entitlement. The leave can be taken as a single block of time, in multiple smaller blocks, as single days, or in smaller blocks combined with single days. Working parents who are full time, part time, self employed, casual, contract and seasonal employees all have access to Parental Leave Pay if they meet the eligibility criteria, and employees need to pass a work history test, an income test and a residency test. One point to be clear about. It is not the employer''s obligation to determine whether an employee is eligible for Parental Leave Pay. Employees lodge their claims with Centrelink, and Centrelink determines eligibility. Parents can share their Parental Leave Pay with each other, and they can even take days at the same time. That can be at the same time as paid leave, and between periods of paid work, which gives families more flexibility to manage work and care arrangements. The birth mother or first adoptive parent must give approval to share any Parental Leave Pay days. If they are sharing days with someone else, they can take up to 10 days at the same time. Part of Parental Leave Pay is also reserved for each parent to use, and how much is reserved depends on the child''s date of birth or adoption. For a child from the first of July 2023, 10 days or two weeks are reserved for the pregnant employee''s partner. From the first of July 2024, again 10 days or two weeks. From the first of July 2025, 15 days or three weeks. And from the first of July 2026, 20 days or four weeks. Any unused portions of Parental Leave Pay days will be lost if they are not used before the child turns two, which is designed to encourage both parents to access the payment. Finally for this standard, the employer''s part in paying Parental Leave Pay. Where the criteria are met, the employer pays Parental Leave Pay to the employee in line with their regular pay cycle. Before the employee submits their claim, the employer needs to opt in to provide Parental Leave Pay. If the employer does not do that before the claim is submitted, they will not be able to pay it, and if the block of Parental Leave Pay is in the past, Services Australia may pay the employee directly. You are required to provide Parental Leave Pay to eligible employees in their usual pay cycle, and you must withhold tax. You must also give the employee a record of the payment, which can be a normal pay slip and can be provided electronically. You will receive the Paid Parental Leave funds before you have to pay them to the employee, so this is not funded out of the employer''s own money. Parental Leave Pay can be salary sacrificed where the employer and the employee agree to the arrangement. And to be clear about what employers do not have to do. Employers do not need to assess their employee''s eligibility for the scheme, and they do not need to pay superannuation on Parental Leave Pay.', '78c2ac08acc591457dfddb9820f6c1ff11ea65963ebbcd4f4d4216c03088441f', 8263, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:26.126+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:26.144892+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:26.126+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('aa21e4d7-35a2-4274-b797-62a1bec8b50c', 'The third National Employment Standard is casual employment. This is the standard that changed most recently and most substantially, so it is worth working through carefully. The rules about casual conversion changed on the twenty sixth of August 2024, and with them came a new definition of a casual employee and a new pathway for casuals who want to become permanent. Click start to begin. Here is the shape of the change. The rules about casual conversion changed on the twenty sixth of August 2024. Two things are now true at the same time. First, a casual employee can change to permanent employment, either full time or part time, at any time if the employer and the employee agree. That has not changed. Second, casuals now also have a pathway to permanent employment under the National Employment Standards, and this is referred to as the employee choice pathway. Under those rules, eligible casuals can notify their employer in writing of their intention to change to permanent employment, and the employer can only refuse the notice for certain specified reasons. So agreement is one route and the employee choice pathway is the other. The rest of this section works through the definition of a casual, the eligibility rules for the pathway, and how an employer has to respond. A person is a casual employee if, when they start employment, two things are true. First, the employment relationship has no firm advance commitment to ongoing work, taking into account a number of factors. Second, they are entitled to a casual loading, or a specific casual pay rate, under an award, a registered agreement or their employment contract. Both limbs have to be satisfied. Notice that the test is applied at the point the employment starts, and notice that paying a casual loading on its own does not make somebody a casual if there is in fact a firm advance commitment to ongoing work. That is the change in emphasis that came in from the twenty sixth of August 2024. Whether there is a firm advance commitment has to be assessed on the real substance, the practical reality and the true nature of the employment relationship, along with several other factors. The factors that must be considered include whether the employer can offer or not offer work to the employee, and whether that is actually happening. Whether the employee can accept or reject work, and whether that is actually happening. Whether it is reasonably likely there will be future work available of the kind the employee usually performs, based on the nature of the business. Whether there are full time or part time employees performing the same kind of work in the business. And whether the employee has a regular pattern of work, even if it changes over time because of things like reasonable absences for illness, injury or other leave. That is not a full list and other factors may apply. Importantly, not all factors need to be satisfied and no single factor decides the question. A regular pattern of work on its own does not indicate a firm advance commitment. An employee with a regular pattern of work may still be a casual employee if there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing work. When assessing this you can look at the contract of employment, or the contract together with any mutual understanding or expectation between the parties that is not part of the contract, which can be worked out from how the contract is performed. Now the eligibility rules for the employee choice pathway. A casual can give written notice to their employer to change to permanent employment, full time or part time, if they have been employed for at least six months, or twelve months if they are employed by a small business, and they believe they no longer meet the requirements of the casual employee definition. There are also two situations where an employee cannot give that notice. They cannot notify if they are currently engaged in an ongoing dispute with their employer about casual conversion. And they cannot notify if, in the last six months, their employer refused a previous notice, or they resolved a dispute with their employer about employee choice under a relevant dispute resolution process. So there is a six month cooling off period after a refusal or a resolved dispute. Before responding to a notice, the employer must consult with the employee. That consultation includes discussing what will change if the employer accepts the notice and the employee is no longer a casual. So whether the employee would be full time or part time, what their hours of work would be, and when the change would take effect. The employer must then respond in writing within 21 days of the employee giving the notice, either accepting or not accepting the change. If the employer accepts, the written response has to state what the new employment status will be, the employee''s new hours of work, and when the change will take effect. The change must take effect from the first day of the employee''s first full pay period starting after the employer gives their response, unless the employee and employer agree to another day. That last point is the one payroll needs to diarise, because it sets the effective date for the change from casual loading to permanent entitlements. If the employer does not accept the change, the written response must include the reasons for the decision, and the reasons can only be one of the following. The employee still meets the definition of a casual employee. Or there are fair and reasonable operational grounds for not accepting the notification, such as substantial changes being required to the way work in the business is organised, significant impacts on the operation of the business, or substantial changes to the employee''s employment conditions being necessary to make sure the employer does not break rules that apply to the employee, such as rules in an award or agreement. Or accepting the change would mean the employer would not comply with a recruitment or selection process required by law. Finally, remember that an employee who starts as a casual stays casual until their employment status actually changes, and that happens in one of three ways. They accept an alternative employment offer and start work on that basis. They change to permanent under the rules in the NES, their award or their agreement. Or a Fair Work Commission order changes it.', '30d03116db82e5122c33cf0bfb84c2b7612363b6f481163e9abf1e2189302a85', 6384, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:30.624+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:30.630914+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:30.624+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('92a56b52-c393-443c-9697-d4038cff0930', 'The second National Employment Standard covers requests for flexible working arrangements. This is a standard that payroll is often asked about, because a change to somebody''s hours or pattern of work flows straight through to their pay, their accruals and often their allowances. The standard sets out who can ask, what they can ask for, and what the employer has to do with the request once it arrives. Click start to begin. Employees who have worked with the same employer for at least 12 months can request flexible working arrangements if they fall into one of these categories. They are the parent, or have responsibility for the care, of a child who is school aged or younger. They are a carer under the Carer Recognition Act 2010. They have a disability and are qualified for a disability support pension under the Social Security Act 1991. They are 55 or older. They are pregnant. They are experiencing family or domestic violence. Or they provide care or support to a member of their household or immediate family who requires care and support because of family or domestic violence. Casual employees can also make a request. To do that, a casual must meet one of those same criteria, they must have been working for the same employer regularly and systematically for at least 12 months, and there must be a reasonable expectation of continuing work on a regular and systematic basis. So what can actually be requested. The standard gives three examples. Changes to hours of work, such as changes to start and finish times. Changes to patterns of work, such as split shifts or job sharing. And changes to locations of work, such as working from home. These are examples rather than a closed list, and in practice a single request often combines more than one of them. From a payroll point of view, each of these can have a downstream effect. A change to hours changes the basis for leave accrual. A change to a pattern of work can change which penalty rates apply. A change to location can affect allowances. So when a flexible working arrangement is approved, make sure payroll is told what has actually changed and from when. Employers who receive a request must give a written response within 21 days, saying whether the request is granted or refused. There are rules for refusing. An employer can only refuse on reasonable business grounds, and only if they have discussed the request with the employee and genuinely tried to reach an agreement on alternative arrangements to accommodate the employee''s circumstances, and considered the consequences of refusing the request. If the request is refused, the written response has to include three things. The reasons for the refusal, including an explanation of the grounds and how they apply to this request. Any other changes the employer is willing to make, or a statement that there are no changes to be made. And information about getting help from the Fair Work Commission for disputes about flexible working arrangements. Note that all three of those are mandatory content in the response. A simple no is not a compliant answer. Reasonable business grounds can include the following. The requested arrangements are too costly. Other employees'' working arrangements cannot be changed to accommodate the request. It would be impractical to change other employees'' working arrangements, or to hire new employees, to accommodate the request. The request is likely to result in a significant loss in efficiency or productivity. Or the request would have a significant negative impact on customer service. The employer''s own circumstances can be factored in when considering whether there are reasonable business grounds. For example, the size of the employer and the nature of the business. What is reasonable for a business with 400 employees may not be reasonable for a business with four. Sitting alongside flexible working requests are individual flexibility agreements, usually called IFAs. All awards, enterprise agreements and other registered agreements have to include an individual flexibility agreement clause. If a registered agreement does not include one, the model clause from the Fair Work Regulations 2009 applies instead. An IFA is a written agreement between an employer and an employee that changes the effect of certain clauses in their award or registered agreement, so that the arrangement suits both parties. An IFA cannot be used to reduce or remove an employee''s entitlements. The employer has to be satisfied that the employee is better off overall with the IFA than without it, compared against their award or registered agreement at the time the IFA was made, looking at financial and non-financial benefits as well as the employee''s personal circumstances. Depending on the instrument, the clauses that can be varied include arrangements for when work is performed such as working hours, overtime rates, penalty rates, allowances, and leave loading. Every one of those five is a payroll item, so if an IFA exists you need a copy of it.', '8f1d9c1112208e02001ac2d5778f9b121ef3b1b735a917373a25bf189732cb08', 5028, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:34.912+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:34.951063+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:34.912+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e07ea10c-014b-4d4e-bf68-a6c80ab50d3f', 'Let us start with the first National Employment Standard, maximum weekly hours of work. This standard does two things. It sets the maximum number of hours an employee can be required to work in a week, and it sets out the circumstances in which an employee can refuse a request to work additional hours. It also deals with the averaging of hours, which is something you will come across in a number of awards. Click start to begin. Under the NES an employee can work a maximum of 38 ordinary hours in a week. For a full time employee that figure is 38 hours. For anyone other than a full time employee it is the lesser of 38 hours or their ordinary hours of work in a week, so a part time employee working 20 hours has a maximum of 20 ordinary hours. The applicable award or enterprise agreement will specify how those hours are to be arranged. Ordinary hours can be structured in a variety of ways. It might be up to eight or ten hours per day, or the very common arrangement of 7.6 hours per day over five days, which totals 38 hours. There is flexibility in how the 38 hours are scheduled, but they represent the maximum number of ordinary hours that can be worked in a week. One more point that people often miss. When you are counting the hours an employee has worked in a week, you must include any hours of leave or absence, paid or unpaid, that has been authorised by the employer, by a term of their employment, or under a law. Overtime is when an employee works extra time. It can include work done beyond their ordinary hours of work, outside the agreed number of hours, or outside the spread of ordinary hours. That spread is sometimes called the span of hours, and it is simply the window of the day inside which ordinary hours can be worked. Overtime is defined in the award or agreement, so it could be defined differently in the instrument that applies to your business and you do need to check. Overtime work is often paid at a higher rate, typically a multiplier such as 1.5 or 2.0 times normal. In some awards, overtime entitlements do not apply to part time and casual workers until they have worked the same number of hours as a full time worker. And in other cases, instead of receiving overtime payments, employees may be able to take time off to make up for the extra time worked. That is called time in lieu, or time off in lieu, often shortened to TOIL. An employer can request that an employee works reasonable overtime, and an employee can refuse to work overtime if the request is unreasonable. An employer can ask an employee to work reasonable additional hours, and an employee can refuse if the request is unreasonable. So the obvious question is what makes additional hours reasonable. The legislation lists the factors that must be taken into account, and these are the ones you see here. Is there any risk to the employee health or safety. An employee who has already worked twelve hours being asked to work another two is a very different proposition to someone who has worked six. What are the employee personal circumstances, including family responsibilities. Telling someone at three in the afternoon that they need to stay back two hours when you know they collect a child from childcare is probably an unreasonable request. The needs of the workplace also count, so it is not only about the employee. Whether the employee will be paid for the additional hours matters, whether that is overtime, penalty rates or a level of remuneration that already reflects an expectation of extra hours. What notice was given, both by the employer requesting the hours and by the employee refusing them. The usual patterns of work in the industry, because in some industries additional hours are simply built into the roster. And the nature of the employee role and their level of responsibility. Generally the higher the level of responsibility and the higher the pay, the more additional hours are expected as part of the job. What is reasonable is assessed case by case. But requiring an employee to work whatever hours it takes is not reasonable, even where that employee is paid a salary above the statutory minimum. The 38 hours does not have to be exactly 38 hours in every single week, because many awards and agreements allow for the averaging of hours over a specified period longer than a week. The average weekly hours over that period must still not exceed 38 hours for a full time employee. Take the Clerks Private Sector Award as an example. Clause 13.2 says the maximum number of ordinary hours that can be worked in a week is an average of 38 hours per week over a period of up to four weeks, or 38 hours per week over a roster period agreed between the employer and the employee. So an employee might work 40 hours in one week and 36 the next, then 40 and 36 again. Over that four week period they have averaged 38 hours per week, and in the weeks they worked 40 hours there is no overtime payable because an averaging arrangement is in place. Three things to remember. The averaging needs to be set in advance so that people know they are working to an average. You cannot force an employee into an averaging arrangement, and under the general workplace protections it is unlawful to try. And additional hours must still be reasonable, so 56 hours one week and 20 the next is unlikely to pass that test. For award and agreement free employees, employers and employees can agree in writing to average their ordinary hours, but the maximum averaging period is 26 weeks. Penalty rates and loadings may apply to an employee ordinary hours. A penalty rate is a higher rate of pay for work done outside usual working hours, such as late at night, on weekends or on public holidays. It might be a Saturday, Sunday, public holiday or evening penalty, or a shift allowance. Take the General Retail Award as an example. Ordinary hours worked after 6pm Monday to Friday attract a loading of 25 per cent on top of the normal rate. Ordinary hours on a Saturday attract 25 per cent. Ordinary hours on a Sunday attract 50 per cent. And ordinary hours on a public holiday attract an extra 125 per cent. Separate from penalty rates, we also have loadings tied to employment type. Casual loading is an amount paid on top of the base rate of pay to casual employees, and it compensates them for not receiving entitlements that permanent employees get, such as paid annual leave and paid personal and carer''s leave. In the General Retail Award that loading is 25 per cent of the minimum hourly rate. Some awards and agreements also provide a part time loading, which is typically lower than the casual loading because part time employees still receive pro rata annual leave and personal leave. There is often confusion about penalty rates and overtime, partly because different awards and agreements use the terms interchangeably. Here is the distinction that will serve you well. A penalty rate is typically applied to hours worked within the employee ordinary hours. So if their ordinary hours are 38 and some of those hours fall on a night shift, a weekend or a public holiday, the extra amount is usually called a penalty rate or a penalty loading. Overtime, on the other hand, is typically applied to hours worked outside the employee ordinary hours. It might be time and a half, double time, or double time and a half, depending on when the overtime is worked and what the award or agreement says. Under the Clerks Private Sector Award, for example, overtime Monday to Saturday is 150 per cent for the first two hours and 200 per cent after that. Sunday overtime is all at double time, and public holiday overtime is all at double time and a half. If your employees are on an annualised salary, you may not need to pay these penalty rates and overtime amounts separately, provided those entitlements are included in the salary and the employee is better off overall. To determine whether overtime applies, work through four steps. Identify the ordinary hours of work fixed by the award. Identify the hours fixed for an ordinary week of work. Identify whether the employee worked outside either of those. Then determine whether the award requires overtime or a penalty rate for those hours.', '92e3aa41a5412ae36e6a9707083874ab93928a10ca37758be6934f54391564ce', 8221, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:39.1+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:39.142358+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:39.1+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('edbd4bb7-ad2b-4bfd-a10c-bb9871275270', 'Welcome to Module 2 of Payroll Essentials. In this module we look at terms and conditions of employment, and in particular the National Employment Standards. The National Employment Standards, or the NES, are the twelve minimum entitlements that apply to employees in the national workplace relations system. They sit at the heart of almost everything payroll does, because they tell you who gets what, how much leave to accrue and what has to be paid when employment ends. Over the next series of videos we will work through each of the twelve standards in turn, look at how they apply in practice and complete four practice activities along the way. Let''s get started. Before we start, here is what you will be able to do by the end of this module. You will be able to name the twelve National Employment Standards and explain what each one provides. You will be able to work out ordinary hours against overtime for a full time, a part time and a casual employee under an award. You will be able to calculate annual leave and personal leave accruals using the standard accrual rates. You will be able to identify the correct notice and redundancy entitlements on termination. And you will know which obligations are mandatory under the legislation, and which parts are simply good practice or vary from one award to the next. That last point matters more than anything else in this module, so we will keep coming back to it. From the first of January 2010, employers and employees in the national workplace relations system have been covered by the National Employment Standards. Under the NES, employees have certain minimum conditions. Together with the pay rates in modern awards and the minimum wage orders, the NES makes up a safety net, and that safety net cannot be altered to the disadvantage of the employee. That is the single most important idea in this module. You can always give an employee more than the NES provides. You can never give them less. It is also worth knowing that the national system is broader than you might expect. Sometimes businesses operating as sole traders, partnerships, other unincorporated entities, non-trading corporations, and state public sector or local government employers have registered agreements in the national system, and employees covered by those agreements are within the national system. Beyond the NES, an employee terms and conditions can also come from a modern award, a registered agreement, a pre-modern award and state or federal laws. The National Employment Standards are set out in the Fair Work Act 2009 and there are twelve of them. Here they are in full. Maximum weekly hours of work. Requests for flexible working arrangements. Casual employment. Parental leave and related entitlements. Annual leave. Personal and carer''s leave, compassionate leave and family and domestic violence leave. Community service leave. Long service leave. Public holidays. Superannuation. Notice of termination and redundancy pay. And the Fair Work Information Statements. Two of these are newer than the rest. Superannuation only became an entitlement under the NES from the first of January 2024, and the casual employment standard changed substantially from the twenty sixth of August 2024. We will cover both of those changes in detail as we go. Twelve standards is a lot to hold in your head, so here is a way of grouping them that makes the list much easier to remember. The first three are about working hours. Come and work for us, and these are the hours you will be working. Maximum weekly hours, requests for flexible working arrangements and casual employment. The next six, numbers four through to nine, are all about time away from work. Parental leave, annual leave, personal and carer''s leave, community service leave, long service leave and public holidays. Number ten sits on its own. Superannuation is money paid on your behalf while you are employed. Number eleven is about ending employment, so notice of termination and redundancy pay. And number twelve is about information, the statements you must give employees when they start. If you can remember hours, leave, super, ending employment and information, you can rebuild the full list of twelve from memory.', 'ae41eb11cc542286368ebf8ca6017cc048737ad7889828adaa960c3e1c083596', 4233, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:45.018+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:45.048012+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:45.018+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bbee91b6-898c-4d93-ab3e-2db0622aedae', 'Welcome to Payroll Essentials. This is Module One, the Australian Industrial Relations System. Over this module we are going to build the foundation that everything else in payroll sits on. Before you can work out what to pay someone, you need to know which system covers them, which instrument sets their pay and conditions, and what type of employee they actually are. We will start with the Fair Work system and the legislation behind it. Then we will look at awards, registered agreements and individual contracts and how they stack together. After that we will work through the different types of employment, and the difference between an employee and an independent contractor. We will finish with a warm up activity that gets you calculating hourly rates and period salaries. Let''s get started. Let''s set out what you will be able to do by the end of this module. First, you will understand which employers and employees are covered by the national workplace relations system, and which ones sit under a state or territory system instead. That matters because it changes which rules you apply. Second, you will be able to describe how the National Employment Standards, awards, registered agreements and individual contracts fit together, and which one takes priority. Third, you will be able to tell the difference between full time, part time and casual employment, and explain what a casual employee actually is under the Fair Work Act. And fourth, you will understand the factors that separate an employee from an independent contractor, and why getting that wrong creates real risk for the business. We will finish with some practical calculations so you can convert between weekly, fortnightly, monthly and hourly rates with confidence.', '78b85163f9f69028842fda59d38da2009c356228b44bb035e94791c3ef5c7b60', 1749, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:20:50.331+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:50.356587+00', '2026-09-09 23:20:50.331+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('1b08e8ea-4643-4c96-8d02-9e635f97cc85', 'Now we turn to types of employment. Getting this right determines what pay and what conditions an employee is entitled to. Click start to begin learning. To work out what pay and conditions an employee is entitled to, you first need to know whether they are full time, part time or casual. Full time employees generally work 38 hours a week and have a continuing contract of employment. They receive the full range of entitlements, including paid personal leave, annual leave, long service leave and carer''s leave. There is a common misconception here. People say full time is 38 hours a week. What the National Employment Standards actually set is a maximum of 38 ordinary hours per week. Some awards set fewer. The road transport and distribution award, for example, has one stream of employees on 38 ordinary hours and oil distribution workers on a 35 hour week. One award, two different standards. Part time employees work regular hours each week, but fewer than a full time employee. They generally receive the same basic entitlements as full timers, calculated on a pro rata basis according to the hours they work. For both full time and part time employees, accrued entitlements such as annual leave must be paid out when they leave. If they are dismissed or made redundant they may also be entitled to notice of termination or payment in lieu, except in cases of serious misconduct, and to redundancy pay. Casual employment has a specific legal meaning. A person is a casual employee if they accept an offer of work knowing that there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing work with an agreed pattern of work. Under the Fair Work Act, three elements must be present. They are offered a job. The offer does not include a firm advance commitment that the work will continue indefinitely with an agreed pattern of work. And they accept the offer knowing that, and become an employee. In return, a casual employee is entitled to receive a casual loading or a specific casual pay rate. The loading is commonly 25 per cent, although some awards and agreements set a different figure. It compensates for the entitlements a casual does not receive, such as annual leave, personal leave, redundancy pay and notice of termination. Once someone starts as a casual, they stay casual until their status changes. That happens in one of two ways. Either through a conversion process or an order of the Fair Work Commission, or by accepting an alternative employment offer and starting work on that basis. From the 26th of August 2024, a new definition of casual employee was introduced into the Fair Work Act. Under this definition, an employee is only a casual if there is no firm advance commitment to continuing and indefinite work, taking into account the real substance, the practical reality and the true nature of the employment relationship. Employees who were employed casually before that date stay casual under the new definition unless they transition to permanent employment. So how do you assess whether a firm advance commitment exists? You look at the substance of the relationship, along with a number of other factors. Whether the employer can offer or not offer work, and whether that is actually happening. Whether the employee can accept or reject work. Whether it is reasonably likely there will be future work available, based on the nature of the business. Whether there are full time or part time employees performing the same kind of work. And whether the employee has a regular pattern of work. That is not a complete list, and other factors may apply. No single factor decides it. You are looking at the overall picture. Let''s work through the example in your book. Josh starts as a bartender for a new summer pop up restaurant. Each week he gets a roster with the shifts he is being offered for the following week, and he decides whether to accept them or tell his employer, Angela, that he cannot work them. His shifts vary from week to week during summer, depending on when Angela needs him. Josh is paid a casual loading of 25 per cent, and all the other employees at the restaurant are casual too. Angela is not sure whether the restaurant will keep operating after summer. So is Josh a casual? Yes. He is a casual employee for two reasons. There is no firm advance commitment to continuing and indefinite work, and he is earning a casual pay rate. Notice how the factors line up. The roster changes week to week, he can accept or decline shifts, and the future of the business itself is uncertain. Together those point clearly to casual employment. Two other terms come up regularly, and both are often misunderstood. A shift worker is not really a separate type of employee. A shift worker is someone whose roster includes hours outside the standard working day, and they can be full time, part time or casual. Two people can do exactly the same job in the same call centre, one working nine to five and the other working one until nine in the evening. Both are employees. The difference is that the shift worker may receive additional penalty rates for those hours, for example a shift loading for hours worked between ten at night and six in the morning. Awards and registered agreements define what counts as shift work and what loadings apply. Daily hire and weekly hire are common in the construction industry. These employees have similar entitlements to full time and part time employees, including annual leave and personal leave, but different notice requirements. A daily hire employee or employer gives one day''s notice to end employment, and a weekly hire employee or employer gives one week. A daily hire tradesperson is entitled to one hour before termination to collect, clean, sharpen and transport their tools. Daily hire employees also receive a follow the job loading to compensate for periods between jobs when they are not working.', 'f5f358a9eef4331d01feb23a775dc36320fb4f8e5fb3f8c3759fee0c487ff2d8', 5883, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:08.198+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:08.211308+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:08.198+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.module_transcripts (module_id, transcript, content_hash, char_count, source, fetched_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('21a82136-d2f5-4785-87c2-4459eb28073b', 'Before we finish, let''s look over the answers to the warm up activity also on page 12 of your workbook. It is straightforward maths, but it is the maths you will use every day in payroll. Click start to begin. Let''s check part one. All you are doing is dividing the weekly rate by the number of hours. One thousand five hundred and eighty four dollars and fifty eight cents divided by 38 hours gives 41 dollars point 6 9 9 5. One thousand seven hundred and eighty four dollars and sixty four cents divided by 40 hours gives 44 dollars point 6 1 6 0. Eight hundred and ninety two dollars and seventy cents divided by 37.5 hours gives 23 dollars point 8 0 5 3. And eight hundred and fifty four dollars and thirty five cents divided by 28 hours gives 30 dollars  point 5 1 2 5. Now part two. Which of those is below the national minimum wage? The answer is 23 dollars point 8 0 5 3, because the adult national minimum wage is 26 dollars and 44 cents an hour. If you saw that rate in a real payroll you would want to check whether the employee is an apprentice, a junior, or on a supported wage. If they are none of those, something is wrong. One other thing to notice in this table. The second row shows 40 ordinary hours in a week. Under the National Employment Standards the maximum is 38 ordinary hours, so anything above that would normally be overtime. Part three. Here you take the annual salary and divide it by the number of pay periods in the year. Seventy five thousand dollars paid fortnightly, divided by 26, gives two thousand eight hundred and eighty four dollars and sixty two cents. One hundred and fifty thousand dollars paid monthly, divided by 12, gives twelve thousand five hundred dollars. Ninety six thousand dollars paid weekly, divided by 52, gives one thousand eight hundred and forty six dollars and fifteen cents. And sixty five thousand dollars paid monthly, divided by 12, gives five thousand four hundred and sixteen dollars and sixty seven cents. These divisors of 52, 26 and 12 are what most organisations use, and they are what the Australian Taxation Office always uses. Some employers, particularly in government, use a different fortnightly divisor, and we will look at why on the next slide. Part four. On the dollars side, an employee on ninety thousand dollars a year. Divided by 52 that is one thousand seven hundred and thirty dollars and seventy seven cents a week. Divided by 26 it is three thousand four hundred and sixty one dollars and fifty four cents a fortnight. Divided by 12 it is seven thousand five hundred dollars a month. On the hours side, an employee working one thousand nine hundred and seventy six hours a year works 38 hours a week, 76 hours a fortnight, and 164 point six seven hours a month. That figure of one thousand nine hundred and seventy six hours is worth recognising on sight. It is 38 hours a week multiplied by 52 weeks. If you see it in your payroll system, that is where it comes from. One point worth understanding before we finish, because it comes up often. There are not exactly 52 weeks or 26 fortnights in a year. In three years out of four there are 365 days, which is 52 point 14 weeks or 26 point zero seven fortnights. Every fourth year has 366 days. Averaged across four years, 365 point two five days divided by 14 gives 26 point zero eight nine three fortnights. Some employers, particularly in government and under some enterprise agreements, use that divisor of 26 point zero eight nine three. Most employers use a simple 26. The difference is small. On a salary of one hundred and four thousand dollars, a divisor of 26 pays four thousand dollars a fortnight, while 26 point zero eight nine three pays about thirteen dollars and sixty nine cents less. Using the simpler divisor pays the employee slightly more, so there is no compliance problem. What matters is that you know how your own system is configured, because an employee may one day ask you why their income statement does not exactly match their contracted salary. And keep in mind that whatever Fair Work or an agreement says, when it comes to tax calculations the Australian Taxation Office always uses 52, 26 and 12. Let''s bring it together. Most employees in Australia are covered by the national workplace relations system under the Fair Work Act and Regulations, but some state public sector, local government and Western Australian employers remain under a state system, so always check. Employment conditions sit in a hierarchy. The National Employment Standards form the floor, awards add industry or occupation specific terms, registered agreements can replace the award as long as employees are better off overall, and individual contracts sit on top without undercutting anything below. The type of employment determines the entitlements. Full time and part time employees accrue leave and receive notice, while casual employees receive a loading in place of those entitlements and are defined by the absence of a firm advance commitment. And whether a worker is an employee or an independent contractor is decided by the substance of the relationship, weighing control, exclusivity, payment, delegation, risk, hours, tools and how they are engaged. In the next module we move on to the twelve National Employment Standards in detail, starting with maximum weekly hours of work. Congratulations! You have now completed Module 1 the Australian Industrial Relations System. Before moving on, make sure you have completed the knowledge check for this module. When you’re ready, continue to the next module to build on what you’ve learned. Well done, and we’ll see you in the next module!', 'a8a6bcdee0ebde7e9ec88fc55101e2ea68b2f6cbdcd04afa8cacf1f9c1ef4569', 5646, 'synthesia-captions', '2026-09-09 23:21:29.211+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:29.241585+00', '2026-09-09 23:21:29.212+00');
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('manager_gone_quiet', true, 21, NULL, 14, 'Manager gone quiet', 'The person who bought the product has stopped showing up. The earliest churn signal there is. Only raised for organisations whose subscription is active — there is no point chasing a lapsed client.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('org_gone_dark', true, 28, NULL, 21, 'Organisation gone dark', 'No learner in the whole client has watched anything in weeks. A renewal conversation you want to have before, not after.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('learners_falling_behind', true, NULL, 50, 14, 'Learners falling behind', 'This share of an organisation''s learners are off-pace against their 90-day plan. The same signal Reports will show; this is the push version.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('seats_nearly_full', true, NULL, 90, 30, 'Seats nearly full', 'A client is close to or over what they bought — an upsell, or a billing correction. Read from the local seat count, which is enforced, not from an invoice.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('invite_never_accepted', true, 10, NULL, 14, 'Invite never accepted', 'A seat paid for and never taken up. Invites expire silently after 14 days, so without this the seat quietly frees itself and nobody learns anything from it.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('questions_awaiting_review', true, 7, NULL, 7, 'Questions awaiting review', 'AI-drafted questions have been sitting unapproved. They are invisible to learners until someone acts, so a growing pile is content that was paid for and is not being served.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
INSERT INTO payroll_iq.notification_rules (key, enabled, threshold_days, threshold_pct, repeat_after_days, title, description, updated_at, updated_by) VALUES ('ingest_idle_or_failed', true, 14, NULL, 7, 'Synthesia ingest idle or failed', 'The ingest pipeline errored, or has not run when it should have. Without this a dead pipeline looks exactly like a month with no new content.', '2026-07-29 07:12:25.501108+00', NULL);
